WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.486
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:13.720
GEROVITCH: So I think
everybody is here,

00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:16.840
so I thought maybe
we could start.

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:18.080
My name is Slava Gerovitch.

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:21.330
And my email is on the
board, which conveniently

00:00:21.330 --> 00:00:24.330
has my first name on it.

00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:26.100
Professor David
[? Mandel, ?] who

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:29.790
is the principal investigator on
this project, is speaking today

00:00:29.790 --> 00:00:33.100
on the National Public Radio
on Science Friday program.

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:35.400
So I'm not sure
if he'll be here.

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:38.100
[? Sandy ?] [? Brown ?] who
is a research assistant,

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:41.130
and who has been in
contact with all of you,

00:00:41.130 --> 00:00:42.416
is currently in New Zealand.

00:00:42.416 --> 00:00:44.040
And he couldn't make
it back because of

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:46.580
the recent tragic events.

00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:48.270
So I think these
events kind of make

00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:50.640
us think that maybe
we should appreciate

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:52.260
every day of our
lives that we have,

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:54.830
and try to do as much
work as we can today.

00:00:58.410 --> 00:01:01.791
I will say a few words about
the overall project for the sake

00:01:01.791 --> 00:01:04.290
of Jim Miller and [? Eric ?]
[? Taylor, ?] who were not here

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:05.165
at the first meeting.

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:11.280
The project is called History
of Recent Science and Technology

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:15.750
on the Web, and its goal is
to develop some tools that

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:19.440
would enable veteran scientists
and engineers to participate

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:23.115
in writing the history of
their particular disciplines

00:01:23.115 --> 00:01:24.990
or projects.

00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:29.040
So our role as historians
here is just be your helpers,

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:33.810
to offer you some tools
and to listen to you what

00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:37.770
you would like to have in
terms of support and logistics

00:01:37.770 --> 00:01:42.090
that would allow you to
contribute to writing history.

00:01:42.090 --> 00:01:44.970
There are different formats
for using the web in this way.

00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:49.410
Some people prefer using
web forums or email

00:01:49.410 --> 00:01:51.390
lists, things like that.

00:01:51.390 --> 00:01:53.850
David [? Mandel ?]
has suggested that we

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:58.110
would have these live
meetings, where people would

00:01:58.110 --> 00:02:01.320
exchange their
memories and discuss

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:03.210
some of the historical issues.

00:02:03.210 --> 00:02:06.030
Then we would have these
meetings transcribed

00:02:06.030 --> 00:02:09.180
and put transcripts on the
web, where everybody could

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:11.800
read them and comment on them.

00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:15.570
We also are planning to put-- or
actually are already doing it--

00:02:15.570 --> 00:02:20.880
to put some archival
documents up

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:22.770
on the web, where
you could read it

00:02:22.770 --> 00:02:25.740
and, again, you
could write comments

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:28.320
on particular documents,
on parts of it, which

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:29.630
will also be posted on the web.

00:02:29.630 --> 00:02:31.255
So people after that
could come and see

00:02:31.255 --> 00:02:33.510
your comments and the
original document,

00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:37.090
and maybe write
some other comments.

00:02:37.090 --> 00:02:40.740
So these are the kind of things
that we're thinking about.

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:46.290
If you would think that some
other forms, like a discussion

00:02:46.290 --> 00:02:49.140
list, would be
helpful, please let us

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:52.960
know, and we'll try that.

00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:57.090
I don't know if anybody of you
took a look at the website,

00:02:57.090 --> 00:02:59.327
which is hrst.mit.edu.

00:03:11.070 --> 00:03:15.190
And it stands for history of
recent science and technology.

00:03:15.190 --> 00:03:18.010
Doesn't have any www.

00:03:18.010 --> 00:03:21.730
So this is the
overall project, which

00:03:21.730 --> 00:03:24.450
includes five
subprojects, one of which

00:03:24.450 --> 00:03:28.120
is Apollo guidance computer, one
is molecular evolution theory,

00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:32.980
one is bioinformatics,
and a couple of others.

00:03:32.980 --> 00:03:35.570
In different fields, people
try different approaches.

00:03:35.570 --> 00:03:37.120
We'll see what
works in our case.

00:03:39.910 --> 00:03:42.730
So far, we've had one
meeting last month.

00:03:42.730 --> 00:03:44.770
And we already have
a transcript of it.

00:03:44.770 --> 00:03:47.680
And as far as I know, it's
been circulated somewhat,

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:50.440
and people wrote
some corrections.

00:03:50.440 --> 00:03:53.200
And we are planning to
put it up on the web

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:55.700
shortly, within the
next couple of weeks,

00:03:55.700 --> 00:03:58.880
where you will all be able
to see it and comment on it.

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:01.450
I should mention
that this website

00:04:01.450 --> 00:04:02.900
is kind of working tool.

00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:04.030
So it has two layers on it.

00:04:04.030 --> 00:04:07.240
One is public, what
everybody can see,

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:12.010
and one is a working area
where only members of our group

00:04:12.010 --> 00:04:12.700
can see.

00:04:12.700 --> 00:04:17.121
And so when you put
your comment on the web,

00:04:17.121 --> 00:04:18.579
only members for
our group would be

00:04:18.579 --> 00:04:20.470
able to see it at the
moment, so because we

00:04:20.470 --> 00:04:25.240
would like to have
some kind of place

00:04:25.240 --> 00:04:28.210
where we would be able to
discuss our views freely

00:04:28.210 --> 00:04:30.586
and have some debate.

00:04:30.586 --> 00:04:32.210
And then we would
decide to what extent

00:04:32.210 --> 00:04:33.790
we would open it
up to the public.

00:04:36.920 --> 00:04:43.370
So at the first
meeting, we generally--

00:04:43.370 --> 00:04:46.180
I'm just saying it for the sake
of Jim Miller and [? Eric ?]

00:04:46.180 --> 00:04:49.370
[? Taylor, ?] who
were not there--

00:04:49.370 --> 00:04:53.140
we generally had an introduction
when people talk a little bit

00:04:53.140 --> 00:04:55.930
about their role in the
development of software

00:04:55.930 --> 00:04:58.220
for the Apollo
guidance computer.

00:04:58.220 --> 00:05:03.770
And some of the
general topics came up.

00:05:03.770 --> 00:05:05.170
What I would like
generally to do

00:05:05.170 --> 00:05:07.780
is to focus on a
few important issues

00:05:07.780 --> 00:05:13.720
in this project that
really made that project so

00:05:13.720 --> 00:05:18.460
salient and significant,
issues that, first of all,

00:05:18.460 --> 00:05:20.980
allow that enormous
enterprise to be

00:05:20.980 --> 00:05:22.810
accomplished successfully.

00:05:22.810 --> 00:05:26.860
So as Fred Martin, I
think, put it last time,

00:05:26.860 --> 00:05:29.337
if we had to do this program
today in the manner in which

00:05:29.337 --> 00:05:31.420
programs are done today,
I don't think the program

00:05:31.420 --> 00:05:32.929
could have ever be done.

00:05:32.929 --> 00:05:34.970
I don't think that the
amount of paper generated,

00:05:34.970 --> 00:05:37.010
the meetings, the views, the
number of people involved,

00:05:37.010 --> 00:05:39.009
I don't think the program
could have ever gotten

00:05:39.009 --> 00:05:39.730
off the ground.

00:05:39.730 --> 00:05:41.230
So that's a very
interesting comment

00:05:41.230 --> 00:05:46.710
that suggests that the way
the project was done back

00:05:46.710 --> 00:05:48.907
in the '60s was kind of unique.

00:05:48.907 --> 00:05:50.740
And we've tried to
recreate that uniqueness.

00:05:50.740 --> 00:05:55.270
And for the reason, your own
reminiscences as participants

00:05:55.270 --> 00:05:56.290
are invaluable.

00:05:56.290 --> 00:05:59.950
That's something that
documents cannot tell us.

00:05:59.950 --> 00:06:06.220
So I would ask you
to bring up as much

00:06:06.220 --> 00:06:10.000
as you can these elements
of uniqueness, of something

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:11.680
that distinguish that
particular project

00:06:11.680 --> 00:06:13.900
and you work on it
from other things.

00:06:13.900 --> 00:06:17.380
If you could compare it
particularly with other things

00:06:17.380 --> 00:06:20.270
that you encountered in
other times and other places,

00:06:20.270 --> 00:06:24.160
organizations, and to
draw explicit comparisons,

00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:27.940
that would help us,
too, to determine

00:06:27.940 --> 00:06:29.590
what distinguishing
features that would

00:06:29.590 --> 00:06:31.427
be particularly helpful.

00:06:31.427 --> 00:06:33.010
Another thing that
we would like to do

00:06:33.010 --> 00:06:38.040
is to avoid writing kind
of linear history, when

00:06:38.040 --> 00:06:42.190
basically what we often can see
is just a line of decisions.

00:06:42.190 --> 00:06:45.166
First there is this, then
that, then this, then that.

00:06:45.166 --> 00:06:47.290
[? And then it's ?]
[? success. ?] There are always

00:06:47.290 --> 00:06:50.560
some points where different
decisions could be made.

00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:52.300
There were debates
when, you know,

00:06:52.300 --> 00:06:53.770
there were some roads not taken.

00:06:53.770 --> 00:06:57.580
At some places, they had to go
back and take the other road,

00:06:57.580 --> 00:06:58.300
and then go back.

00:06:58.300 --> 00:07:02.290
So we'd like to recreate that
rather complex road map instead

00:07:02.290 --> 00:07:09.040
of just a sequence of
solutions that we eventually

00:07:09.040 --> 00:07:11.170
found their way in
the eventual product.

00:07:11.170 --> 00:07:15.520
So we'd like to have that sense
of debate, of controversy, what

00:07:15.520 --> 00:07:17.560
exactly was the
points of contention

00:07:17.560 --> 00:07:19.510
at different points
of the project?

00:07:19.510 --> 00:07:22.930
Which design decisions or
organizational decisions

00:07:22.930 --> 00:07:26.320
were particularly important
and debated at the time,

00:07:26.320 --> 00:07:30.760
so that history
would be alive rather

00:07:30.760 --> 00:07:32.335
than a sequence of facts.

00:07:35.050 --> 00:07:37.930
A few things that came
up in terms of topics

00:07:37.930 --> 00:07:40.750
that we discussed last
time, or the issue

00:07:40.750 --> 00:07:51.220
of the degree to which the
automatic and manual systems

00:07:51.220 --> 00:07:56.050
must be combined in the
guidance of a spacecraft.

00:07:56.050 --> 00:08:00.520
And as far as I can
see in this discussion,

00:08:00.520 --> 00:08:05.170
mostly the issue of trust in the
electronic equipment came up.

00:08:05.170 --> 00:08:07.720
And the astronauts
didn't really trust it.

00:08:07.720 --> 00:08:12.360
So the automatic side had
to be justified somehow.

00:08:12.360 --> 00:08:15.657
While in the Soviet
program, curiously--

00:08:15.657 --> 00:08:18.240
and I should mention that I am
actually working on the Russian

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:19.970
side of this story--

00:08:19.970 --> 00:08:22.350
in the Soviet program, the
issue was to what extent

00:08:22.350 --> 00:08:26.700
we can trust the humans,
the opposite issue.

00:08:26.700 --> 00:08:29.070
So somehow, the Soviets
had a greater belief

00:08:29.070 --> 00:08:31.189
in automatic systems,
in their reliability,

00:08:31.189 --> 00:08:33.480
and they thought that the
humans were the weakest link.

00:08:36.210 --> 00:08:41.940
So I'd like to
kind of frame that

00:08:41.940 --> 00:08:46.770
issue a little bit
around this question.

00:08:46.770 --> 00:08:50.166
Maybe these issues came up
in your project, as well,

00:08:50.166 --> 00:08:51.540
but you simply
didn't mention it.

00:08:51.540 --> 00:08:54.120
So that would be interesting.

00:08:54.120 --> 00:08:57.180
Another thing is man-machine
interface, to what extent

00:08:57.180 --> 00:09:03.390
the astronauts had an input into
the way they wanted to interact

00:09:03.390 --> 00:09:05.640
with the computer,
to what extent

00:09:05.640 --> 00:09:10.270
computer designers
had their say.

00:09:10.270 --> 00:09:13.920
The role of warning
systems, to what extent

00:09:13.920 --> 00:09:19.470
computer was allowed
to let the astronaut

00:09:19.470 --> 00:09:23.490
knows that something
that astronaut is doing

00:09:23.490 --> 00:09:24.480
is not really right.

00:09:27.010 --> 00:09:31.200
The issue of memory constraints
and speed constraints

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:36.210
that really put a limit on
these kind of backup and warning

00:09:36.210 --> 00:09:37.740
systems.

00:09:37.740 --> 00:09:39.820
Also, series of
implementation meetings

00:09:39.820 --> 00:09:45.300
was mentioned in the issue
of disciplining the software

00:09:45.300 --> 00:09:50.460
program, introducing
all sorts of measures

00:09:50.460 --> 00:09:54.150
that kind of
streamlined that process

00:09:54.150 --> 00:09:57.150
and made it manageable from
the NASA point of view.

00:09:57.150 --> 00:10:00.000
And I would like to--

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:02.100
maybe if you could
say a few words

00:10:02.100 --> 00:10:06.150
about the way that was
done, how it was accepted,

00:10:06.150 --> 00:10:09.390
what consequence did it have,
and a little bit more detail

00:10:09.390 --> 00:10:10.870
on that.

00:10:10.870 --> 00:10:14.650
And lastly, the question of
openness of the whole project.

00:10:14.650 --> 00:10:17.277
Again, there is a
startling contrast

00:10:17.277 --> 00:10:19.110
with the Soviet program,
which, as you know,

00:10:19.110 --> 00:10:23.360
was all classified and run
under the military umbrella,

00:10:23.360 --> 00:10:26.070
while the Apollo
project was open.

00:10:26.070 --> 00:10:29.630
And some of the participants
in the last meeting

00:10:29.630 --> 00:10:33.785
stressed that it had
a very important role.

00:10:33.785 --> 00:10:35.910
And I really want to push
you a little bit on that,

00:10:35.910 --> 00:10:39.754
and see whether this
openness actually maybe posed

00:10:39.754 --> 00:10:41.670
some problems for you,
in terms of interacting

00:10:41.670 --> 00:10:42.650
with military people.

00:10:42.650 --> 00:10:43.830
Maybe military
people didn't want

00:10:43.830 --> 00:10:46.371
to tell you something because
they knew that your project was

00:10:46.371 --> 00:10:47.670
open, or--

00:10:47.670 --> 00:10:49.650
I'm just speculating
about that, maybe

00:10:49.650 --> 00:10:50.950
it wasn't a problem at all.

00:10:50.950 --> 00:10:55.060
So maybe you could say
a few words about that.

00:10:55.060 --> 00:11:03.230
So today's meeting, I would
suggest to start by asking Jim

00:11:03.230 --> 00:11:05.460
Miller and [? Eric ?]
[? Taylor, ?] who were not here

00:11:05.460 --> 00:11:06.210
the first time--

00:11:06.547 --> 00:11:07.680
PRESENTER 7: [? I ?] [? was ?]
[? here. ?] [INAUDIBLE] Alex

00:11:07.680 --> 00:11:08.180
[INAUDIBLE].

00:11:08.180 --> 00:11:10.440
GEROVITCH: Oh, Alex
[INAUDIBLE],, sorry.

00:11:10.440 --> 00:11:14.460
To introduce themselves
and to talk a little bit

00:11:14.460 --> 00:11:19.110
about your role in the Apollo
guidance computer story.

00:11:19.110 --> 00:11:21.000
And then we would
go around the table

00:11:21.000 --> 00:11:24.050
and have people talking
about the issues.

00:11:24.050 --> 00:11:24.937
Start with Alex?

00:11:24.937 --> 00:11:25.520
PRESENTER: OK.

00:11:29.880 --> 00:11:31.450
I think I started the--

00:11:31.450 --> 00:11:35.317
I joined the lab in '63.

00:11:35.317 --> 00:11:36.400
PRESENTER: For the second.

00:11:36.400 --> 00:11:37.200
PRESENTER: Hm?

00:11:37.200 --> 00:11:38.100
PRESENTER: For the second time.

00:11:38.100 --> 00:11:39.475
PRESENTER 5: For
the second time.

00:11:39.475 --> 00:11:41.400
Dan was one who winkled
me out of England

00:11:41.400 --> 00:11:44.630
to come over at the start
of the Apollo program.

00:11:44.630 --> 00:11:46.980
I came as a physics graduate.

00:11:46.980 --> 00:11:50.460
I knew nothing about
computers, analog or digital.

00:11:50.460 --> 00:11:53.010
I knew nothing
about programming.

00:11:53.010 --> 00:11:54.272
PRESENTER: I knew that.

00:11:54.272 --> 00:11:57.044
[LAUGHTER]

00:11:57.044 --> 00:11:58.860
PRESENTER 5: And one
of my observations

00:11:58.860 --> 00:12:02.890
actually has to do with that
because one of the successes

00:12:02.890 --> 00:12:03.390
here--

00:12:03.390 --> 00:12:05.131
I mean, I know when
Margaret joined,

00:12:05.131 --> 00:12:07.630
she didn't know anything about
inertial navigation systems--

00:12:07.630 --> 00:12:09.870
I hope I'm not putting
words in your mouth--

00:12:09.870 --> 00:12:13.260
and yet, we grew this
knowledge to the extent

00:12:13.260 --> 00:12:17.160
that we needed to understand it,
to control the right programs

00:12:17.160 --> 00:12:18.930
that control it.

00:12:18.930 --> 00:12:23.400
To me, that was always amazing,
that so few people, relatively

00:12:23.400 --> 00:12:27.640
inexperienced in the subject
that we were dealing,

00:12:27.640 --> 00:12:30.480
they were just smart
people, managed

00:12:30.480 --> 00:12:35.220
to acquire enough information
from a vast array of resources

00:12:35.220 --> 00:12:40.470
around us in the lab to not be
blocked by a wall of ignorance.

00:12:40.470 --> 00:12:42.482
I can't move because
I don't know.

00:12:42.482 --> 00:12:43.440
You went and found out.

00:12:43.440 --> 00:12:44.190
You talked to people.

00:12:44.190 --> 00:12:45.981
And there weren't very
many people that you

00:12:45.981 --> 00:12:47.760
needed to do that with.

00:12:47.760 --> 00:12:50.190
To me, one of the
strengths of that program

00:12:50.190 --> 00:12:53.060
was the few people
that were working.

00:12:53.060 --> 00:12:55.140
And we always came to
the conclusion-- and Dan

00:12:55.140 --> 00:12:56.890
and I talked about
this in the early days,

00:12:56.890 --> 00:13:00.570
that the optimum size of
a software group producing

00:13:00.570 --> 00:13:03.750
complicated software like
this was about five or six,

00:13:03.750 --> 00:13:05.880
because during some of
the stressful period

00:13:05.880 --> 00:13:07.546
where we were really
churning stuff out,

00:13:07.546 --> 00:13:10.760
there were little knots of
five or six people that really

00:13:10.760 --> 00:13:13.490
put the foundations to this.

00:13:13.490 --> 00:13:17.390
One of my roles when I began to
feel my way around that I can

00:13:17.390 --> 00:13:19.880
remember-- and my memory is
really starting to come apart

00:13:19.880 --> 00:13:21.200
at the edges--

00:13:21.200 --> 00:13:25.100
was to integrate
some of these areas.

00:13:25.100 --> 00:13:27.950
There was a launch area,
there was a reentry area

00:13:27.950 --> 00:13:31.410
[? on the dam, ?] there was
a powered flight area, who

00:13:31.410 --> 00:13:34.640
were investigating some
of the technical problems

00:13:34.640 --> 00:13:36.380
in these various areas.

00:13:36.380 --> 00:13:41.060
I had to stick these together
in order to achieve our first

00:13:41.060 --> 00:13:44.750
guided flight, which
I still have pictures

00:13:44.750 --> 00:13:50.450
and [? stuff of ?] which we
knew as Apollo [? 202 ?] is

00:13:50.450 --> 00:13:55.430
to manufacture the glue and just
make stuff up that integrated

00:13:55.430 --> 00:13:59.750
the astronauts' actions, the
powered flight equations,

00:13:59.750 --> 00:14:02.090
track the launch, which
we didn't control.

00:14:02.090 --> 00:14:05.870
So when the time we took
over, we knew where we were

00:14:05.870 --> 00:14:08.630
and how fast we were going
[INAUDIBLE] then we took over.

00:14:08.630 --> 00:14:12.800
The midcourse stuff, which was
a whole other ball game, which

00:14:12.800 --> 00:14:14.180
I knew absolute nothing of.

00:14:14.180 --> 00:14:15.638
And even at the
end of the program,

00:14:15.638 --> 00:14:19.190
I really didn't
understand it very well.

00:14:19.190 --> 00:14:22.380
So my role was to knit some
of these pieces together.

00:14:22.380 --> 00:14:27.500
And my memory is dim, but it was
a heck of a lot of hard work.

00:14:27.500 --> 00:14:28.370
Long hours.

00:14:28.370 --> 00:14:31.160
Stuff didn't work.

00:14:31.160 --> 00:14:34.950
Stuff we made up as we
went along didn't work.

00:14:34.950 --> 00:14:39.110
But in the end, it all did work.

00:14:39.110 --> 00:14:41.300
If I remember rightly,
that first flight

00:14:41.300 --> 00:14:47.109
landed remarkably close to
its objective in the Pacific.

00:14:47.109 --> 00:14:48.650
PRESENTER: Being
late late for lunch,

00:14:48.650 --> 00:14:50.270
because I wouldn't
let you go you

00:14:50.270 --> 00:14:52.890
when you were dying of hunger.

00:14:52.890 --> 00:14:54.990
You remember a few
of those, right?

00:14:54.990 --> 00:14:57.650
PRESENTER 5: I can ramble
on, but I think to me

00:14:57.650 --> 00:15:01.310
the amazing thing about
this, looking back,

00:15:01.310 --> 00:15:07.880
was the quality of the people,
the [INAUDIBLE] of the people,

00:15:07.880 --> 00:15:10.970
the immense support that you
got from all the resources

00:15:10.970 --> 00:15:12.920
that were arrayed around you.

00:15:12.920 --> 00:15:17.720
The openness, I guess, is what
you were referring to before.

00:15:17.720 --> 00:15:19.820
We ourselves, at least
that's what I felt,

00:15:19.820 --> 00:15:22.402
were the only blocks,
because of ignorance.

00:15:22.402 --> 00:15:24.860
But we overcame that as we went
along, and we made it work.

00:15:29.560 --> 00:15:31.542
I'll just shut up at that point.

00:15:31.542 --> 00:15:34.042
I'd like to hear what some of
the other people [INAUDIBLE]..

00:15:34.042 --> 00:15:34.930
PRESENTER: You'll
get another chance.

00:15:34.930 --> 00:15:35.805
GEROVITCH: All right.

00:15:35.805 --> 00:15:37.890
[? Jim? ?]

00:15:37.890 --> 00:15:40.290
PRESENTER 1: Not sure
exactly how much of what

00:15:40.290 --> 00:15:43.980
to cover, but let me cover
some things briefly about how

00:15:43.980 --> 00:15:48.360
I got there and what I did.

00:15:48.360 --> 00:15:52.290
I evaded the draft
during the Korean War

00:15:52.290 --> 00:15:55.290
by going off to graduate
school and staying in college.

00:15:55.290 --> 00:15:58.380
Got a master's degree in
electrical engineering.

00:15:58.380 --> 00:16:02.400
And that was as long as I could
postpone being in the service.

00:16:02.400 --> 00:16:06.690
And I entered the service
in 1956, the Air Force,

00:16:06.690 --> 00:16:10.920
and was assigned to a place
called Cape "Cana-vare-al,"

00:16:10.920 --> 00:16:13.710
or something I had
never heard of.

00:16:13.710 --> 00:16:16.320
John [? Daley, ?] who
read the news on TV,

00:16:16.320 --> 00:16:19.500
hadn't heard of it either, and
that's how he pronounced it.

00:16:19.500 --> 00:16:23.100
But within a week or
two of being there,

00:16:23.100 --> 00:16:24.640
I was out at the
Cape, and watched

00:16:24.640 --> 00:16:27.360
the Redstone launched at night.

00:16:27.360 --> 00:16:32.870
And I was completely hooked
into the idea of rocketry

00:16:32.870 --> 00:16:34.610
and guiding the things.

00:16:34.610 --> 00:16:38.040
And I stayed in the
service a couple of years,

00:16:38.040 --> 00:16:43.160
and came up to MIT to get
a doctorate in program--

00:16:43.160 --> 00:16:45.310
Doc Draper-- called
instrumentation,

00:16:45.310 --> 00:16:49.230
which was a combination
of academic disciplines,

00:16:49.230 --> 00:16:50.648
multi-departmental.

00:16:53.400 --> 00:16:57.840
I started writing code for
computers in '57 or something.

00:16:57.840 --> 00:17:01.570
We had a digital
computer down there.

00:17:01.570 --> 00:17:06.720
And eventually, I got
into programming it

00:17:06.720 --> 00:17:07.770
for whatever reason.

00:17:07.770 --> 00:17:10.170
And writing my
doctoral thesis, I

00:17:10.170 --> 00:17:12.359
got into both
celestial mechanics--

00:17:12.359 --> 00:17:18.329
because I wrote on guidance of a
one-pound thrust rocket vehicle

00:17:18.329 --> 00:17:21.630
from Earth orbit to lunar orbit.

00:17:21.630 --> 00:17:24.894
1,000-pound vehicle with a
one-pound engine, if I recall.

00:17:24.894 --> 00:17:29.159
It takes a long time
to build up the energy

00:17:29.159 --> 00:17:30.450
you need to get up to the moon.

00:17:30.450 --> 00:17:32.040
But in the process,
I learned a lot

00:17:32.040 --> 00:17:35.100
about celestial mechanics
and trajectories,

00:17:35.100 --> 00:17:39.420
and about writing
code for computers.

00:17:39.420 --> 00:17:45.060
And I really have to say
that the computer part of it

00:17:45.060 --> 00:17:48.090
appealed to me more than
any of the other parts,

00:17:48.090 --> 00:17:50.550
although I liked
celestial mechanics.

00:17:50.550 --> 00:17:55.890
But when I finished my
work, Doc Draper had,

00:17:55.890 --> 00:17:58.800
through his
incredible influence,

00:17:58.800 --> 00:18:03.150
landed a sole-source
contract at the laboratory--

00:18:03.150 --> 00:18:07.890
instrumentation lab,
where I was based--

00:18:07.890 --> 00:18:11.730
to do the Apollo guidance
system in late '61.

00:18:11.730 --> 00:18:16.080
And with Bob [? Stern, ?] Dick
[? Batten, ?] and Hal Laning,

00:18:16.080 --> 00:18:19.020
we were a group of what I think
was called Advanced Guidance

00:18:19.020 --> 00:18:23.219
or something to work
the Apollo problem.

00:18:23.219 --> 00:18:25.260
There were other people
in the [? Polaris ?] side

00:18:25.260 --> 00:18:27.640
that were busy, as well.

00:18:27.640 --> 00:18:31.260
But we started without
really having any contact

00:18:31.260 --> 00:18:33.210
with those folks.

00:18:33.210 --> 00:18:36.540
I worked on
trajectories and trying

00:18:36.540 --> 00:18:39.510
to figure out abort
trajectories and so forth.

00:18:39.510 --> 00:18:41.700
But pretty soon--
maybe that was later,

00:18:41.700 --> 00:18:48.990
but Hal Laning had written an
executive and an interpreter

00:18:48.990 --> 00:18:51.890
for a computer, which
was being proposed

00:18:51.890 --> 00:18:53.790
to be the onboard
computer for Apollo, which

00:18:53.790 --> 00:18:58.320
was a computer that had
originally been intended

00:18:58.320 --> 00:19:01.320
to fly to Mars and
take one photograph,

00:19:01.320 --> 00:19:05.010
and come back and
drop it somewhere

00:19:05.010 --> 00:19:06.345
where it could be retrieved.

00:19:06.345 --> 00:19:09.120
It never happened,
but that computer,

00:19:09.120 --> 00:19:12.360
which was a 15-bit
computer, of all things,

00:19:12.360 --> 00:19:16.440
with three bits for op code
and 12 bits for address,

00:19:16.440 --> 00:19:17.820
was very nicely designed.

00:19:17.820 --> 00:19:22.110
It had eight op codes and
4K of memory, of which I

00:19:22.110 --> 00:19:25.910
think 500 words were erasable.

00:19:25.910 --> 00:19:27.600
The rest was fixed memory.

00:19:27.600 --> 00:19:29.560
These folks know more
about that than I do.

00:19:29.560 --> 00:19:33.810
But Hal had written this
executive that dispatched tasks

00:19:33.810 --> 00:19:37.280
on a priority basis,
and an interpreter,

00:19:37.280 --> 00:19:41.980
which was something I'd never
even thought of or heard of.

00:19:41.980 --> 00:19:46.910
Hal was probably not the first
to do that, but a real pioneer

00:19:46.910 --> 00:19:49.270
in a lot of ways.

00:19:49.270 --> 00:19:53.550
But he had written
its code, and I

00:19:53.550 --> 00:19:56.160
started reading it
and trying to make it

00:19:56.160 --> 00:19:58.080
better, which was difficult.

00:19:58.080 --> 00:20:02.219
But it occurred to me that
one of the things that we

00:20:02.219 --> 00:20:04.760
were going to need is something
that simulated that computer.

00:20:04.760 --> 00:20:09.345
Because we didn't have a
computer to run, and if we had,

00:20:09.345 --> 00:20:12.870
it wouldn't have been designed
for debugging and so forth.

00:20:12.870 --> 00:20:17.970
So I got, eventually, into
the game of writing a computer

00:20:17.970 --> 00:20:21.900
simulator for the Apollo--

00:20:21.900 --> 00:20:26.140
for what we called
then the Mars computer.

00:20:26.140 --> 00:20:28.450
And that developed,
over time-- and I

00:20:28.450 --> 00:20:31.750
sort of skip around a
lot-- but over time,

00:20:31.750 --> 00:20:36.700
into a full simulation of the
spacecraft, [? the ?] guidance

00:20:36.700 --> 00:20:39.790
computer, all of the hardware
that interacted directly

00:20:39.790 --> 00:20:44.170
with the computer, or that
controlled the spacecraft,

00:20:44.170 --> 00:20:49.360
and even with the crew,
including where the fixed

00:20:49.360 --> 00:20:55.390
stars were, and all the sextant
operations and so forth.

00:20:55.390 --> 00:20:57.070
And ended up in the
library developing

00:20:57.070 --> 00:20:58.880
a star catalog, which
as far as I know,

00:20:58.880 --> 00:21:00.880
was the one that
went to the moon.

00:21:03.487 --> 00:21:04.570
Well, I don't really know.

00:21:04.570 --> 00:21:06.740
I lost track of it.

00:21:06.740 --> 00:21:10.630
But they don't change very fast,
so it probably stayed the same.

00:21:13.420 --> 00:21:19.270
And the group developed into, I
don't know, half a dozen people

00:21:19.270 --> 00:21:23.920
or so on different aspects
of writing the simulation.

00:21:23.920 --> 00:21:27.940
And we were racing to get it
ready in time for Alex's Tool

00:21:27.940 --> 00:21:30.100
[? Two ?] software.

00:21:30.100 --> 00:21:32.410
And I say Alex's because
he was in charge of it,

00:21:32.410 --> 00:21:35.470
and bore the brunt
of all the stress.

00:21:35.470 --> 00:21:38.880
Other folks just
took life easy, and--

00:21:38.880 --> 00:21:40.190
PRESENTER 6: Like Margaret.

00:21:40.190 --> 00:21:43.810
PRESENTER 1: Margaret
and Dan, and [INAUDIBLE]..

00:21:43.810 --> 00:21:50.510
But trying to keep up with their
needs to do the simulation.

00:21:50.510 --> 00:21:57.440
We had, at that time, moved
from 650 powerhouse [? of the ?]

00:21:57.440 --> 00:22:00.590
instrumentation lab up
to a Honeywell machine,

00:22:00.590 --> 00:22:04.340
which was designed to do
insurance programming,

00:22:04.340 --> 00:22:07.190
I think, and had
lots of tape drives.

00:22:07.190 --> 00:22:10.760
And fun machine, but
wasn't quite fast enough,

00:22:10.760 --> 00:22:12.590
so we eventually
got a faster one,

00:22:12.590 --> 00:22:14.420
and then we moved
onto IBM machines.

00:22:16.973 --> 00:22:19.150
PRESENTER 5: Is that the
one with [INAUDIBLE] on it?

00:22:19.150 --> 00:22:22.620
[INAUDIBLE]

00:22:22.620 --> 00:22:29.790
PRESENTER 1: I remember one day,
we had two [? 370 ?] 75's, just

00:22:29.790 --> 00:22:33.030
about the world's fastest
commercially-available

00:22:33.030 --> 00:22:35.170
machines, other than
maybe some other--

00:22:35.170 --> 00:22:37.290
[? I'd seen ?] more
[? crazy ?] machines.

00:22:37.290 --> 00:22:38.820
And we had fixed
it up so each one

00:22:38.820 --> 00:22:42.400
had two big [? 23 ?] 14 arrays.

00:22:42.400 --> 00:22:44.170
23 [? 14 ?] was
a big disk drive,

00:22:44.170 --> 00:22:47.440
which was about the
footprint of this table.

00:22:47.440 --> 00:22:50.420
It had eight
multi-platter disks in it.

00:22:50.420 --> 00:22:54.150
Nine, actually, because one
would fail or something.

00:22:54.150 --> 00:22:56.860
I guess it was for
loading up another on one

00:22:56.860 --> 00:22:59.790
while the other
ones were in use.

00:22:59.790 --> 00:23:02.290
And as I recall,
we had two of those

00:23:02.290 --> 00:23:03.420
connected to each machine.

00:23:03.420 --> 00:23:06.402
And because of cable
restrictions and distance

00:23:06.402 --> 00:23:07.860
of the machines,
we had a fifth one

00:23:07.860 --> 00:23:10.500
that was halfway between
the two machines,

00:23:10.500 --> 00:23:13.180
with a cable running from
each, so we had a shared disk.

00:23:13.180 --> 00:23:14.850
And I remember standing
there thinking,

00:23:14.850 --> 00:23:19.140
we have a gigabyte of storage
on this machine-- well, almost--

00:23:19.140 --> 00:23:20.110
between these machines.

00:23:20.110 --> 00:23:21.109
And I thought that was--

00:23:21.109 --> 00:23:25.140
I mean, it was, at the time,
absolutely mind-boggling.

00:23:25.140 --> 00:23:28.060
Not nearly as mind-boggling
as what's happened since then.

00:23:28.060 --> 00:23:29.972
[LAUGHTER]

00:23:30.930 --> 00:23:33.720
PRESENTER: You should point out,
a megabyte on the 75 cost about

00:23:33.720 --> 00:23:34.650
$1 million.

00:23:34.650 --> 00:23:35.460
[INAUDIBLE]

00:23:35.460 --> 00:23:37.920
PRESENTER 1: Well, the
machine that I ordered--

00:23:37.920 --> 00:23:41.520
the 75 that I initially ordered
had 1/2 a megabyte of memory.

00:23:41.520 --> 00:23:44.730
And little did I know--
and it [? had ?] four 23

00:23:44.730 --> 00:23:47.990
[? 11's ?] because the 23
[? 14's ?] weren't available.

00:23:47.990 --> 00:23:50.017
I've forgotten how
small the 23 11's were.

00:23:50.017 --> 00:23:51.900
PRESENTER 5: I [? think ?]
the 28 megabytes.

00:23:51.900 --> 00:23:52.560
PRESENTER 1: They were small.

00:23:52.560 --> 00:23:52.860
PRESENTER 5: Yeah.

00:23:52.860 --> 00:23:54.930
PRESENTER 1: And the
operating system used up,

00:23:54.930 --> 00:23:57.900
I think, all of the space,
and all of the memory,

00:23:57.900 --> 00:24:00.090
leaving no space
for anything else.

00:24:00.090 --> 00:24:01.820
But memory was so expensive.

00:24:01.820 --> 00:24:03.600
We just-- well, we had to buy--

00:24:03.600 --> 00:24:06.090
we went up to a whole megabyte.

00:24:06.090 --> 00:24:09.110
I remember, living in
the Netherlands, bought

00:24:09.110 --> 00:24:13.890
a megabyte of memory for
my AT that I had bought.

00:24:13.890 --> 00:24:15.720
And rode home from
the electronics store

00:24:15.720 --> 00:24:19.040
with a megabyte of memory
in my pocket on my bicycle.

00:24:19.040 --> 00:24:22.360
And I thought, gee, things
have really progressed.

00:24:22.360 --> 00:24:25.380
Now my 60-gigabyte disk that
I have on my machine at home

00:24:25.380 --> 00:24:27.570
fits on my shirt pocket.

00:24:27.570 --> 00:24:29.420
Just can't believe
what's happened.

00:24:29.420 --> 00:24:33.990
And it is very difficult for
me, having even been there--

00:24:33.990 --> 00:24:35.880
and I think for people
who weren't there,

00:24:35.880 --> 00:24:37.560
almost impossible--

00:24:37.560 --> 00:24:42.750
to imagine the difficulties
that people dealing

00:24:42.750 --> 00:24:48.020
with a machine with a 15-bit
word and lots of restrictions

00:24:48.020 --> 00:24:51.170
upon expanding it-- even though
the technology was available,

00:24:51.170 --> 00:24:55.800
the reliability requirements
prevented much happening

00:24:55.800 --> 00:24:59.760
that would take advantage of
what could have been done.

00:24:59.760 --> 00:25:02.070
Trying to squeeze
enough software in there

00:25:02.070 --> 00:25:05.820
to fly to the moon and
back into what became,

00:25:05.820 --> 00:25:09.370
I think, 36 K-words
of fixed memory,

00:25:09.370 --> 00:25:13.604
and 1K of erasable memory.

00:25:13.604 --> 00:25:14.270
PRESENTER 6: 2K.

00:25:14.270 --> 00:25:17.440
PRESENTER 1: 2K, yeah,
and 1K accessible.

00:25:17.440 --> 00:25:19.650
Right.

00:25:19.650 --> 00:25:24.120
And squeezing that out of 3-bit
op code and 12-bit address

00:25:24.120 --> 00:25:27.390
took some funny
shenanigans, which caused

00:25:27.390 --> 00:25:31.250
a lot of debugging activity.

00:25:31.250 --> 00:25:36.450
But I don't think anybody can
imagine the kinds of problems

00:25:36.450 --> 00:25:41.890
that were faced
trying to squeeze

00:25:41.890 --> 00:25:46.790
a huge amount of software into
that tiny machine, which really

00:25:46.790 --> 00:25:49.120
was small.

00:25:52.000 --> 00:25:53.750
After getting the
simulation group going--

00:25:53.750 --> 00:25:55.640
and there are lots of
war stories about that,

00:25:55.640 --> 00:25:59.300
probably of little interest
to most and known by the folks

00:25:59.300 --> 00:26:00.770
here--

00:26:00.770 --> 00:26:04.970
I foolishly accepted
the responsibility

00:26:04.970 --> 00:26:08.540
of becoming the leader
of the mission program

00:26:08.540 --> 00:26:10.680
for the first lunar module.

00:26:10.680 --> 00:26:14.710
This was after the Apollo
fire, and long hiatus

00:26:14.710 --> 00:26:18.470
of flights during which time
everybody moved to the block

00:26:18.470 --> 00:26:21.480
two stuff.

00:26:21.480 --> 00:26:27.950
And I remember a
long, long time,

00:26:27.950 --> 00:26:30.950
it seemed that Alex was
sweating and working

00:26:30.950 --> 00:26:33.760
on the [? 202 ?] software.

00:26:33.760 --> 00:26:35.450
The 204 software had never flew.

00:26:35.450 --> 00:26:38.570
It was going to fly in that
[INAUDIBLE] which the three

00:26:38.570 --> 00:26:43.280
astronauts died on the
[? pad. ?] But I went to Alex,

00:26:43.280 --> 00:26:44.990
and I don't know if
Alex remembers this,

00:26:44.990 --> 00:26:46.070
but I remember it.

00:26:46.070 --> 00:26:47.930
I've told a lot of people.

00:26:47.930 --> 00:26:50.030
I said, Alex, I
don't know anything

00:26:50.030 --> 00:26:51.391
about the mission software.

00:26:51.391 --> 00:26:53.390
I've been doing the
simulation and trajectories,

00:26:53.390 --> 00:26:54.500
and I know all that stuff.

00:26:54.500 --> 00:26:54.999
Cool.

00:26:54.999 --> 00:26:59.510
But I don't know anything
about the mission program.

00:26:59.510 --> 00:27:00.510
What do I do?

00:27:00.510 --> 00:27:01.850
What did you do?

00:27:01.850 --> 00:27:04.460
He says, well, anytime
somebody comes to you

00:27:04.460 --> 00:27:09.510
and asks you what they
should do, make something up.

00:27:09.510 --> 00:27:10.900
And I said, you're kidding.

00:27:10.900 --> 00:27:12.637
He said, absolutely not.

00:27:12.637 --> 00:27:14.720
He said, if they come to
ask you, they don't know,

00:27:14.720 --> 00:27:16.344
and they know better
than anybody else.

00:27:16.344 --> 00:27:18.950
So if they don't know
and you don't know,

00:27:18.950 --> 00:27:21.030
you got to make something up.

00:27:21.030 --> 00:27:23.570
Well, he was exactly right.

00:27:23.570 --> 00:27:25.160
It worked.

00:27:25.160 --> 00:27:27.770
You just had to not
get stuck on something.

00:27:27.770 --> 00:27:29.450
You just had to
take a path, and not

00:27:29.450 --> 00:27:31.700
all of the things that
either us made up,

00:27:31.700 --> 00:27:34.400
probably, but certainly
in my case, worked.

00:27:34.400 --> 00:27:36.741
But if they didn't work,
you found out about it.

00:27:36.741 --> 00:27:39.240
And then somebody would come
back and say, this didn't work.

00:27:39.240 --> 00:27:40.115
What should I do you?

00:27:40.115 --> 00:27:42.200
And make up something
else different.

00:27:42.200 --> 00:27:46.130
And that usually worked
because then you knew.

00:27:46.130 --> 00:27:48.780
It was the seat of the
pants a lot of the way.

00:27:48.780 --> 00:27:57.580
And that's probably a good
place for me to shut up for now.

00:27:57.580 --> 00:28:00.504
[INAUDIBLE]

00:28:00.504 --> 00:28:01.920
PRESENTER: [INAUDIBLE]
about 9:15.

00:28:01.920 --> 00:28:05.509
[INAUDIBLE]

00:28:05.509 --> 00:28:07.800
PRESENTER 2: Well, I got
involved in the Apollo project

00:28:07.800 --> 00:28:11.570
by way of a backdoor.

00:28:11.570 --> 00:28:15.790
I was working for
Raytheon, and attached

00:28:15.790 --> 00:28:19.205
to the instrumentation
lab on the Polaris project

00:28:19.205 --> 00:28:21.910
as a resident.

00:28:21.910 --> 00:28:26.875
And shortly after that
petered out a little bit,

00:28:26.875 --> 00:28:31.500
I became reattached as a
resident on the Apollo program

00:28:31.500 --> 00:28:34.580
in the computer group.

00:28:34.580 --> 00:28:39.340
And when I-- just putting
things in context,

00:28:39.340 --> 00:28:44.780
I was all of three
years out of school.

00:28:44.780 --> 00:28:50.200
So not what you'd call
really, tremendously

00:28:50.200 --> 00:28:53.090
experienced in computer design.

00:28:53.090 --> 00:29:01.240
But got into the computer
group and was doing work

00:29:01.240 --> 00:29:09.010
with Ramone and Dr. Hopkins,
Dave [? Shanksy ?] to go ahead

00:29:09.010 --> 00:29:11.410
with the Morris computer.

00:29:11.410 --> 00:29:14.830
And then, shortly
after arriving,

00:29:14.830 --> 00:29:17.890
the concept of the
integrated circuit arose.

00:29:22.190 --> 00:29:25.540
I remember a meeting in
Elden Hall's office, where

00:29:25.540 --> 00:29:30.130
we all gathered around and tried
to determine which direction

00:29:30.130 --> 00:29:33.670
the group ought to go as
far as implementing either

00:29:33.670 --> 00:29:36.790
with the Morris circuit
technology, the core transistor

00:29:36.790 --> 00:29:44.320
logic, or the integrated circuit
approach, and all of the issues

00:29:44.320 --> 00:29:46.480
that that was going to raise.

00:29:46.480 --> 00:29:49.630
And the decision was made to
go ahead with the integrated

00:29:49.630 --> 00:29:53.152
circuit approach.

00:29:53.152 --> 00:29:55.360
Of course, none of us knew
what an integrated circuit

00:29:55.360 --> 00:29:58.300
was at that time.

00:29:58.300 --> 00:30:03.070
But I ended up with
the responsibility

00:30:03.070 --> 00:30:07.180
of re-implementing the Morris
design in the integrated

00:30:07.180 --> 00:30:15.620
circuit world, and
carried that through.

00:30:15.620 --> 00:30:19.030
It's a very interesting
computer design.

00:30:19.030 --> 00:30:21.942
Many levels of interrupt.

00:30:25.320 --> 00:30:29.530
I remember tech words like
pink, mink, shink, and shank--

00:30:29.530 --> 00:30:31.230
[LAUGHTER]

00:30:34.110 --> 00:30:37.200
--for, essentially,
instantaneous reaction

00:30:37.200 --> 00:30:41.280
to requests from
resolvers and sink rows,

00:30:41.280 --> 00:30:45.410
and communication links,
and things like that.

00:30:45.410 --> 00:30:49.920
And a multi-structured level of
interrupts and software levels

00:30:49.920 --> 00:30:53.760
of hierarchy.

00:30:53.760 --> 00:30:59.040
I also remember a
fateful day in 1963,

00:30:59.040 --> 00:31:03.360
sitting in the Apollo
computer lab, which

00:31:03.360 --> 00:31:06.910
was a big room, and then
a little room behind it.

00:31:06.910 --> 00:31:10.350
I was in the little
room behind it

00:31:10.350 --> 00:31:16.410
with the first brass board
Apollo computer prototype.

00:31:16.410 --> 00:31:23.250
I was debugging it when the
word came that Kennedy was shot.

00:31:23.250 --> 00:31:27.210
That was kind of a
real seminal moment.

00:31:27.210 --> 00:31:32.010
I think everybody became
particularly re-energized

00:31:32.010 --> 00:31:33.780
in making this project work.

00:31:41.974 --> 00:31:44.750
And that was my role.

00:31:44.750 --> 00:31:47.690
I did a little bit
of programming,

00:31:47.690 --> 00:31:57.480
when the design was finished,
to some orbital calculations.

00:31:57.480 --> 00:32:04.820
But my role was primarily in
the design of the hardware.

00:32:04.820 --> 00:32:08.450
PRESENTER 3: I'd like to add
something to what Herb did,

00:32:08.450 --> 00:32:14.210
which was a quote which I think
applies to the business of you

00:32:14.210 --> 00:32:16.340
could do this project today.

00:32:16.340 --> 00:32:22.560
At the time the Apollo computer
first became available,

00:32:22.560 --> 00:32:24.830
there was an internal
need to have something

00:32:24.830 --> 00:32:26.240
that could show that it worked.

00:32:26.240 --> 00:32:29.880
Not that it did the job, that
this computer was [? alive. ?]

00:32:29.880 --> 00:32:32.760
And Herb programmed
the little game,

00:32:32.760 --> 00:32:36.530
which would be completely
primitive by today's standards.

00:32:36.530 --> 00:32:39.140
But it was a little version
of "Pong," in which the ball

00:32:39.140 --> 00:32:41.400
bounced all over the place.

00:32:41.400 --> 00:32:45.260
And it was after doing that,
and then all the work that

00:32:45.260 --> 00:32:47.080
came after, that Herb--

00:32:47.080 --> 00:32:49.640
I heard Herb, when they say,
you can do so much with 4K

00:32:49.640 --> 00:32:52.410
and so little with 40.

00:32:52.410 --> 00:32:52.910
You know?

00:32:52.910 --> 00:32:56.510
You can do so much with five
people and so little with 400

00:32:56.510 --> 00:32:58.370
people all trying to
do the same job, which

00:32:58.370 --> 00:33:01.080
I think is part of the thing.

00:33:01.080 --> 00:33:03.410
And what I think the--

00:33:03.410 --> 00:33:08.000
in terms of why the
project can be done,

00:33:08.000 --> 00:33:10.100
I think the genius was
in the partitioning

00:33:10.100 --> 00:33:13.190
of this project that [? was ?]
based on several things.

00:33:13.190 --> 00:33:16.470
One is having a clear goal.

00:33:16.470 --> 00:33:20.010
The other having an institution
that was already talented.

00:33:20.010 --> 00:33:21.090
[? You ?] can't do that.

00:33:21.090 --> 00:33:22.640
You're going to go anywhere.

00:33:22.640 --> 00:33:27.230
But then, the fact that the job
was broken up so that people go

00:33:27.230 --> 00:33:30.200
work, as to what they did.

00:33:30.200 --> 00:33:33.620
And didn't have to constantly
cross-check everything

00:33:33.620 --> 00:33:34.490
at every second.

00:33:34.490 --> 00:33:36.260
There was plenty
of that to be done.

00:33:36.260 --> 00:33:37.760
But I think that
that's probably one

00:33:37.760 --> 00:33:40.820
of the [? signature ?]
characteristics

00:33:40.820 --> 00:33:42.530
of that project.

00:33:42.530 --> 00:33:46.430
I've been in many projects since
then-- and everybody here has,

00:33:46.430 --> 00:33:49.700
too-- and I think that those
projects that fail almost

00:33:49.700 --> 00:33:54.260
always had enormous
bureaucracies of cross-checking

00:33:54.260 --> 00:33:55.825
that just didn't seem--

00:33:55.825 --> 00:33:56.950
the partitioning was wrong.

00:34:02.534 --> 00:34:04.450
GEROVITCH: Do you think
the right partitioning

00:34:04.450 --> 00:34:07.030
was achieved by
intuition, or did

00:34:07.030 --> 00:34:09.620
that idea come from somewhere?

00:34:09.620 --> 00:34:13.389
PRESENTER 3: Well, generally,
somebody's-- there's got to be

00:34:13.389 --> 00:34:17.740
a combination of political and
technical architecting done.

00:34:17.740 --> 00:34:19.239
I don't know if
it's any one person.

00:34:19.239 --> 00:34:21.070
But I think the lab had--

00:34:21.070 --> 00:34:24.070
first of all, the lab was
divided by stovepipes.

00:34:24.070 --> 00:34:27.074
So there was the gyro group, the
[? Herschel ?] platform group.

00:34:27.074 --> 00:34:29.199
Then there were the single
commissionings, and then

00:34:29.199 --> 00:34:29.920
the optics group.

00:34:29.920 --> 00:34:31.780
And then the
computer group, which

00:34:31.780 --> 00:34:34.300
was kind of a foreign
object in the lab,

00:34:34.300 --> 00:34:37.929
as I recall, for many years.

00:34:37.929 --> 00:34:43.600
But so that partitioning by
discipline already existed.

00:34:43.600 --> 00:34:45.442
But even within that,
I think once the job

00:34:45.442 --> 00:34:46.900
was done that there
was going to be

00:34:46.900 --> 00:34:49.540
a mid-term guidance
and a booster guidance,

00:34:49.540 --> 00:34:50.350
and a reentry.

00:34:50.350 --> 00:34:52.090
And there's going
to be an abort.

00:34:52.090 --> 00:34:53.739
All these things
were partitioned--

00:34:53.739 --> 00:34:58.360
[? it's ?] probably Davie
[? Hoag ?] was the person who

00:34:58.360 --> 00:35:02.800
just thought that this was
the way the world should be.

00:35:02.800 --> 00:35:07.420
So I think that it requires
a [? architecting ?]

00:35:07.420 --> 00:35:08.910
visionary on that.

00:35:13.310 --> 00:35:14.810
PRESENTER 1: Our
space life consists

00:35:14.810 --> 00:35:19.240
of a lot of periods of
thrust and activity,

00:35:19.240 --> 00:35:22.490
followed by long
periods of just tracking

00:35:22.490 --> 00:35:25.280
where you are flying to.

00:35:25.280 --> 00:35:28.940
And so some of the partitioning
just came from the separation

00:35:28.940 --> 00:35:31.550
by periods of inactive flight.

00:35:31.550 --> 00:35:33.980
GEROVITCH: Sure, sure.

00:35:33.980 --> 00:35:35.585
PRESENTER 4: You want us to--

00:35:35.585 --> 00:35:36.710
are you through over there?

00:35:36.710 --> 00:35:37.750
Should we all start?

00:35:37.750 --> 00:35:38.416
PRESENTER: Sure.

00:35:38.416 --> 00:35:39.412
Now it's all yours.

00:35:39.412 --> 00:35:41.370
PRESENTER 4: Well, I
have, I think, two things.

00:35:41.370 --> 00:35:43.610
One, the overall
speed, I think you

00:35:43.610 --> 00:35:47.390
got to look that it was
a totally different era.

00:35:47.390 --> 00:35:50.860
It sort of had followed not that
long after World War II, when

00:35:50.860 --> 00:35:53.100
it still had the spirit
of whatever you got to do,

00:35:53.100 --> 00:35:56.720
you got to do it quick
and done, because there's

00:35:56.720 --> 00:35:58.250
a [? per ?] pressure.

00:35:58.250 --> 00:36:00.180
And the aerospace
industry, for instance,

00:36:00.180 --> 00:36:02.180
started with nothing when the--

00:36:02.180 --> 00:36:03.320
'41.

00:36:03.320 --> 00:36:09.330
And by '45, they had built
300,000 airplanes and bombers

00:36:09.330 --> 00:36:10.690
and transports.

00:36:10.690 --> 00:36:12.800
So they had an industry
that really tooled

00:36:12.800 --> 00:36:14.480
up and moved fast.

00:36:14.480 --> 00:36:18.200
And after Sputnik, we
were in another mode

00:36:18.200 --> 00:36:19.850
of moving that way.

00:36:19.850 --> 00:36:22.210
Some of us had worked
with [? Davy ?] [? Hoag ?]

00:36:22.210 --> 00:36:26.480
on the Polaris, and we worked
on the guidance and so forth.

00:36:26.480 --> 00:36:27.660
But those guys--

00:36:27.660 --> 00:36:30.650
Admiral Rayburn and
the special projects--

00:36:30.650 --> 00:36:32.060
they started, said go.

00:36:32.060 --> 00:36:36.290
In 18 months, they had a
submarine built and ready

00:36:36.290 --> 00:36:38.830
to take a missile by short--

00:36:38.830 --> 00:36:42.170
I mean, imagine sitting down
now and telling somebody, go,

00:36:42.170 --> 00:36:45.530
and building a
submarine and having it

00:36:45.530 --> 00:36:47.130
out there in 18 months.

00:36:47.130 --> 00:36:50.750
You couldn't do the
paperwork in 18 months.

00:36:50.750 --> 00:36:52.552
But those guys really moved.

00:36:52.552 --> 00:36:55.010
I never quite understood--
well, they took a submarine that

00:36:55.010 --> 00:36:57.605
existed, and split it
in two, and redid--

00:36:57.605 --> 00:37:00.270
it took all sorts
of tricks to do it.

00:37:00.270 --> 00:37:02.750
But those of us
who worked on that

00:37:02.750 --> 00:37:04.370
also saw that,
whatever you did, it

00:37:04.370 --> 00:37:06.560
was going to move
very, very rapidly.

00:37:06.560 --> 00:37:09.840
And we had [? Davy ?] [? Hoag ?]
to partition-- to lead us,

00:37:09.840 --> 00:37:12.420
who was really a genius,
as far as I'm concerned.

00:37:12.420 --> 00:37:15.410
A lot of us think he's the
mentor for engineering,

00:37:15.410 --> 00:37:17.990
showed us what
engineering really was.

00:37:17.990 --> 00:37:22.700
Then, when we moved to Apollo,
which [? Davy ?] said--

00:37:22.700 --> 00:37:25.460
last time was August 8, 1961.

00:37:25.460 --> 00:37:29.570
It was in Washington when they
awarded it, and some of us

00:37:29.570 --> 00:37:32.870
were there then, so we were
there from even the start.

00:37:32.870 --> 00:37:34.460
But it started, as Jim said.

00:37:34.460 --> 00:37:36.610
Then they started to
put together teams.

00:37:36.610 --> 00:37:38.340
There was a little
before, but not much.

00:37:38.340 --> 00:37:41.120
And built it in late '61.

00:37:41.120 --> 00:37:42.680
But you got to have two--

00:37:42.680 --> 00:37:44.510
there's really two
distinct phases

00:37:44.510 --> 00:37:47.600
with different objectives
and organization.

00:37:47.600 --> 00:37:49.640
First is the
engineering phase that

00:37:49.640 --> 00:37:52.650
lasted, well, two to
four or five years,

00:37:52.650 --> 00:37:53.930
depending on what part.

00:37:53.930 --> 00:37:58.340
And that's when you had
powered flight, rendezvous.

00:37:58.340 --> 00:38:00.210
I was in the reentry.

00:38:00.210 --> 00:38:01.820
Something else.

00:38:01.820 --> 00:38:05.840
LEM later, but I don't know
that it started that way.

00:38:05.840 --> 00:38:07.430
And you guys were
doing the simulator.

00:38:07.430 --> 00:38:07.929
Who else?

00:38:07.929 --> 00:38:10.070
There was about
five or six groups.

00:38:10.070 --> 00:38:12.620
But it was definitely--
engineering organized

00:38:12.620 --> 00:38:14.690
different parts of it.

00:38:14.690 --> 00:38:18.530
And Dick [? Batten ?] was
the guy that ran all of it.

00:38:18.530 --> 00:38:20.180
But we were all involved--

00:38:20.180 --> 00:38:20.660
PRESENTER 5: Well, he
didn't really run it.

00:38:20.660 --> 00:38:22.201
PRESENTER 4: He
didn't really run it.

00:38:22.201 --> 00:38:23.129
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:38:23.129 --> 00:38:24.170
PRESENTER 4: He presided.

00:38:24.170 --> 00:38:26.630
PRESENTER 5: And he
was real hands-off.

00:38:26.630 --> 00:38:27.887
PRESENTER 4: Real hands-off.

00:38:27.887 --> 00:38:28.970
PRESENTER 5: Just a guru--

00:38:28.970 --> 00:38:29.180
PRESENTER 4: Right.

00:38:29.180 --> 00:38:30.080
PRESENTER 5: --in
his corner office.

00:38:30.080 --> 00:38:31.170
PRESENTER 4: Very
smart in his corner,

00:38:31.170 --> 00:38:32.670
and smoked his pipe and nodded.

00:38:32.670 --> 00:38:33.770
PRESENTER 5: Everybody thought
he was just writing a book,

00:38:33.770 --> 00:38:35.990
but by golly, he knew
exactly what was going on.

00:38:35.990 --> 00:38:37.170
And he didn't interfere.

00:38:37.170 --> 00:38:39.600
And to me, that is one of
the secrets of success.

00:38:39.600 --> 00:38:42.789
Again, as I said before, it
was the fewness of the people--

00:38:42.789 --> 00:38:43.580
PRESENTER 4: Right.

00:38:43.580 --> 00:38:45.830
PRESENTER 5: --that allowed
this massive project

00:38:45.830 --> 00:38:47.789
to be shoehorned into
this teeny box, and work.

00:38:47.789 --> 00:38:49.663
PRESENTER 4: Each of
those engineering groups

00:38:49.663 --> 00:38:51.800
was quite small, as
[INAUDIBLE] said.

00:38:51.800 --> 00:38:54.740
We had three or four in our
entry, and each of these others

00:38:54.740 --> 00:38:55.940
didn't have a lot.

00:38:55.940 --> 00:38:57.140
And you had a free hand.

00:38:57.140 --> 00:39:00.500
NASA was very busy
with a lot of other--

00:39:00.500 --> 00:39:02.180
they had a lot of
other things cooking.

00:39:02.180 --> 00:39:04.280
So we had a lot of ability.

00:39:04.280 --> 00:39:07.730
We went to these panel
meetings every month or two.

00:39:07.730 --> 00:39:09.620
In my case, it was
Aaron [? Cohen. ?]

00:39:09.620 --> 00:39:12.050
And a lot of other
NASA that you-- you

00:39:12.050 --> 00:39:13.730
informed them what
you were doing,

00:39:13.730 --> 00:39:15.680
and waited for
critical comments.

00:39:15.680 --> 00:39:17.480
But you weren't ridden.

00:39:17.480 --> 00:39:18.230
PRESENTER 5: Yeah.

00:39:18.230 --> 00:39:20.459
I had a single point
of contact with NASA.

00:39:20.459 --> 00:39:21.500
Tom Gibson, remember him?

00:39:21.500 --> 00:39:22.291
PRESENTER 4: Right.

00:39:22.291 --> 00:39:24.080
PRESENTER 5: The
cigar-chomping guy?

00:39:24.080 --> 00:39:25.316
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:39:25.316 --> 00:39:26.690
PRESENTER 5: He
oversaw our work.

00:39:26.690 --> 00:39:27.830
But he didn't interfere.

00:39:27.830 --> 00:39:28.190
PRESENTER 4: Right.

00:39:28.190 --> 00:39:29.090
PRESENTER 5: We told him.

00:39:29.090 --> 00:39:30.710
There were not
hordes of bureaucrats

00:39:30.710 --> 00:39:32.684
who caused you to
fill in forms and make

00:39:32.684 --> 00:39:34.850
present [INAUDIBLE] briefings
and that kind of crap.

00:39:34.850 --> 00:39:36.560
We didn't have that at all.

00:39:36.560 --> 00:39:40.169
You kept Tom happy, he
kept them off your back.

00:39:40.169 --> 00:39:41.710
And you went on with
the engineering.

00:39:41.710 --> 00:39:44.180
PRESENTER 4: But then we
awoke, several years later,

00:39:44.180 --> 00:39:48.240
realizing we had a big
programming problem.

00:39:48.240 --> 00:39:49.820
So we switched from
the engineering--

00:39:49.820 --> 00:39:53.420
develop equations, make
simulations, study,

00:39:53.420 --> 00:39:55.220
develop guidance equations.

00:39:55.220 --> 00:39:58.170
We shifted to a different mode.

00:39:58.170 --> 00:40:00.080
We had a program,
[? this ?] AGC.

00:40:00.080 --> 00:40:02.360
And now, we needed
a new organization,

00:40:02.360 --> 00:40:03.650
different people leading it.

00:40:03.650 --> 00:40:06.750
The organization
changed entirely.

00:40:06.750 --> 00:40:08.720
And now, it was more
mission-oriented,

00:40:08.720 --> 00:40:11.570
where you integrated
all the needs into--

00:40:11.570 --> 00:40:14.480
like, he started the 202
one, and different people

00:40:14.480 --> 00:40:17.510
took over other ones, and
integrated the efforts

00:40:17.510 --> 00:40:20.690
of these different groups,
and really had to program it.

00:40:20.690 --> 00:40:22.610
Because in most
cases, the engineers

00:40:22.610 --> 00:40:25.115
did a so-so
programming job, and it

00:40:25.115 --> 00:40:28.100
had to be redone and put into
a form that would [INAUDIBLE]

00:40:28.100 --> 00:40:31.880
consistent for a
particular flight.

00:40:31.880 --> 00:40:35.120
And you found stupid errors
from engineers here and there,

00:40:35.120 --> 00:40:37.880
like somebody, for
pi, using 22 over 7.

00:40:37.880 --> 00:40:39.770
[LAUGHTER]

00:40:39.770 --> 00:40:41.840
We kept getting errors,
and couldn't figure out.

00:40:41.840 --> 00:40:44.070
And then this guy--

00:40:44.070 --> 00:40:45.860
I mean, he was from
the old school.

00:40:45.860 --> 00:40:46.972
[LAUGHTER]

00:40:46.972 --> 00:40:47.930
[? Good ?] enough for--

00:40:47.930 --> 00:40:49.066
PRESENTER 5: Good enough
for a [INAUDIBLE]..

00:40:49.066 --> 00:40:49.672
Exactly.

00:40:49.672 --> 00:40:51.380
PRESENTER 4: And there
were subtle errors

00:40:51.380 --> 00:40:53.270
that even got to the flight.

00:40:53.270 --> 00:40:55.930
You think Earth rate is
a well-known constant,

00:40:55.930 --> 00:40:58.100
hasn't changed very much.

00:40:58.100 --> 00:40:59.360
Earth rate is Earth rate.

00:40:59.360 --> 00:41:03.620
But for us novices, although
we knew more at the time,

00:41:03.620 --> 00:41:06.260
the earth rotates
once in 24 hours.

00:41:06.260 --> 00:41:09.290
And that's what the guy
used on his calculation.

00:41:09.290 --> 00:41:10.730
But that isn't right.

00:41:10.730 --> 00:41:13.850
The Earth does not
rotate once in 24 hours.

00:41:13.850 --> 00:41:17.030
24 hours is when it gets
back to the sun again.

00:41:17.030 --> 00:41:18.980
But if you're doing the
Earth's rotation rate,

00:41:18.980 --> 00:41:22.760
it actually rotates in about
23 hours and 56 minutes,

00:41:22.760 --> 00:41:23.730
as I recall.

00:41:23.730 --> 00:41:26.170
And if you use the
difference, your longitude

00:41:26.170 --> 00:41:28.310
is going to be way
off, which it was.

00:41:28.310 --> 00:41:31.970
And I can't remember what
early flight there was.

00:41:31.970 --> 00:41:33.850
So things did get through.

00:41:33.850 --> 00:41:38.600
But the programming effort
was directed differently

00:41:38.600 --> 00:41:39.890
and organized differently.

00:41:39.890 --> 00:41:41.090
And these guys really--

00:41:41.090 --> 00:41:48.680
Margaret, Alex, Fred all really
took over and, in a very good,

00:41:48.680 --> 00:41:49.660
delete all of those.

00:41:49.660 --> 00:41:52.250
Then we had a break into
LEM versus command module.

00:41:52.250 --> 00:41:54.590
LEM engineering took a lot
longer, because [? Grumman ?]

00:41:54.590 --> 00:41:55.910
was longer, they didn't know--

00:41:55.910 --> 00:41:59.330
some of us thought we were
done, and they were just almost

00:41:59.330 --> 00:42:02.090
getting started,
Allen [? Klumpf. ?]

00:42:02.090 --> 00:42:04.310
And then the auto
pilots came along, which

00:42:04.310 --> 00:42:07.310
required some more engineering.

00:42:07.310 --> 00:42:08.522
Anyway, I've--

00:42:08.522 --> 00:42:09.980
PRESENTER 1: For
a long time, there

00:42:09.980 --> 00:42:14.330
was a myth that was absolutely
nothing more than a myth,

00:42:14.330 --> 00:42:19.100
that each mission program
was a slight enhancement

00:42:19.100 --> 00:42:21.020
of the one that went before.

00:42:21.020 --> 00:42:23.300
So that all you had to
do for the next one was

00:42:23.300 --> 00:42:24.640
the enhancement.

00:42:24.640 --> 00:42:26.930
The myth part was
that you had to start

00:42:26.930 --> 00:42:30.110
all over again because
everything was different.

00:42:30.110 --> 00:42:31.780
Somehow the hardware
was different

00:42:31.780 --> 00:42:33.730
or the mission was different.

00:42:33.730 --> 00:42:36.020
There were very
few things that--

00:42:36.020 --> 00:42:38.150
PRESENTER 4: I think I'm
the only one that we flew

00:42:38.150 --> 00:42:39.830
exactly the same thing twice.

00:42:39.830 --> 00:42:40.540
[LAUGHTER]

00:42:41.137 --> 00:42:42.470
I don't think there's any other.

00:42:42.470 --> 00:42:43.219
Maybe [INAUDIBLE].

00:42:43.219 --> 00:42:46.550
501 and 502 were identical
because they were unmanned.

00:42:46.550 --> 00:42:47.300
There was nothing.

00:42:47.300 --> 00:42:49.970
And I think it was
absolutely the same thing.

00:42:49.970 --> 00:42:51.846
But I don't think we
did any others that way.

00:42:51.846 --> 00:42:53.636
PRESENTER 1: But the
trouble is a lot of us

00:42:53.636 --> 00:42:55.970
were fooled into believing
the myth somehow,

00:42:55.970 --> 00:42:59.322
even after we knew it really
wasn't working that way.

00:42:59.322 --> 00:43:01.280
And thought, with these
small groups of people,

00:43:01.280 --> 00:43:02.830
we could handle it.

00:43:02.830 --> 00:43:05.480
I remember these talks
in which we said,

00:43:05.480 --> 00:43:08.970
we got to do this with either
about five people or maybe six.

00:43:08.970 --> 00:43:10.910
Or we got to do it with 150.

00:43:10.910 --> 00:43:12.880
The number we've got,
which is about 30 or 40,

00:43:12.880 --> 00:43:16.040
is absolutely going to
kill us if we keep on.

00:43:16.040 --> 00:43:18.800
We have to do either
one way or the other.

00:43:18.800 --> 00:43:21.290
I think, happily,
we went to the 150,

00:43:21.290 --> 00:43:23.070
although it wasn't
fun at the time.

00:43:23.070 --> 00:43:25.120
PRESENTER 4: No, it
was very difficult.

00:43:25.120 --> 00:43:26.665
PRESENTER 1: A
whole new culture.

00:43:26.665 --> 00:43:29.040
PRESENTER 4: Especially when
you had what's-his-name from

00:43:29.040 --> 00:43:30.380
IBM's--

00:43:30.380 --> 00:43:32.660
Brooks [? Law, ?] that
adding more people

00:43:32.660 --> 00:43:35.792
to a job that's in trouble makes
it later and things like that.

00:43:35.792 --> 00:43:37.250
PRESENTER 1: We
couldn't figure out

00:43:37.250 --> 00:43:40.040
why anybody else needed to
know what was in the guidance

00:43:40.040 --> 00:43:41.460
computer besides us.

00:43:45.497 --> 00:43:46.830
What does NASA need to know for?

00:43:46.830 --> 00:43:48.746
Why do we have to publish
all these documents?

00:43:52.460 --> 00:43:56.330
But we, of course, did have
to publish all that stuff.

00:43:56.330 --> 00:43:58.390
Speaking of the point
that Fred apparently

00:43:58.390 --> 00:44:03.490
raised in the prior meeting,
and that Dan touched on,

00:44:03.490 --> 00:44:04.776
I remember--

00:44:04.776 --> 00:44:06.400
I mentioned Hal
Laning, who had written

00:44:06.400 --> 00:44:10.480
the executive for
the Mars computer,

00:44:10.480 --> 00:44:12.280
and who stayed
involved for a while,

00:44:12.280 --> 00:44:17.140
but decided he didn't like the
big money and the big pressure.

00:44:17.140 --> 00:44:17.940
Got off.

00:44:17.940 --> 00:44:20.470
But one day, there
was a meeting sort

00:44:20.470 --> 00:44:24.190
of late in the Apollo
project, in which--

00:44:24.190 --> 00:44:28.720
it was a two-day meeting at the
laboratory of about 30 people

00:44:28.720 --> 00:44:32.680
to decide whether the executive
program for the shuttle

00:44:32.680 --> 00:44:34.470
computer should be
[? interrupt-driven ?]

00:44:34.470 --> 00:44:36.910
or schedule-driven.

00:44:36.910 --> 00:44:39.340
And I'm not sure it
came to a conclusion

00:44:39.340 --> 00:44:41.500
after 60 person days.

00:44:41.500 --> 00:44:44.740
But Hal said to me,
these people have just

00:44:44.740 --> 00:44:47.550
spent more time-- more
man days-- in that meeting

00:44:47.550 --> 00:44:51.280
than it took me to conceive
of and write and debug

00:44:51.280 --> 00:44:54.940
the executive for
the Apollo computer.

00:44:54.940 --> 00:44:58.900
I mean, things just had
changed by orders of magnitude,

00:44:58.900 --> 00:45:03.490
and it was clear to me it wasn't
going to be fun anymore to work

00:45:03.490 --> 00:45:06.264
on that sort of
stuff, so I didn't.

00:45:06.264 --> 00:45:08.680
PRESENTER 6: I'm very familiar
with this particular issue.

00:45:08.680 --> 00:45:11.700
And I'll get into it
a little bit later.

00:45:11.700 --> 00:45:19.880
It is the tail,
almost, of a long story

00:45:19.880 --> 00:45:26.410
that I would categorize
as almost a religious war

00:45:26.410 --> 00:45:31.180
between the synchronous
executive folks

00:45:31.180 --> 00:45:35.350
and the [? interim ?]
priority-driven folks.

00:45:35.350 --> 00:45:40.000
And it mushroomed in
the shuttle program,

00:45:40.000 --> 00:45:42.880
and it had its roots in Apollo.

00:45:42.880 --> 00:45:47.970
And perhaps, I'll get into
that a little bit later.

00:45:54.677 --> 00:45:57.010
PRESENTER 1: You asked about
trust in automatic systems.

00:45:57.010 --> 00:46:00.790
I remember-- just
a short anecdote.

00:46:00.790 --> 00:46:02.860
We were visited at the
laboratory one day--

00:46:02.860 --> 00:46:05.850
and I don't remember when,
but fairly early on--

00:46:05.850 --> 00:46:07.690
by John Glenn and
[? Alan ?] Shepard,

00:46:07.690 --> 00:46:09.690
and I think there was
a third astronaut.

00:46:09.690 --> 00:46:11.820
I don't remember who.

00:46:11.820 --> 00:46:14.740
And it was at the time
when reporters were still

00:46:14.740 --> 00:46:17.830
chasing around after
the seven astronauts,

00:46:17.830 --> 00:46:22.060
and it was a big thing when
the astronauts were coming.

00:46:22.060 --> 00:46:28.700
And the laboratory was showing
those folks what we were doing.

00:46:28.700 --> 00:46:32.800
And one of the things
that, at the time,

00:46:32.800 --> 00:46:36.480
we kind of had the arrogance
to believe we should do

00:46:36.480 --> 00:46:40.690
was to make sure
that nothing could

00:46:40.690 --> 00:46:45.730
be done by mistake by the
crew that would endanger them.

00:46:45.730 --> 00:46:47.180
So there were a
lot of tests that

00:46:47.180 --> 00:46:50.290
said if the delta v is
in the wrong direction,

00:46:50.290 --> 00:46:51.220
or this or that--

00:46:51.220 --> 00:46:53.136
I can't remember what
all the conditions were.

00:46:53.136 --> 00:46:54.790
There were lots of conditions.

00:46:54.790 --> 00:46:58.990
Then they couldn't
execute the proceed order

00:46:58.990 --> 00:47:01.100
to go ahead because
something was-- you know,

00:47:01.100 --> 00:47:03.740
the software thought
something was wrong.

00:47:03.740 --> 00:47:07.970
And [? Shepard, ?] with his
marvelous way about words,

00:47:07.970 --> 00:47:10.780
I remember two
things he said there.

00:47:10.780 --> 00:47:13.450
But this one, he
said, you guys think

00:47:13.450 --> 00:47:15.340
you know more
about what is going

00:47:15.340 --> 00:47:19.930
to face us during flight than
we will know during the flight.

00:47:19.930 --> 00:47:21.560
He says, you're wrong.

00:47:21.560 --> 00:47:24.100
He said, take all of
those inhibitions out.

00:47:24.100 --> 00:47:27.070
He said, if we want to
kill ourselves, let us.

00:47:27.070 --> 00:47:30.000
It may involve saving ourselves.

00:47:30.000 --> 00:47:32.740
Anyway, of course he was right,
and I think everybody sort

00:47:32.740 --> 00:47:33.670
of woke up to that.

00:47:33.670 --> 00:47:35.402
It made the software
a lot easier, too,

00:47:35.402 --> 00:47:37.610
[? because we didn't ?] have
to worry about all those

00:47:37.610 --> 00:47:38.151
[? checks. ?]

00:47:38.151 --> 00:47:39.460
[LAUGHTER]

00:47:39.460 --> 00:47:41.004
But he was adamant about that.

00:47:41.004 --> 00:47:43.420
PRESENTER 4: They did let him
start the pre-launch program

00:47:43.420 --> 00:47:43.800
on the way--

00:47:43.800 --> 00:47:44.883
PRESENTER 1: That's right.

00:47:44.883 --> 00:47:46.160
[LAUGHTER]

00:47:46.660 --> 00:47:49.659
Well, they weren't
capable of perfection.

00:47:49.659 --> 00:47:51.700
They were pretty well-trained,
and they certainly

00:47:51.700 --> 00:47:52.150
were motivated.

00:47:52.150 --> 00:47:54.566
PRESENTER 4: They were very
well-trained, I will say that.

00:47:54.566 --> 00:47:56.320
But they also got very tired.

00:47:56.320 --> 00:47:57.070
PRESENTER 1: Yeah.

00:47:57.070 --> 00:47:58.611
PRESENTER 4: I had
the reentry, which

00:47:58.611 --> 00:48:01.690
is at the end of
five days out there.

00:48:01.690 --> 00:48:03.550
And they swore, through
all the early years,

00:48:03.550 --> 00:48:05.480
that they were going
to fly it manually.

00:48:05.480 --> 00:48:06.970
And as far as I
know, none of them

00:48:06.970 --> 00:48:08.800
ever touched the manual stick.

00:48:08.800 --> 00:48:09.970
I mean, they were so beat.

00:48:09.970 --> 00:48:12.760
And then, all of a
sudden, you hit seven G's.

00:48:12.760 --> 00:48:17.590
I'm not sure they could even
see what was going on by then.

00:48:17.590 --> 00:48:21.190
Because I mean, they
didn't get any sleep out

00:48:21.190 --> 00:48:25.510
there for all that
time, really, did they?

00:48:25.510 --> 00:48:28.000
I'm surprised they were as--

00:48:28.000 --> 00:48:30.520
doing as well as they
did after all that time.

00:48:33.490 --> 00:48:34.240
PRESENTER 7: Yeah.

00:48:34.240 --> 00:48:38.050
I think [? our ?] we
had a different opinion

00:48:38.050 --> 00:48:43.290
of this subject matter than
the astronauts did, or at least

00:48:43.290 --> 00:48:46.780
the software group did,
at the time that this took

00:48:46.780 --> 00:48:48.720
place, the pre-launch.

00:48:48.720 --> 00:48:51.830
And we talked about
that the last time,

00:48:51.830 --> 00:48:57.190
when he selected
pre-launch during flight.

00:48:57.190 --> 00:49:00.010
So I think there's
sort of a in-between.

00:49:00.010 --> 00:49:02.500
You don't want to
overdo it, but you also

00:49:02.500 --> 00:49:06.960
want to make the
crucial areas, where

00:49:06.960 --> 00:49:09.042
you can't have a catastrophe.

00:49:11.395 --> 00:49:13.020
PRESENTER 1: Shepherd,
in that meeting,

00:49:13.020 --> 00:49:15.560
was responsible, I think, for
the demise of another thought

00:49:15.560 --> 00:49:19.310
that we had had, which
was that the computer, not

00:49:19.310 --> 00:49:20.900
being perfectly
reliable, it was going

00:49:20.900 --> 00:49:23.702
to be possible to do
in-flight repairs.

00:49:23.702 --> 00:49:25.160
And so there were
a lot of thoughts

00:49:25.160 --> 00:49:26.930
about diagnosing
what had gone wrong

00:49:26.930 --> 00:49:28.790
and changing modules
and so forth.

00:49:28.790 --> 00:49:31.220
I don't know how far along
that got, but [? Shepherd's ?]

00:49:31.220 --> 00:49:33.053
comment to that was,
yeah, and we should all

00:49:33.053 --> 00:49:35.730
train to be brain surgeons so
we can operate on each other.

00:49:35.730 --> 00:49:38.390
And I think that was about the
end of the in-flight repair.

00:49:42.294 --> 00:49:44.210
PRESENTER 6: I think
there were two areas here

00:49:44.210 --> 00:49:47.110
that people are talking about
that somewhat different.

00:49:47.110 --> 00:49:54.110
There was the small group,
seat-of-the-pants effort that

00:49:54.110 --> 00:49:59.360
was unfettered and unhindered,
for the most part, by NASA,

00:49:59.360 --> 00:50:05.940
and devoid, pretty much,
of real bureaucracy.

00:50:05.940 --> 00:50:09.880
And I think that, as Dan
pointed out, you had--

00:50:09.880 --> 00:50:11.900
a lot of the fundamental
engineering work

00:50:11.900 --> 00:50:14.040
was done during that period.

00:50:14.040 --> 00:50:17.180
And it was very
fortunate that we

00:50:17.180 --> 00:50:20.110
were able to do it unfettered.

00:50:20.110 --> 00:50:26.330
Because I think that a lot of
the fundamental issues on how

00:50:26.330 --> 00:50:30.320
to get to the moon,
let's say, were solved

00:50:30.320 --> 00:50:34.790
in this period of isolation--

00:50:34.790 --> 00:50:38.450
almost isolation
[? and ?] small groups.

00:50:38.450 --> 00:50:43.010
And these various
flights that have

00:50:43.010 --> 00:50:45.670
been talked about, I think the
term-- when Alex was talking,

00:50:45.670 --> 00:50:47.659
the term system mother
came into my head.

00:50:47.659 --> 00:50:48.950
PRESENTER 1: [? Rope ?] mother.

00:50:48.950 --> 00:50:49.080
PRESENTER 7: [? Rope ?] mother.

00:50:49.080 --> 00:50:49.570
PRESENTER 6: Rope mother.

00:50:49.570 --> 00:50:50.291
PRESENTER 1: Yes.

00:50:50.291 --> 00:50:50.790
[INAUDIBLE]

00:50:50.790 --> 00:50:53.690
PRESENTER 6: And this
was a person who was--

00:50:53.690 --> 00:50:57.110
the responsibility was
focused upon this person

00:50:57.110 --> 00:51:01.050
for a particular
flight, even though it

00:51:01.050 --> 00:51:03.630
was a relatively small group.

00:51:03.630 --> 00:51:06.260
But I think this era was--

00:51:06.260 --> 00:51:10.130
I would say this is the
pre-built [INAUDIBLE] era.

00:51:10.130 --> 00:51:13.560
And I think that,
somewhere along the line,

00:51:13.560 --> 00:51:16.820
someone did some, let's
say, back-of-the-envelope

00:51:16.820 --> 00:51:22.490
calculations about how many
lines of code we have to do,

00:51:22.490 --> 00:51:24.560
how many person months--

00:51:24.560 --> 00:51:26.780
how many lines of code per
person month and things

00:51:26.780 --> 00:51:27.770
like that.

00:51:27.770 --> 00:51:30.140
And came up with
a number of people

00:51:30.140 --> 00:51:34.490
that were just completely
different than what the lab had

00:51:34.490 --> 00:51:36.360
in doing this work.

00:51:36.360 --> 00:51:38.420
So instead of
having 30 people, we

00:51:38.420 --> 00:51:41.280
needed 200 people or
something like that.

00:51:41.280 --> 00:51:44.990
And not only that, but we
needed all this documentation,

00:51:44.990 --> 00:51:47.030
and we needed all
these review meetings,

00:51:47.030 --> 00:51:48.950
and we needed all
NASA supervision,

00:51:48.950 --> 00:51:53.380
and we needed a lot of stuff.

00:51:53.380 --> 00:51:57.680
And at that point, there was a
LEM that was being split off,

00:51:57.680 --> 00:52:04.220
and we sort of became quote,
unquote "professionalized,"

00:52:04.220 --> 00:52:06.830
in the sense that I
remember one day--

00:52:06.830 --> 00:52:11.120
and I can remember
the day very clearly.

00:52:14.480 --> 00:52:18.590
There was an evening meeting
about 7:30 in the evening,

00:52:18.590 --> 00:52:21.600
with Ralph Reagan
running the meeting.

00:52:21.600 --> 00:52:25.880
And the lab has been under a lot
of pressure in previous weeks

00:52:25.880 --> 00:52:30.530
with respect to NASA
coming down and questioning

00:52:30.530 --> 00:52:32.300
their organization,
and questioning

00:52:32.300 --> 00:52:35.300
their documentation, and
questioning their testing

00:52:35.300 --> 00:52:37.340
and questioning everything.

00:52:37.340 --> 00:52:41.057
And I was told, that
evening, that we're

00:52:41.057 --> 00:52:42.640
going to have a
different organization

00:52:42.640 --> 00:52:44.014
and we're going
to have something

00:52:44.014 --> 00:52:46.240
called Project Managers.

00:52:46.240 --> 00:52:48.464
And we didn't have
any project managers.

00:52:48.464 --> 00:52:49.880
We didn't have
that title, anyway,

00:52:49.880 --> 00:52:51.710
even though you might
think of a [? rope ?]

00:52:51.710 --> 00:52:54.050
mother being a project manager.

00:52:54.050 --> 00:52:56.900
And that's when I was
appointed the project

00:52:56.900 --> 00:53:00.530
manager of CSM software.

00:53:00.530 --> 00:53:02.360
And another individual,
George Cherry,

00:53:02.360 --> 00:53:06.770
was appointed the project
manager of LEM software.

00:53:06.770 --> 00:53:09.710
And these groups now
were fairly large,

00:53:09.710 --> 00:53:13.580
and NASA wanted to
see formal schedules,

00:53:13.580 --> 00:53:18.110
and time to complete
this module,

00:53:18.110 --> 00:53:19.640
and time to complete
that module,

00:53:19.640 --> 00:53:22.430
and reporting on a monthly--

00:53:22.430 --> 00:53:24.770
I can't even remember the
schedule, what it was.

00:53:24.770 --> 00:53:32.240
But things started to build up
in reviews and in paperwork,

00:53:32.240 --> 00:53:35.720
and in monitoring and so on.

00:53:35.720 --> 00:53:38.670
And I would say that that
became a different era

00:53:38.670 --> 00:53:40.940
of doing work at
the lab, and much

00:53:40.940 --> 00:53:44.025
less fun for a lot of people.

00:53:44.025 --> 00:53:44.900
GEROVITCH: Excuse me.

00:53:44.900 --> 00:53:46.722
Can you put a date
on that switch?

00:53:46.722 --> 00:53:49.180
PRESENTER 4: It was after Gemini
that NASA descended on us,

00:53:49.180 --> 00:53:50.960
but I don't know quite
when the date was.

00:53:50.960 --> 00:53:51.710
PRESENTER 6: Yeah.

00:53:51.710 --> 00:53:53.720
I would say--

00:53:53.720 --> 00:53:59.060
I'm guessing, and I'm doing
it on my own time schedule.

00:53:59.060 --> 00:54:01.110
I would say it's either--

00:54:01.110 --> 00:54:04.160
it's either in the
fall of [? 1960-- ?] I

00:54:04.160 --> 00:54:09.560
think it's the fall of 1966.

00:54:09.560 --> 00:54:11.674
I don't think it's
into '67, But I--

00:54:11.674 --> 00:54:13.590
PRESENTER 4: By '67, you
were [? in plates. ?]

00:54:13.590 --> 00:54:16.727
PRESENTER 6: I think it
was the fall of 1966.

00:54:16.727 --> 00:54:18.810
GEROVITCH: And what do you
think actually prompted

00:54:18.810 --> 00:54:20.839
the change in NASA's attitude?

00:54:20.839 --> 00:54:22.880
Was it the concern of one
particular [? faser? ?]

00:54:22.880 --> 00:54:25.940
PRESENTER 6: Well, I think
that's a very good question,

00:54:25.940 --> 00:54:29.790
and I have an
opinion, but it's just

00:54:29.790 --> 00:54:33.510
a completely personal view.

00:54:33.510 --> 00:54:40.890
The people at MIT were
very smart, very talented.

00:54:40.890 --> 00:54:43.140
We certainly produced a lot.

00:54:43.140 --> 00:54:47.700
But we were also, I think, sort
of arrogant from the outside.

00:54:47.700 --> 00:54:50.610
And I think that that
arrogance started

00:54:50.610 --> 00:54:58.320
to irritate people who were
tired of hearing that MIT

00:54:58.320 --> 00:55:00.160
knew best, and knew better.

00:55:00.160 --> 00:55:01.800
And you don't even
know what you want.

00:55:01.800 --> 00:55:03.670
We know what you
want, and so on.

00:55:03.670 --> 00:55:09.990
And I think that that was part
of why there was an effort

00:55:09.990 --> 00:55:14.580
to almost sort of rein
in MIT, or to get them

00:55:14.580 --> 00:55:17.280
on this program, not
their own program.

00:55:17.280 --> 00:55:20.760
And then that NASA
is the boss, not MIT.

00:55:20.760 --> 00:55:22.770
And I think there was
a little bit of that.

00:55:22.770 --> 00:55:29.220
Plus, there may have been some
perfectly honest professionals

00:55:29.220 --> 00:55:32.730
at NASA who had had experience
with large programs,

00:55:32.730 --> 00:55:34.350
and just felt that
this was not going

00:55:34.350 --> 00:55:40.250
to get there in the manner in
which it had been going so far.

00:55:40.250 --> 00:55:42.500
GEROVITCH: So you don't
recall any particular conflict

00:55:42.500 --> 00:55:43.708
that may have prompted NASA--

00:55:43.708 --> 00:55:47.310
PRESENTER 4: Well,
those operations guys

00:55:47.310 --> 00:55:51.900
that you see down in all those
rooms in Houston were doing

00:55:51.900 --> 00:55:54.470
first Mercury and Gemini,
and zillions of [? them-- ?]

00:55:54.470 --> 00:55:55.380
flight controllers.

00:55:55.380 --> 00:55:56.970
And all of a sudden,
they said, now

00:55:56.970 --> 00:55:58.800
what are we going
to do for Apollo?

00:55:58.800 --> 00:56:02.070
And they had nothing in the
way of documents, procedures.

00:56:02.070 --> 00:56:03.237
All of it was really scanty.

00:56:03.237 --> 00:56:05.361
PRESENTER 6: [INAUDIBLE]
what they created, though,

00:56:05.361 --> 00:56:06.550
[INAUDIBLE] as well.

00:56:06.550 --> 00:56:08.010
I can remember this.

00:56:08.010 --> 00:56:09.990
They had a--

00:56:09.990 --> 00:56:14.770
NASA, at Houston, on this
seventh floor in Building 1,

00:56:14.770 --> 00:56:15.270
I think.

00:56:15.270 --> 00:56:20.790
They had this beautiful
conference room,

00:56:20.790 --> 00:56:22.620
really beautiful
conference room.

00:56:22.620 --> 00:56:25.860
And that's where they held a
lot of their big conferences.

00:56:25.860 --> 00:56:28.890
And they built a wall
on this conference

00:56:28.890 --> 00:56:36.270
room of sliding
translucent boards that

00:56:36.270 --> 00:56:38.810
were like this blackboard--

00:56:38.810 --> 00:56:39.330
whiteboard.

00:56:39.330 --> 00:56:42.750
And they were white,
and they were--

00:56:42.750 --> 00:56:46.700
they went deep, like six deep,
so that you could push one

00:56:46.700 --> 00:56:49.020
and there was one behind
it, and push another one.

00:56:49.020 --> 00:56:51.750
And you had a big, long-- it
was about the size of this wall,

00:56:51.750 --> 00:56:54.240
and you could move things
from one side to the other

00:56:54.240 --> 00:56:55.440
and so on.

00:56:55.440 --> 00:56:59.540
And on this wall, they
created a schedule.

00:56:59.540 --> 00:57:02.640
Today, we call it a Gantt chart.

00:57:02.640 --> 00:57:05.910
And they had somebody who
was taking care of this.

00:57:05.910 --> 00:57:11.340
Some contractor who would come
up every day with his blue tape

00:57:11.340 --> 00:57:13.740
or red tape or
yellow tape, and mark

00:57:13.740 --> 00:57:16.070
where the schedules were,
and all these things,

00:57:16.070 --> 00:57:17.230
and what was going on.

00:57:17.230 --> 00:57:21.720
And they could roll this board
back from that board and so on.

00:57:21.720 --> 00:57:24.240
And this was being
kept, eventually,

00:57:24.240 --> 00:57:28.200
daily by this contractor.

00:57:28.200 --> 00:57:32.040
And it was
magnificent to see it.

00:57:32.040 --> 00:57:34.910
That's how much they
got into the schedules

00:57:34.910 --> 00:57:39.490
and sort of the project
management of the program.

00:57:39.490 --> 00:57:41.160
PRESENTER 4: Yeah,
and MIT was woefully

00:57:41.160 --> 00:57:46.900
unprepared for all the tons of
stuff and support and so forth.

00:57:46.900 --> 00:57:49.350
So they had to
reorganize all together.

00:57:49.350 --> 00:57:50.647
And it would have been--

00:57:50.647 --> 00:57:52.230
I don't know how it
would have worked.

00:57:52.230 --> 00:57:53.850
There were a few
people, like Fred,

00:57:53.850 --> 00:57:56.940
who really did a great
job of dealing with them,

00:57:56.940 --> 00:57:59.850
and trying to shape
the rest of us up,

00:57:59.850 --> 00:58:02.310
put on some reasonable
form for it.

00:58:02.310 --> 00:58:05.280
Plus, there was Bill Tindall,
that you have Tindall grams

00:58:05.280 --> 00:58:07.890
that we dealt with,
who was up here a lot

00:58:07.890 --> 00:58:11.070
trying to work the
problem, as they

00:58:11.070 --> 00:58:12.690
would have said in those days.

00:58:12.690 --> 00:58:15.390
And say what you may,
he did a great job

00:58:15.390 --> 00:58:21.570
in trying to match the--

00:58:21.570 --> 00:58:24.540
GEROVITCH: So were you seriously
adopting these techniques

00:58:24.540 --> 00:58:26.520
in your own group,
or were you just

00:58:26.520 --> 00:58:31.050
kind of shielding
that group from NASA,

00:58:31.050 --> 00:58:33.990
allowing old practices
in the groups to go on?

00:58:36.462 --> 00:58:38.670
PRESENTER 6: There was a
certain amount of shielding,

00:58:38.670 --> 00:58:41.250
but I would have
to say, personally,

00:58:41.250 --> 00:58:44.610
I was trying, as
a project manager,

00:58:44.610 --> 00:58:47.940
to adopt project
management methods.

00:58:47.940 --> 00:58:50.550
And I was trying to
keep track of schedules.

00:58:50.550 --> 00:58:54.150
And I was trying to encourage
people to document things,

00:58:54.150 --> 00:58:58.780
and put documentation in
listings and so on and so on.

00:58:58.780 --> 00:59:03.419
So in that sense, I was
almost one of the enemy,

00:59:03.419 --> 00:59:04.710
if you want to put it that way.

00:59:04.710 --> 00:59:12.160
Because there are-- there were
a lot of people, and I'm sure,

00:59:12.160 --> 00:59:14.980
looking back at it, people
would still have those feelings,

00:59:14.980 --> 00:59:16.990
that this was just--

00:59:16.990 --> 00:59:19.840
all this nonsense was
just getting in the way

00:59:19.840 --> 00:59:22.060
of getting this job done.

00:59:22.060 --> 00:59:25.990
Well, NASA just didn't
view it that way.

00:59:25.990 --> 00:59:28.720
That was part of the job.

00:59:28.720 --> 00:59:33.430
You had to work to schedules,
you had to work to--

00:59:33.430 --> 00:59:36.550
you had to have reasons why
you couldn't meet a schedule.

00:59:36.550 --> 00:59:39.370
You couldn't just say, well,
I promised this in two weeks

00:59:39.370 --> 00:59:40.800
and I couldn't get it, you know?

00:59:40.800 --> 00:59:43.330
PRESENTER 7: But I think
one thing that helped,

00:59:43.330 --> 00:59:46.269
it was a matrix
management setup.

00:59:46.269 --> 00:59:47.560
PRESENTER 6: It definitely was.

00:59:47.560 --> 00:59:50.620
PRESENTER 7: And so Fred
and George, at that time,

00:59:50.620 --> 00:59:52.630
were project managers,
but then there

00:59:52.630 --> 00:59:54.880
were the functional managers.

00:59:54.880 --> 00:59:58.730
So while they were worried about
the schedules of each mission,

00:59:58.730 --> 01:00:02.080
from the functional
people worried about, hey,

01:00:02.080 --> 01:00:03.550
is this stuff working--

01:00:03.550 --> 01:00:05.860
the stuff we're supposed
to be delivering?

01:00:05.860 --> 01:00:09.460
The actual-- maybe the
man-machine interface part.

01:00:09.460 --> 01:00:11.710
This was a whole
separate division.

01:00:11.710 --> 01:00:16.240
So the functional people and
the project managers would--

01:00:16.240 --> 01:00:18.610
I thought they worked
together very well.

01:00:18.610 --> 01:00:19.720
PRESENTER 6: Well, there
was a certain tension.

01:00:19.720 --> 01:00:21.160
PRESENTER 7:
[? Didn't ?] [? we? ?]

01:00:21.160 --> 01:00:22.618
PRESENTER 6: There's
always tension

01:00:22.618 --> 01:00:24.030
in a matrix organization.

01:00:24.030 --> 01:00:24.310
PRESENTER 7: Right?

01:00:24.310 --> 01:00:25.750
PRESENTER 4: But
Fred did a great job.

01:00:25.750 --> 01:00:26.020
PRESENTER 7: Yeah.

01:00:26.020 --> 01:00:27.436
PRESENTER 4: Because
he came to us

01:00:27.436 --> 01:00:30.360
and wasn't as high-handed
as some people at NASA,

01:00:30.360 --> 01:00:33.710
and pounding on the table--
give me this or else.

01:00:33.710 --> 01:00:35.980
PRESENTER 7: No, Fred was
like a psychiatrist project.

01:00:35.980 --> 01:00:36.410
PRESENTER 6: Right.

01:00:36.410 --> 01:00:37.660
You had to be a psychiatrist--

01:00:37.660 --> 01:00:38.615
PRESENTER 4: He was--

01:00:38.615 --> 01:00:40.980
PRESENTER 7: He made
everybody work together.

01:00:40.980 --> 01:00:42.480
PRESENTER 5: Fred
was a psychiatrist

01:00:42.480 --> 01:00:44.500
drawn from our ranks,
so he understood.

01:00:44.500 --> 01:00:47.470
I always thought of you,
Fred, as Mr. Powered Flight.

01:00:47.470 --> 01:00:49.870
That probably was a fairly
short period when you--

01:00:49.870 --> 01:00:50.780
PRESENTER 1: It
was a short period.

01:00:50.780 --> 01:00:51.530
PRESENTER 7: Yeah.

01:00:51.530 --> 01:00:53.269
PRESENTER 5: But
that's what you were.

01:00:53.269 --> 01:00:55.060
And you were one of
the guys that I needed.

01:00:55.060 --> 01:00:57.518
PRESENTER 1: Was [? Ed ?] in
powered [? flight? ?] Who else

01:00:57.518 --> 01:00:58.270
was in [? NASA? ?]

01:00:58.270 --> 01:00:59.769
PRESENTER 4: I just
remembered Fred.

01:01:03.312 --> 01:01:04.270
PRESENTER 5: Actually--

01:01:04.270 --> 01:01:07.480
PRESENTER 6: You might say that,
from the background [? the ?]

01:01:07.480 --> 01:01:11.200
people, it's interesting.

01:01:11.200 --> 01:01:13.660
In those days,
we, of course, had

01:01:13.660 --> 01:01:19.090
physicists on the job, and
mathematicians, and engineering

01:01:19.090 --> 01:01:20.880
graduates and students.

01:01:20.880 --> 01:01:24.250
We probably had a literature
major here and there, too,

01:01:24.250 --> 01:01:25.940
who jumped in there.

01:01:25.940 --> 01:01:32.050
But there was no computer
science discipline, as such.

01:01:32.050 --> 01:01:35.767
There was nobody there who was
a computer scientist, per se.

01:01:35.767 --> 01:01:37.850
PRESENTER 7: Did they even
teach computer science?

01:01:37.850 --> 01:01:39.870
PRESENTER 6: And I don't
think that even taught it.

01:01:39.870 --> 01:01:40.860
PRESENTER 7: It
was not a course.

01:01:40.860 --> 01:01:41.330
PRESENTER 6: It
was not a course.

01:01:41.330 --> 01:01:42.460
PRESENTER: Right, right.

01:01:42.460 --> 01:01:45.160
PRESENTER 6: But I
often felt that part

01:01:45.160 --> 01:01:49.750
of our success at that time
was due to the engineering

01:01:49.750 --> 01:01:55.910
and physical science backgrounds
of a lot of those people.

01:01:55.910 --> 01:02:01.250
Because maybe they weren't the
most elegant programmers, OK?

01:02:01.250 --> 01:02:04.930
But they understood what a
specific impulse of an engine

01:02:04.930 --> 01:02:09.460
was, or what the physics of
what you were trying to do.

01:02:09.460 --> 01:02:13.000
And you weren't taking
somebody who was a--

01:02:13.000 --> 01:02:17.140
sort of from the inside of a
computer, and then trying to--

01:02:17.140 --> 01:02:19.240
who knew that, and
then trying to get

01:02:19.240 --> 01:02:23.620
them to understand the problem
that you were trying to solve.

01:02:23.620 --> 01:02:26.016
And I'm not saying
they couldn't do that,

01:02:26.016 --> 01:02:27.640
but I'm just saying
that, at that time,

01:02:27.640 --> 01:02:30.430
I thought that
one of the reasons

01:02:30.430 --> 01:02:33.095
that we so rapidly
were able to do this--

01:02:33.095 --> 01:02:34.720
I think there was a
great understanding

01:02:34.720 --> 01:02:37.630
of what the problem
was, even if we

01:02:37.630 --> 01:02:40.030
had to do some fast learning.

01:02:40.030 --> 01:02:43.140
But people had the basic tools.

01:02:43.140 --> 01:02:44.390
I think that was important.

01:02:44.390 --> 01:02:46.015
PRESENTER 5: Yeah,
I remember-- [? I ?]

01:02:46.015 --> 01:02:48.700
illustrated before, I remember
when you joined, Margaret.

01:02:48.700 --> 01:02:50.620
And I didn't want to
tell this against you,

01:02:50.620 --> 01:02:54.490
but one of the first things
I did in interfacing with you

01:02:54.490 --> 01:03:00.550
was explain inertial
navigation system to you.

01:03:00.550 --> 01:03:04.460
And you were a math major,
if I remember rightly.

01:03:04.460 --> 01:03:05.740
And you hadn't worked--

01:03:05.740 --> 01:03:08.950
you understood, but you hadn't
worked with coordinate systems,

01:03:08.950 --> 01:03:14.290
and I spent a couple of
hours, a couple of days,

01:03:14.290 --> 01:03:17.139
going through what the basis
of the guidance system was

01:03:17.139 --> 01:03:18.680
and how it worked
and blah-blah-blah.

01:03:18.680 --> 01:03:23.270
I never talked to her again
because you understood,

01:03:23.270 --> 01:03:25.780
and you became an inertial--

01:03:25.780 --> 01:03:28.450
at least somebody who knew
what inertial navigation

01:03:28.450 --> 01:03:30.820
system-- how you describe
them and how you control them.

01:03:33.127 --> 01:03:35.460
PRESENTER 6: I want to get
back something that Jim said,

01:03:35.460 --> 01:03:37.240
and what he did in that group.

01:03:40.450 --> 01:03:43.720
There was another fellow
who, perhaps, should be here,

01:03:43.720 --> 01:03:45.975
as well, who also
contributed to that group,

01:03:45.975 --> 01:03:51.760
[? that ?] is Bill
[? Wittennall. ?] We needed

01:03:51.760 --> 01:03:56.770
those simulations so much
because it was the only thing

01:03:56.770 --> 01:04:00.100
that grounded us to
what the truth was.

01:04:00.100 --> 01:04:02.560
I mean, you could
never tell what

01:04:02.560 --> 01:04:05.140
you were doing was going
to work until you banged it

01:04:05.140 --> 01:04:06.746
against a simulator.

01:04:06.746 --> 01:04:08.620
PRESENTER 7: Unless the
simulation was wrong.

01:04:08.620 --> 01:04:10.495
PRESENTER 6: Unless the
simulation was wrong,

01:04:10.495 --> 01:04:11.120
and I didn't--

01:04:11.120 --> 01:04:12.190
I suppose that happened.

01:04:12.190 --> 01:04:12.950
I don't remember too many--

01:04:12.950 --> 01:04:14.150
PRESENTER 4: But that's
why it was so important.

01:04:14.150 --> 01:04:16.024
PRESENTER 6: I don't
remember too many times,

01:04:16.024 --> 01:04:18.020
and I was really
impressed when somebody

01:04:18.020 --> 01:04:24.230
had five degrees of freedom
in the fuel [? slashing ?]

01:04:24.230 --> 01:04:28.220
module of the vehicle.

01:04:28.220 --> 01:04:32.120
And they had all the stars in
the right places, and all the--

01:04:32.120 --> 01:04:34.280
everything else
that would happen.

01:04:34.280 --> 01:04:36.210
And it was really--

01:04:36.210 --> 01:04:40.260
it became our truth.

01:04:40.260 --> 01:04:42.260
And I was thinking
about this last night

01:04:42.260 --> 01:04:45.731
before I came here, also.

01:04:45.731 --> 01:04:50.580
This was, basically,
a fun project,

01:04:50.580 --> 01:04:52.730
although there were
plenty of difficult times

01:04:52.730 --> 01:04:55.400
in the project when you're
in the middle of it.

01:04:55.400 --> 01:04:59.330
But I was-- we did a lot
of testing in this project,

01:04:59.330 --> 01:05:03.640
a lot of testing with
these simulators.

01:05:03.640 --> 01:05:05.750
And I think that
if all we ever did

01:05:05.750 --> 01:05:10.560
was fly the nominal
mission with nothing wrong,

01:05:10.560 --> 01:05:12.765
it would've been pretty
boring after a while,

01:05:12.765 --> 01:05:14.930
if you had this just
one set of parameters,

01:05:14.930 --> 01:05:16.950
and you always fly this
one set of parameters.

01:05:16.950 --> 01:05:19.220
And I think that part
of the fun that we

01:05:19.220 --> 01:05:25.400
had was thinking up and creating
these crazy off-nominal cases

01:05:25.400 --> 01:05:29.520
that probably could
never, never happen.

01:05:29.520 --> 01:05:33.470
And then see-- and then bang
that against the software that

01:05:33.470 --> 01:05:37.790
we designed, and design certain
margins into the software that

01:05:37.790 --> 01:05:41.620
would make sure that if you--

01:05:41.620 --> 01:05:43.370
which way, or what
way the [? gimbal ?]

01:05:43.370 --> 01:05:45.920
would go that you'd be
able to get out of it.

01:05:45.920 --> 01:05:55.730
And other things that made
such variety in the testing.

01:05:55.730 --> 01:06:00.430
That for one nominal case,
we ran 100 off-nominal cases.

01:06:03.070 --> 01:06:07.310
PRESENTER 7: Also, an advantage,
from a computer science

01:06:07.310 --> 01:06:09.940
or software engineering
point of view,

01:06:09.940 --> 01:06:12.830
of having different missions
is that we learned all

01:06:12.830 --> 01:06:15.920
about the advantages of things
that people don't think too

01:06:15.920 --> 01:06:19.040
much about today,
like reuse and how

01:06:19.040 --> 01:06:20.540
that could cut down on testing.

01:06:23.090 --> 01:06:25.040
We learned all the
things about what

01:06:25.040 --> 01:06:27.830
it means to manage these
different missions, what's

01:06:27.830 --> 01:06:31.040
reused between the LEM
and the command module,

01:06:31.040 --> 01:06:33.710
between this mission and
the one that's going next.

01:06:33.710 --> 01:06:34.760
How do you evolve?

01:06:34.760 --> 01:06:36.820
How do you do it
in a reliable way?

01:06:36.820 --> 01:06:40.130
In other words, it
was a great place

01:06:40.130 --> 01:06:44.300
to learn about how to
do things the right way.

01:06:44.300 --> 01:06:46.169
So that was--

01:06:46.169 --> 01:06:48.460
PRESENTER 6: The whole thing
had to be structured, too.

01:06:48.460 --> 01:06:51.010
I mean, all that
software had to be--

01:06:51.010 --> 01:06:55.090
we structured it into
programs with numbers.

01:06:55.090 --> 01:06:56.230
We'd call them things.

01:06:56.230 --> 01:06:58.060
We had names for them.

01:06:58.060 --> 01:07:01.430
The astronauts eventually picked
up some of those same names.

01:07:01.430 --> 01:07:03.100
They would get into P40.

01:07:03.100 --> 01:07:05.145
I mean, they didn't
know what P40 was.

01:07:05.145 --> 01:07:09.040
These things were-- not that
those names were so great,

01:07:09.040 --> 01:07:12.220
but the fact of the matter
is that that software all had

01:07:12.220 --> 01:07:14.950
to be structured and organized.

01:07:14.950 --> 01:07:17.140
And there had to be
a nomenclature on it,

01:07:17.140 --> 01:07:19.207
and there had to be
interfaces built.

01:07:19.207 --> 01:07:21.040
PRESENTER 7: It took
on a life of their own.

01:07:21.040 --> 01:07:22.210
PRESENTER 6: It took
on a life of its own.

01:07:22.210 --> 01:07:24.280
PRESENTER 4: --banks
manually here, there.

01:07:24.280 --> 01:07:25.410
PRESENTER 6: Right.

01:07:25.410 --> 01:07:28.060
PRESENTER 3: And it had to
be ready about-- what was it?

01:07:28.060 --> 01:07:29.404
8, 10 weeks before flight time.

01:07:29.404 --> 01:07:30.820
You couldn't change
it after that.

01:07:30.820 --> 01:07:31.750
PRESENTER: At least.

01:07:31.750 --> 01:07:33.125
PRESENTER 7: And
also, we learned

01:07:33.125 --> 01:07:35.640
about how important
it was to prioritize

01:07:35.640 --> 01:07:37.750
as to what was most important.

01:07:37.750 --> 01:07:42.820
Because there was so little room
to put the programs into that

01:07:42.820 --> 01:07:49.140
you would learn to delete
what was less important.

01:07:49.140 --> 01:07:51.220
But prioritizing
became something,

01:07:51.220 --> 01:07:54.100
and backup systems
also became something

01:07:54.100 --> 01:07:58.960
that you lived your life
later by doing things

01:07:58.960 --> 01:08:00.850
like prioritizing,
and doing things

01:08:00.850 --> 01:08:03.520
like having backup systems.

01:08:03.520 --> 01:08:05.260
[? And ?] in your daily life--

01:08:05.260 --> 01:08:07.840
and even going to the
grocery store later on,

01:08:07.840 --> 01:08:09.080
it was such an influence.

01:08:09.080 --> 01:08:11.500
But we had a Black Friday--

01:08:11.500 --> 01:08:13.240
I can't remember what
Black Friday was.

01:08:13.240 --> 01:08:15.592
I just remember we were
deleting all kinds of things

01:08:15.592 --> 01:08:17.550
because something more
important was coming in.

01:08:17.550 --> 01:08:18.050
What was it?

01:08:18.050 --> 01:08:21.159
PRESENTER 4: [INAUDIBLE]
sessions where they cut--

01:08:21.159 --> 01:08:22.750
PRESENTER 7: Yeah.

01:08:22.750 --> 01:08:26.364
PRESENTER 6: [INAUDIBLE] I think
they called it Black Friday.

01:08:26.364 --> 01:08:27.114
PRESENTER 7: Yeah.

01:08:27.114 --> 01:08:29.529
But we deleted an awful lot
because something really

01:08:29.529 --> 01:08:30.609
important had to be done.

01:08:30.609 --> 01:08:32.380
Maybe it was backups or--

01:08:32.380 --> 01:08:34.870
I don't remember now.

01:08:34.870 --> 01:08:37.600
I wrote about it, but I
can't remember what it--

01:08:37.600 --> 01:08:39.443
I'll have to go back
and find that out.

01:08:39.443 --> 01:08:41.109
PRESENTER 3: I'm sorry
to have to leave,

01:08:41.109 --> 01:08:42.863
but thank you very much.

01:08:42.863 --> 01:08:44.529
PRESENTER 4: Ray,
nice to see you again.

01:08:44.529 --> 01:08:45.862
PRESENTER: Good to see you, Ray.

01:08:45.862 --> 01:08:48.350
PRESENTER 6: Ray,
were you the guy who--

01:08:48.350 --> 01:08:49.389
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

01:08:49.389 --> 01:08:50.930
PRESENTER 6: Ray,
were you the person

01:08:50.930 --> 01:08:54.689
who inflicted banks on us
so we had to worry about--

01:08:54.689 --> 01:08:57.399
PRESENTER 3: That was a
solution to a very bad problem.

01:08:57.399 --> 01:09:00.220
PRESENTER 6: It was a very bad
solution to a very bad problem.

01:09:00.220 --> 01:09:01.479
[LAUGHTER]

01:09:01.479 --> 01:09:04.899
PRESENTER 3: Well, that solution
exists today in Windows, right?

01:09:04.899 --> 01:09:09.850
Or in the 8086 architecture.

01:09:09.850 --> 01:09:11.710
Because you still
have to do banking

01:09:11.710 --> 01:09:13.270
in order to get the
extended address

01:09:13.270 --> 01:09:14.300
fields and things like that.

01:09:14.300 --> 01:09:14.410
PRESENTER: Right.

01:09:14.410 --> 01:09:15.819
PRESENTER 2: It's
just invisible.

01:09:15.819 --> 01:09:17.130
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

01:09:17.130 --> 01:09:19.758
PRESENTER 6: It drove us
bananas, I'll tell you that.

01:09:22.569 --> 01:09:24.370
It was almost like
area codes, I would

01:09:24.370 --> 01:09:27.670
say, in the telephone system.

01:09:27.670 --> 01:09:28.550
I mean, you had to--

01:09:28.550 --> 01:09:29.080
PRESENTER 7: Oh, yeah.

01:09:29.080 --> 01:09:31.455
PRESENTER 6: --you had to be
able to address [? this-- ?]

01:09:31.455 --> 01:09:34.840
with the [? addressing-- ?]
how many bits you had

01:09:34.840 --> 01:09:36.020
for addressing.

01:09:36.020 --> 01:09:38.870
And in order to get
unique addressing,

01:09:38.870 --> 01:09:42.760
you had to mark it with
another set of field--

01:09:42.760 --> 01:09:46.319
another field that would
let you address uniquely.

01:09:46.319 --> 01:09:48.370
And so you had to
manage that other field,

01:09:48.370 --> 01:09:51.890
and that got very
confusing in the software.

01:09:51.890 --> 01:09:54.010
That was a difficult issue.

01:09:54.010 --> 01:09:56.890
PRESENTER 4: And the
erasable memory was so small.

01:09:56.890 --> 01:09:57.571
What was it?

01:09:57.571 --> 01:09:58.070
2K?

01:09:58.070 --> 01:09:59.200
PRESENTER: It was about 2K.

01:09:59.200 --> 01:10:01.199
PRESENTER 4: That certain
of that was permanent,

01:10:01.199 --> 01:10:03.780
but a lot of it
used during burn.

01:10:03.780 --> 01:10:06.560
And when you were done
burns, that same area

01:10:06.560 --> 01:10:08.860
was used for something
in a later phase.

01:10:08.860 --> 01:10:11.590
And so these phases
were sequential.

01:10:11.590 --> 01:10:17.120
And all the previous information
was gone, and not used anymore.

01:10:17.120 --> 01:10:18.860
So you didn't have unique--

01:10:18.860 --> 01:10:20.401
PRESENTER 7: You
could see there were

01:10:20.401 --> 01:10:22.539
a lot of things to do with
interfacing correctly.

01:10:22.539 --> 01:10:24.080
And therefore, we
had the opportunity

01:10:24.080 --> 01:10:26.590
to make a lot of
interface errors

01:10:26.590 --> 01:10:28.360
because we had to
squeeze everything

01:10:28.360 --> 01:10:30.160
into such a small space.

01:10:30.160 --> 01:10:36.310
But also, it's amazing what one
could do in so little space.

01:10:36.310 --> 01:10:40.260
And how little one can do
with so much space today.

01:10:40.260 --> 01:10:42.080
[LAUGHTER]

01:10:42.080 --> 01:10:45.530
PRESENTER 5: One of the
things that I have remarked on

01:10:45.530 --> 01:10:50.260
to myself, over 30 years
since then, is at the time,

01:10:50.260 --> 01:10:52.150
we brought software
into a machine

01:10:52.150 --> 01:10:54.430
that, by today's
standards, would be

01:10:54.430 --> 01:10:56.860
a very advanced architecture.

01:10:56.860 --> 01:10:58.720
I mean, we had-- you
heard it all the time--

01:10:58.720 --> 01:11:02.200
preemptive,
event-driven executives.

01:11:02.200 --> 01:11:04.690
I mean, I've worked with
people who say, what?

01:11:04.690 --> 01:11:06.440
You programmed
into one of those?

01:11:06.440 --> 01:11:11.110
A real-time control computer
with event-driven asynchronous?

01:11:11.110 --> 01:11:13.097
We don't have that today.

01:11:13.097 --> 01:11:15.180
PRESENTER 4: Yeah, with
auto pilots and [? jets ?]

01:11:15.180 --> 01:11:16.094
sending signals.

01:11:16.094 --> 01:11:18.385
PRESENTER 5: Multiple levels
of interrupts and control.

01:11:18.385 --> 01:11:19.900
PRESENTER 7: Man-machine
interface, all of it.

01:11:19.900 --> 01:11:21.670
PRESENTER 5: We invented
it for this job,

01:11:21.670 --> 01:11:26.970
and it hasn't reemerged as a
standard of computer design

01:11:26.970 --> 01:11:28.307
today.

01:11:28.307 --> 01:11:29.890
Surely, there are
interrupts and stuff

01:11:29.890 --> 01:11:32.770
like-- but they were not used
in this coordinated fashion

01:11:32.770 --> 01:11:34.060
that we had then.

01:11:34.060 --> 01:11:36.460
Something else
that we invented--

01:11:36.460 --> 01:11:40.870
and it's saved those guys
[INAUDIBLE] on the moon--

01:11:40.870 --> 01:11:49.720
was the software restart, which
allowed reaction to any kind

01:11:49.720 --> 01:11:56.650
of failure for the computer to
retrack back to its last known,

01:11:56.650 --> 01:12:01.612
properly coordinated state,
and to [? re-move ?] forward.

01:12:01.612 --> 01:12:03.820
PRESENTER 4: You don't have
to do Control-Alt-Delete.

01:12:03.820 --> 01:12:04.486
PRESENTER 5: No.

01:12:04.486 --> 01:12:07.754
This thing sets-- what
do we call those things?

01:12:07.754 --> 01:12:08.920
PRESENTER 4: Restart points.

01:12:08.920 --> 01:12:10.797
PRESENTER 5: Restart
points as it went along.

01:12:10.797 --> 01:12:12.630
PRESENTER 7: And Dan
was the father of that.

01:12:12.630 --> 01:12:14.754
PRESENTER 5: I was going
to say, this guy and Woody

01:12:14.754 --> 01:12:16.590
[? Vandiver-- ?] I was--

01:12:16.590 --> 01:12:17.410
Dan's the father.

01:12:17.410 --> 01:12:19.460
Woody was the guy
that put together--

01:12:19.460 --> 01:12:21.880
and I advised that
Woody should be.

01:12:21.880 --> 01:12:28.780
[INAUDIBLE] But not only did
the software restart concept,

01:12:28.780 --> 01:12:32.470
I think, rescue the
LEM from failure

01:12:32.470 --> 01:12:35.000
just as the [INAUDIBLE]
[? touched ?] down,

01:12:35.000 --> 01:12:37.750
they were also a terrific
help in debugging

01:12:37.750 --> 01:12:39.800
this complex piece of software.

01:12:39.800 --> 01:12:40.976
We used them.

01:12:40.976 --> 01:12:41.850
Do you remember that?

01:12:41.850 --> 01:12:42.310
PRESENTER 7: Yeah.

01:12:42.310 --> 01:12:43.768
PRESENTER 5: We
triggered restarts,

01:12:43.768 --> 01:12:48.130
because if this thing was able
to go over the same piece,

01:12:48.130 --> 01:12:51.220
we could change things
and rerun chunks

01:12:51.220 --> 01:12:54.880
using the characteristics
of the software itself.

01:12:54.880 --> 01:12:58.020
But I think Margaret probably
remembers more of that

01:12:58.020 --> 01:12:58.520
than I do.

01:12:58.520 --> 01:13:00.430
But I remember, if
we did not have that,

01:13:00.430 --> 01:13:03.160
testing of this real-time stuff
would have been a hell of a lot

01:13:03.160 --> 01:13:04.159
more difficult.

01:13:04.159 --> 01:13:05.950
PRESENTER 7: Well, you
brought up something

01:13:05.950 --> 01:13:07.240
that was really interesting.

01:13:07.240 --> 01:13:10.960
Because people today,
when they use computers,

01:13:10.960 --> 01:13:14.530
they try something, it doesn't
work, they try something again,

01:13:14.530 --> 01:13:16.090
it doesn't work,
and they just have

01:13:16.090 --> 01:13:17.440
the computer at their disposal.

01:13:17.440 --> 01:13:21.460
We could only put
in runs overnight.

01:13:21.460 --> 01:13:24.100
So you'd have to
think very carefully.

01:13:24.100 --> 01:13:27.220
Because if it didn't
work, then you'd

01:13:27.220 --> 01:13:28.800
have to wait till the next day.

01:13:28.800 --> 01:13:30.050
PRESENTER 5: You wasted a day.

01:13:30.050 --> 01:13:31.420
PRESENTER 7: A whole day.

01:13:31.420 --> 01:13:33.400
So then what we
learned to do was

01:13:33.400 --> 01:13:37.090
to find a way to run 100 runs
at once, thinking about it

01:13:37.090 --> 01:13:40.270
before the fact, and the
restarts remind me of that.

01:13:40.270 --> 01:13:43.480
So you'd try all these
different things all at once.

01:13:43.480 --> 01:13:45.670
And in a way, you got
more done because you had

01:13:45.670 --> 01:13:47.790
to think it out so carefully.

01:13:47.790 --> 01:13:51.296
You got done maybe more
in that 24-hour period

01:13:51.296 --> 01:13:52.670
than if you were
sitting here, ad

01:13:52.670 --> 01:13:55.670
[? hocly, ?] just
trying something.

01:13:55.670 --> 01:13:59.570
And so you're basically doing
things more in parallel,

01:13:59.570 --> 01:14:01.750
I think, in your
thinking process

01:14:01.750 --> 01:14:04.066
and your design of the test.

01:14:04.066 --> 01:14:05.440
So that was a
totally different--

01:14:05.440 --> 01:14:05.650
PRESENTER 5: Yeah.

01:14:05.650 --> 01:14:07.774
I don't know what lessons
can be learned from that,

01:14:07.774 --> 01:14:10.169
but we did this
with cards offline.

01:14:10.169 --> 01:14:11.210
PRESENTER 7: Yeah, cards.

01:14:11.210 --> 01:14:12.040
PRESENTER: Punch cards.

01:14:12.040 --> 01:14:13.623
PRESENTER 5: There
were no such things

01:14:13.623 --> 01:14:16.360
as terminals or interaction.

01:14:16.360 --> 01:14:20.320
As Margaret said, you got
your stack of 2,000 cards.

01:14:20.320 --> 01:14:23.260
You gave it to the guys in the
computer room, who by then, had

01:14:23.260 --> 01:14:24.790
cordoned themselves off.

01:14:24.790 --> 01:14:27.040
You couldn't-- we used to
go in there and run the runs

01:14:27.040 --> 01:14:28.330
ourselves, but that--

01:14:28.330 --> 01:14:31.159
at some stage, when Jim's
double Honeywell came in,

01:14:31.159 --> 01:14:32.200
that was the end of that.

01:14:32.200 --> 01:14:33.970
PRESENTER 7: And the thing
is, if you dropped the cards,

01:14:33.970 --> 01:14:34.928
you had a real problem.

01:14:34.928 --> 01:14:36.580
[LAUGHTER]

01:14:36.580 --> 01:14:38.062
But it made you realize--

01:14:38.062 --> 01:14:39.520
I mean, that was
part of the reason

01:14:39.520 --> 01:14:41.410
we worked on doing
something where,

01:14:41.410 --> 01:14:43.510
if you drop the
instruction sets,

01:14:43.510 --> 01:14:45.520
it wouldn't matter because
it was smart enough

01:14:45.520 --> 01:14:47.200
to get it back together again.

01:14:47.200 --> 01:14:48.760
That was later.

01:14:48.760 --> 01:14:51.210
But we used to take
pieces like cards

01:14:51.210 --> 01:14:53.290
and say, here, put
this in your deck,

01:14:53.290 --> 01:14:55.210
and that would be the reusable.

01:14:55.210 --> 01:14:56.080
You remember that?

01:14:56.080 --> 01:14:56.320
PRESENTER 5: Yeah.

01:14:56.320 --> 01:14:58.330
PRESENTER 4: We had
to learn to use 519's.

01:14:58.330 --> 01:15:01.370
PRESENTER 5: These are like
horror stories, or war stories.

01:15:01.370 --> 01:15:05.710
There's probably something to
be learned from this experience

01:15:05.710 --> 01:15:09.310
that the few, under
arduous circumstances,

01:15:09.310 --> 01:15:12.070
were able to turn out
something as complex

01:15:12.070 --> 01:15:14.980
as this piece of software was,
and have it work-- and prove

01:15:14.980 --> 01:15:16.930
that it worked.

01:15:16.930 --> 01:15:18.857
That seems to be missing today.

01:15:18.857 --> 01:15:20.440
I don't know how to
put that together,

01:15:20.440 --> 01:15:24.820
but the fewer people, and the
more crude the development

01:15:24.820 --> 01:15:26.620
environment was,
the better seemed

01:15:26.620 --> 01:15:28.960
to have been the progress
and the end product.

01:15:28.960 --> 01:15:30.320
There's a conundrum there.

01:15:30.320 --> 01:15:30.850
I'm not sure what it is.

01:15:30.850 --> 01:15:32.933
PRESENTER 6: We did something
that I know Margaret

01:15:32.933 --> 01:15:35.210
was intimately involved with.

01:15:35.210 --> 01:15:37.320
And that is that--
and I think it's

01:15:37.320 --> 01:15:43.690
written up in Microsoft's method
of doing their software today.

01:15:43.690 --> 01:15:51.010
But we had a nightly
configuration control

01:15:51.010 --> 01:15:56.620
group that looked
at all changes that

01:15:56.620 --> 01:15:59.560
were created by
anybody during the day,

01:15:59.560 --> 01:16:02.440
and had to pass this little
group of people, which

01:16:02.440 --> 01:16:05.380
might have been four people.

01:16:05.380 --> 01:16:06.580
And we had the--

01:16:06.580 --> 01:16:08.080
PRESENTER 7: The
[? rope ?] mothers.

01:16:08.080 --> 01:16:09.913
PRESENTER 6: We had
this, quote, "advantage"

01:16:09.913 --> 01:16:15.160
of doing an assembly
every night so

01:16:15.160 --> 01:16:19.060
that these changes were
brought in to one listing,

01:16:19.060 --> 01:16:21.070
you might say.

01:16:21.070 --> 01:16:26.290
And then, by the next day, you'd
have an update of that program.

01:16:26.290 --> 01:16:27.970
And so you had a very--

01:16:27.970 --> 01:16:33.880
an extremely tight
configuration control system.

01:16:33.880 --> 01:16:37.920
Today, if everybody is
programming on monitors,

01:16:37.920 --> 01:16:42.730
and they're all
distributed and so on.

01:16:42.730 --> 01:16:47.020
This is a much more
difficult environment

01:16:47.020 --> 01:16:49.600
than we were in
this one building,

01:16:49.600 --> 01:16:53.040
everybody producing
changes and cards,

01:16:53.040 --> 01:16:56.310
and write-ups of what
their changes were, all

01:16:56.310 --> 01:16:59.460
being funneled to one
small configuration control

01:16:59.460 --> 01:17:01.950
group, who would then
pass on everything

01:17:01.950 --> 01:17:04.170
that went into that system.

01:17:04.170 --> 01:17:06.720
And that was done continuously.

01:17:06.720 --> 01:17:08.730
I don't know if it
started right at day one,

01:17:08.730 --> 01:17:11.520
but it was done on the
flights that I remember.

01:17:11.520 --> 01:17:15.420
And it was a very effective
way of keeping errors out

01:17:15.420 --> 01:17:16.530
of the program.

01:17:16.530 --> 01:17:18.270
PRESENTER 7: But
there was more to add

01:17:18.270 --> 01:17:22.050
to that, in that every single
day, the person who reviewed

01:17:22.050 --> 01:17:27.690
the changes would put out a memo
that would go to everyone that

01:17:27.690 --> 01:17:30.750
was involved in that
mission, so that they were

01:17:30.750 --> 01:17:32.820
aware of what the changes were.

01:17:32.820 --> 01:17:34.920
So then, if they
had problems, they'd

01:17:34.920 --> 01:17:38.100
know that a change had
been made in that area.

01:17:38.100 --> 01:17:40.370
So it was that communication--

01:17:40.370 --> 01:17:43.880
just hard-copy
memos that went out

01:17:43.880 --> 01:17:46.824
alongside each daily
revision that I think helped.

01:17:46.824 --> 01:17:48.990
PRESENTER 1: It shouldn't
be forgotten that this was

01:17:48.990 --> 01:17:50.720
an assembly language program.

01:17:50.720 --> 01:17:51.650
PRESENTER 6: Yes.

01:17:51.650 --> 01:17:51.960
PRESENTER 7: Yeah.

01:17:51.960 --> 01:17:53.670
PRESENTER 1: And if
somebody had set up

01:17:53.670 --> 01:17:56.490
a simulation with what we call
special requests, addressed

01:17:56.490 --> 01:17:58.950
to certain locations,
and somebody changed

01:17:58.950 --> 01:18:01.320
the underlying
stuff, the locations

01:18:01.320 --> 01:18:02.820
could move in that
simulation run,

01:18:02.820 --> 01:18:05.550
or just be incorrectly
formulated,

01:18:05.550 --> 01:18:06.660
as it would turn out.

01:18:06.660 --> 01:18:08.190
But that would take a day.

01:18:08.190 --> 01:18:11.400
And so you had to be
careful not to kill somebody

01:18:11.400 --> 01:18:13.849
by shooting down
something somebody else

01:18:13.849 --> 01:18:15.765
was planning to do with
a fixed configuration.

01:18:15.765 --> 01:18:16.500
PRESENTER 7: Exactly.

01:18:16.500 --> 01:18:18.791
PRESENTER 1: Which was never
fixed because somebody had

01:18:18.791 --> 01:18:21.180
to change something every day.

01:18:21.180 --> 01:18:23.130
Towards the end
of the development

01:18:23.130 --> 01:18:26.040
of a single mission program--

01:18:26.040 --> 01:18:28.200
this is a fascinating thing.

01:18:28.200 --> 01:18:31.050
It's been mentioned that NASA
let us do just about anything

01:18:31.050 --> 01:18:32.610
we wanted to in the code.

01:18:32.610 --> 01:18:36.120
But we did have some help from
a famous guy [? at ?] [? TRW. ?]

01:18:36.120 --> 01:18:41.550
But basically, we were
completely given free reign

01:18:41.550 --> 01:18:45.670
until we delivered the software
to Raytheon to build the core

01:18:45.670 --> 01:18:46.410
[? rope. ?]

01:18:46.410 --> 01:18:48.930
And then we would go
into a series of tests

01:18:48.930 --> 01:18:51.390
with absolutely
fixed configuration,

01:18:51.390 --> 01:18:53.160
the purpose being
to find any more

01:18:53.160 --> 01:18:55.050
bugs that hadn't been found.

01:18:55.050 --> 01:18:57.240
And sometimes, when you found--

01:18:57.240 --> 01:18:59.130
sometimes you would
find bugs that just

01:18:59.130 --> 01:19:00.630
you felt had to be fixed.

01:19:00.630 --> 01:19:03.060
And usually, they could be.

01:19:03.060 --> 01:19:06.240
But once the program
was delivered,

01:19:06.240 --> 01:19:08.610
this group of people,
who were the only people

01:19:08.610 --> 01:19:11.100
in the world who NASA thought
could write the software,

01:19:11.100 --> 01:19:14.760
suddenly were so stupid
that no change they proposed

01:19:14.760 --> 01:19:16.740
could be plausible at all.

01:19:16.740 --> 01:19:20.200
And so anything you
suggested, the answer was no.

01:19:20.200 --> 01:19:22.740
And you had to beg and so
forth, for the interest

01:19:22.740 --> 01:19:25.620
of the project, to
be allowed to remove

01:19:25.620 --> 01:19:28.440
this desperately bad thing.

01:19:28.440 --> 01:19:32.340
And I remember one night,
in the LEM 1 software game,

01:19:32.340 --> 01:19:34.170
when one of these showed up.

01:19:34.170 --> 01:19:36.780
Probably the digital
autopilot, which was new.

01:19:36.780 --> 01:19:39.480
And it was late at night.

01:19:39.480 --> 01:19:42.030
It must have been 9:00
or 10:00 at night.

01:19:42.030 --> 01:19:43.620
And I called the
guy, and I'm not

01:19:43.620 --> 01:19:47.700
going to mention any names
for reasons that you'll see.

01:19:47.700 --> 01:19:50.420
I called the guy who was
monitoring us at NASA at home

01:19:50.420 --> 01:19:52.770
because I had to
get his permission

01:19:52.770 --> 01:19:56.400
to put this change in, and to
let him know it was coming.

01:19:56.400 --> 01:20:00.090
And his wife answered the
phone and said he was at work.

01:20:03.280 --> 01:20:05.880
So I called him at his office.

01:20:05.880 --> 01:20:07.200
Nobody there.

01:20:07.200 --> 01:20:11.880
Well, I knew that this
guy wasn't always telling

01:20:11.880 --> 01:20:15.630
his wife what he was up
to, to say the least.

01:20:15.630 --> 01:20:18.870
And here I was
with this problem.

01:20:18.870 --> 01:20:20.010
It was 10 o'clock at night.

01:20:20.010 --> 01:20:21.480
We wanted to put this change in.

01:20:21.480 --> 01:20:23.910
It was, in my mind--
whatever it was--

01:20:23.910 --> 01:20:26.220
really important to
put this change in.

01:20:26.220 --> 01:20:30.422
I knew the guy wasn't where
his wife thought he was,

01:20:30.422 --> 01:20:31.630
and I didn't know what to do.

01:20:31.630 --> 01:20:33.046
I couldn't leave
a message at work

01:20:33.046 --> 01:20:34.825
because they didn't
have voicemail then.

01:20:34.825 --> 01:20:36.450
And I didn't want to
call his wife back

01:20:36.450 --> 01:20:38.491
and say he wasn't at work,
for fear of what would

01:20:38.491 --> 01:20:40.420
happen to him when he got home.

01:20:40.420 --> 01:20:42.660
And what do you do
in a case like that?

01:20:42.660 --> 01:20:46.290
We were absolutely at the
mercy of getting approval

01:20:46.290 --> 01:20:47.700
on those things.

01:20:47.700 --> 01:20:49.920
I don't remember what happened.

01:20:49.920 --> 01:20:51.744
PRESENTER 7: He was
in the bathroom.

01:20:51.744 --> 01:20:54.210
PRESENTER 1: I doubt it.

01:20:54.210 --> 01:20:57.510
But there were some funny
situations like that.

01:20:57.510 --> 01:21:00.750
Many times, the
software was changed

01:21:00.750 --> 01:21:02.930
after the rope had
actually been built,

01:21:02.930 --> 01:21:05.010
and they would sometimes
have already potted it,

01:21:05.010 --> 01:21:08.370
and they'd have to depot it, and
change the wires and so forth.

01:21:08.370 --> 01:21:14.760
It was an incredibly small and
arduous process to go through.

01:21:14.760 --> 01:21:18.390
One of the things that I think
deserves mention to illustrate

01:21:18.390 --> 01:21:22.800
the frugality that went into
the design and the software was

01:21:22.800 --> 01:21:27.540
that the guys that invented
these interpreters--

01:21:27.540 --> 01:21:30.750
and we had a second generation
of them later than the one that

01:21:30.750 --> 01:21:33.480
Hal Laning had developed--

01:21:33.480 --> 01:21:35.700
realized that
single precision was

01:21:35.700 --> 01:21:39.720
too small to do
Earth-to-moon navigation,

01:21:39.720 --> 01:21:42.700
but double precision
was adequate.

01:21:42.700 --> 01:21:46.930
And so the interpreter would do
everything in double precision,

01:21:46.930 --> 01:21:49.560
or lots of things
in double precision.

01:21:49.560 --> 01:21:51.400
It was a ones'
complement machine, which

01:21:51.400 --> 01:21:52.710
is funny enough by itself.

01:21:52.710 --> 01:22:01.510
So that there were 32,767
states, not 32,768 states.

01:22:01.510 --> 01:22:04.140
And unfortunately, there
were some optical encoders

01:22:04.140 --> 01:22:08.970
on rotating gadgets that needed
2 to the 15th exactly, and not

01:22:08.970 --> 01:22:11.130
2 to the 15th minus 1.

01:22:11.130 --> 01:22:18.190
And so it also was
capable of doing

01:22:18.190 --> 01:22:20.080
[? two's ?]
[? complement ?] arithmetic,

01:22:20.080 --> 01:22:21.480
which caused some
other problems.

01:22:21.480 --> 01:22:23.760
But here we had a ones'
complement machine

01:22:23.760 --> 01:22:25.920
with two forms of 0.

01:22:25.920 --> 01:22:28.350
And double precision,
in which the interpreter

01:22:28.350 --> 01:22:31.080
was perfectly happy to let the
two parts of the [? word ?]

01:22:31.080 --> 01:22:31.960
have different signs.

01:22:31.960 --> 01:22:36.720
So you could have a plus 1 over
here and a minus 1/2 over here,

01:22:36.720 --> 01:22:39.600
and the result would
be whatever it is.

01:22:39.600 --> 01:22:40.830
Plus 1/2.

01:22:40.830 --> 01:22:43.620
And that was clever
because the software

01:22:43.620 --> 01:22:45.600
to do sign correction
all the time

01:22:45.600 --> 01:22:49.210
took time, took space,
and was eliminated.

01:22:49.210 --> 01:22:50.640
Unfortunately,
however, there were

01:22:50.640 --> 01:22:52.830
a lot of cases where
that just turned out

01:22:52.830 --> 01:22:55.530
to be a real pain
in the backside.

01:22:55.530 --> 01:22:58.050
And there were lots
and lots of little bugs

01:22:58.050 --> 01:23:01.695
that showed up when there was
sign disagreement in there,

01:23:01.695 --> 01:23:06.090
in a way that somehow got
through all the checks.

01:23:06.090 --> 01:23:09.660
And so there were things that
we did to ourselves sometimes,

01:23:09.660 --> 01:23:13.530
in the interest of frugality,
or [? needed ?] to.

01:23:16.120 --> 01:23:18.030
It was a different day.

01:23:18.030 --> 01:23:20.350
It was a different day.

01:23:20.350 --> 01:23:22.770
Dan, of course, solved the
assembly language problem.

01:23:22.770 --> 01:23:24.540
He solved lots of problems.

01:23:24.540 --> 01:23:25.980
Wrote an assembler.

01:23:25.980 --> 01:23:28.890
We had an assembler on
the Honeywell machine,

01:23:28.890 --> 01:23:30.310
which was a batch assembly.

01:23:30.310 --> 01:23:32.167
You would take all of
the assembly programs

01:23:32.167 --> 01:23:33.750
that you were going
to update, and you

01:23:33.750 --> 01:23:35.640
would assemble this
huge deck of cards

01:23:35.640 --> 01:23:37.540
that would affect all of them.

01:23:37.540 --> 01:23:39.540
And one of the characteristics
of this assembler

01:23:39.540 --> 01:23:42.090
was it was designed
for the tape machine,

01:23:42.090 --> 01:23:45.240
so it had tapes up and
down, and so forth.

01:23:45.240 --> 01:23:47.460
If your cards were
in the wrong place,

01:23:47.460 --> 01:23:49.500
or had the wrong
number on them, they

01:23:49.500 --> 01:23:51.770
wouldn't go where
you put them in,

01:23:51.770 --> 01:23:54.510
and they wouldn't necessarily
even go into the program

01:23:54.510 --> 01:23:56.190
that you had them aimed at.

01:23:56.190 --> 01:23:59.850
And so you had to meticulously
check your assembly listing

01:23:59.850 --> 01:24:03.420
in the morning when you got up
to see if all your changes went

01:24:03.420 --> 01:24:04.010
in there.

01:24:04.010 --> 01:24:05.843
And if they weren't
where you expected them,

01:24:05.843 --> 01:24:08.250
you had to go try to figure
out, where did they go?

01:24:08.250 --> 01:24:13.920
And it was an arduous
process, and a careless person

01:24:13.920 --> 01:24:18.180
could wreak havoc on the
world in those assemblies.

01:24:18.180 --> 01:24:20.390
And they would run
for a long time.

01:24:20.390 --> 01:24:26.020
The saving grace of the Apollo
computer was that it was slow.

01:24:26.020 --> 01:24:28.650
And why I say that was that
the simulations that we

01:24:28.650 --> 01:24:31.750
did, and the assemblies we
had to do, were very long.

01:24:31.750 --> 01:24:33.960
And if the machine had
been 10 times as fast,

01:24:33.960 --> 01:24:36.600
it would have taken 10 times
longer on the best hardware

01:24:36.600 --> 01:24:39.300
that we could buy to
simulate this stuff.

01:24:39.300 --> 01:24:42.390
And we would never have been
able to run these simulations.

01:24:42.390 --> 01:24:45.380
They would have
just taken more time

01:24:45.380 --> 01:24:48.250
than we could've possibly had.

01:24:48.250 --> 01:24:52.720
So sometimes, the
limited resources

01:24:52.720 --> 01:24:54.270
are a blessing in disguise.

01:24:56.725 --> 01:24:58.350
PRESENTER 4: That's
why those emulators

01:24:58.350 --> 01:25:01.770
people have on today's
computers have so much trouble.

01:25:01.770 --> 01:25:04.860
They're trying to emulate
something that's so fast.

01:25:04.860 --> 01:25:06.090
PRESENTER 1: Exactly.

01:25:06.090 --> 01:25:09.750
We had things in the simulation
called clock advance, which

01:25:09.750 --> 01:25:12.420
you'd tell a simulator you
were willing-- when nothing was

01:25:12.420 --> 01:25:15.300
happening-- to just jump
the clock up to the point

01:25:15.300 --> 01:25:16.590
where something happened.

01:25:16.590 --> 01:25:18.750
And the simulator would
figure out when that was,

01:25:18.750 --> 01:25:20.280
and just take that
leap up there.

01:25:20.280 --> 01:25:20.508
PRESENTER 7: That's great.

01:25:20.508 --> 01:25:20.870
I really liked that.

01:25:20.870 --> 01:25:22.328
PRESENTER 1: And
it really advanced

01:25:22.328 --> 01:25:24.210
the speed of the
simulation to the point

01:25:24.210 --> 01:25:26.490
where it was nearly real
time in a lot of cases,

01:25:26.490 --> 01:25:28.890
dependent on how busy
the computer was.

01:25:28.890 --> 01:25:31.320
But there were lots of periods
where it was just idling,

01:25:31.320 --> 01:25:34.660
and maybe a few instructions,
or maybe a few seconds.

01:25:34.660 --> 01:25:37.140
But it would just leap
the clock forward.

01:25:37.140 --> 01:25:40.620
And that, as far as I know,
never caused any difficulties.

01:25:40.620 --> 01:25:43.080
Because we didn't
pass anything where,

01:25:43.080 --> 01:25:45.410
if an interrupt happened,
that's where we'd go

01:25:45.410 --> 01:25:47.950
or something else.

01:25:47.950 --> 01:25:49.950
There [? were ?] a lot
of tricky stuff in there.

01:25:49.950 --> 01:25:51.366
PRESENTER 7: I
keep telling people

01:25:51.366 --> 01:25:54.756
what that simulator used to
do, and they can't believe it.

01:25:54.756 --> 01:25:57.790
[? It's ?] many things
it did that people

01:25:57.790 --> 01:25:59.617
don't do today that
they should do.

01:25:59.617 --> 01:26:01.450
PRESENTER 1: One of the
fascinating problems

01:26:01.450 --> 01:26:05.050
was the reuse of
erasable memory.

01:26:05.050 --> 01:26:07.390
And like every memory
problem, a piece

01:26:07.390 --> 01:26:10.360
of software that would
use somebody else's memory

01:26:10.360 --> 01:26:12.190
would likely get away
with it because if it

01:26:12.190 --> 01:26:15.820
started writing into that
area, and then completed what

01:26:15.820 --> 01:26:17.170
it would do, it would go away.

01:26:17.170 --> 01:26:20.530
And then the person who had left
something in there for its use

01:26:20.530 --> 01:26:22.074
when it came back
would hit something

01:26:22.074 --> 01:26:23.740
that somebody else
had written in there,

01:26:23.740 --> 01:26:25.630
and do it completely wrong.

01:26:25.630 --> 01:26:29.470
And then, by then, the
real violator was long gone

01:26:29.470 --> 01:26:31.560
and you had to figure
out who did it.

01:26:31.560 --> 01:26:33.190
PRESENTER 4: Who done it?

01:26:33.190 --> 01:26:35.380
PRESENTER 1: And there
were also problems

01:26:35.380 --> 01:26:39.050
of using erasable that
hadn't been initialized

01:26:39.050 --> 01:26:40.870
the way you thought it had.

01:26:40.870 --> 01:26:45.880
And we came up with a technique
to put a kind of random number

01:26:45.880 --> 01:26:47.889
into all of erasable
when we started.

01:26:47.889 --> 01:26:49.180
PRESENTER 7: In the background.

01:26:49.180 --> 01:26:54.820
PRESENTER 1: And if that was
ever hit, you could see it,

01:26:54.820 --> 01:26:58.210
and we had a way to put
that same random number back

01:26:58.210 --> 01:27:01.191
in so you could do all kinds
of diagnosis to find it.

01:27:01.191 --> 01:27:03.190
There were just things
that nobody does anymore.

01:27:03.190 --> 01:27:05.440
PRESENTER 4: You know, they
still haven't solved that.

01:27:05.440 --> 01:27:08.080
You don't know how many
students of mine now,

01:27:08.080 --> 01:27:09.710
their programs only
work when they're

01:27:09.710 --> 01:27:12.700
0 in the location
when they start.

01:27:12.700 --> 01:27:14.320
That's the worst
thing, I've decided,

01:27:14.320 --> 01:27:16.557
that you can put
in [? unused-- ?]

01:27:16.557 --> 01:27:17.890
PRESENTER 1: Yeah, that's right.

01:27:17.890 --> 01:27:20.060
PRESENTER 7: But why aren't
people doing these things?

01:27:20.060 --> 01:27:21.810
There are so many
things that would really

01:27:21.810 --> 01:27:27.970
help debug systems today that
never were brought forward.

01:27:27.970 --> 01:27:31.010
Many things are, but that
was one that was, I think,

01:27:31.010 --> 01:27:36.120
more advanced than most
debugging tools we have.

01:27:36.120 --> 01:27:37.410
Why do you think that is?

01:27:42.040 --> 01:27:46.010
GEROVITCH: Was there any
effort to disseminate,

01:27:46.010 --> 01:27:48.430
in a systematic way, the kind
of knowledge and programming

01:27:48.430 --> 01:27:51.080
that you gained
within that project

01:27:51.080 --> 01:27:55.330
to flesh out the most
important principles that

01:27:55.330 --> 01:27:59.610
were worked out in the work of
this project, and published?

01:27:59.610 --> 01:28:04.630
PRESENTER 6: There have been a
few summaries of the program.

01:28:04.630 --> 01:28:07.270
People have written volumes
I think that you probably

01:28:07.270 --> 01:28:09.917
have gotten your hands on.

01:28:09.917 --> 01:28:11.500
I know there was a
late volume written

01:28:11.500 --> 01:28:16.960
by that woman,
[? Madeline ?] Sullivan?

01:28:16.960 --> 01:28:23.670
And sometime, she wrote a
multi-volume history of Apollo.

01:28:23.670 --> 01:28:26.280
I think it was one
of the lab documents.

01:28:26.280 --> 01:28:30.160
But I don't know
that anybody has

01:28:30.160 --> 01:28:43.180
tried to record the essence
of, let's say, programming

01:28:43.180 --> 01:28:46.900
breakthroughs, or programming
inventions, or something

01:28:46.900 --> 01:28:48.030
of that nature.

01:28:48.030 --> 01:28:53.914
Or the salient discoveries,
if you will, in the Apollo.

01:28:53.914 --> 01:28:55.330
I haven't seen
anything like that.

01:28:55.330 --> 01:28:56.746
PRESENTER 4: No,
I don't think so.

01:28:56.746 --> 01:28:58.810
Some parts of the
engineering have been

01:28:58.810 --> 01:29:01.360
covered with some good papers--

01:29:01.360 --> 01:29:04.720
well, [? Batten's ?] got a book,
but that's more theoretical.

01:29:04.720 --> 01:29:11.060
But haven't there been some
papers on LEM's autopilot lunar

01:29:11.060 --> 01:29:11.830
landing?

01:29:11.830 --> 01:29:12.704
PRESENTER 6: Perhaps.

01:29:12.704 --> 01:29:13.259
I haven't--

01:29:13.259 --> 01:29:14.300
PRESENTER 1: Bill wrote--

01:29:14.300 --> 01:29:16.649
Bill [? Wittennall ?]
wrote a book on autopilot.

01:29:16.649 --> 01:29:17.440
PRESENTER 4: Right.

01:29:17.440 --> 01:29:19.750
PRESENTER 1: I think it
was the CSM autopilot.

01:29:19.750 --> 01:29:21.790
PRESENTER 4: Right.

01:29:21.790 --> 01:29:24.070
PRESENTER 1: Published
by MIT Press.

01:29:24.070 --> 01:29:25.857
PRESENTER 4: That's
vaguely coming back.

01:29:25.857 --> 01:29:26.440
Maybe that's--

01:29:26.440 --> 01:29:27.430
PRESENTER 1: But
not too many people

01:29:27.430 --> 01:29:28.596
were thinking of publishing.

01:29:28.596 --> 01:29:31.870
They were trying
to just get home.

01:29:31.870 --> 01:29:33.370
I mean, I don't
know how many people

01:29:33.370 --> 01:29:36.760
were working six or seven-day
weeks, 80-hour weeks,

01:29:36.760 --> 01:29:39.130
and doing everything
they possibly could

01:29:39.130 --> 01:29:42.010
in their power to get these
guys to the moon and back.

01:29:42.010 --> 01:29:44.340
PRESENTER 5: A
number of marriages

01:29:44.340 --> 01:29:48.907
sank during that
period, including mine.

01:29:48.907 --> 01:29:51.490
PRESENTER 6: I think there was
an element in this program that

01:29:51.490 --> 01:29:56.440
is missing in a lot
of current programs.

01:29:56.440 --> 01:30:01.330
And the 60, 70-hour week
that we just spoke about,

01:30:01.330 --> 01:30:03.140
I'm sure that people
do that today, too.

01:30:03.140 --> 01:30:05.755
But there was an--

01:30:05.755 --> 01:30:10.720
I don't know how to say this--
an immediacy, or a familiarity

01:30:10.720 --> 01:30:12.177
with the astronauts.

01:30:12.177 --> 01:30:14.260
I'm talking about people
at MIT, but it's probably

01:30:14.260 --> 01:30:17.380
true in other parts
of the Apollo program.

01:30:17.380 --> 01:30:24.940
That you felt a certain
personal responsibility to--

01:30:24.940 --> 01:30:27.730
either you made promises,
or you had conversations

01:30:27.730 --> 01:30:30.250
with these people,
or you were doing it

01:30:30.250 --> 01:30:37.780
because you wanted to make sure
that there was absolutely zero

01:30:37.780 --> 01:30:38.950
errors in this program.

01:30:38.950 --> 01:30:41.890
Because you knew that
their lives were at stake.

01:30:41.890 --> 01:30:46.240
There was some sort of
connection to the astronauts

01:30:46.240 --> 01:30:48.130
that we at--

01:30:48.130 --> 01:30:51.460
I felt, and I think other
people at MIT felt--

01:30:51.460 --> 01:30:54.670
perhaps because they
came there for training.

01:30:54.670 --> 01:30:57.310
Or I know there were occasions
we went to dinner with Doc

01:30:57.310 --> 01:31:01.140
Draper at [? Lockovers ?]
with some of the astronauts

01:31:01.140 --> 01:31:03.910
after a flight.

01:31:03.910 --> 01:31:06.580
And I think that affected some--

01:31:06.580 --> 01:31:14.320
or kept morale up, and kept
people's attention, and feeling

01:31:14.320 --> 01:31:19.410
of dedication to the job because
of that sort of connection.

01:31:19.410 --> 01:31:21.550
You weren't working for
some big-- necessarily some

01:31:21.550 --> 01:31:23.260
big bureaucracy.

01:31:23.260 --> 01:31:32.240
It was this-- in my case, it
was sort of a feeling that--

01:31:32.240 --> 01:31:38.110
some sort of connection
with Chris [? Craft, ?]

01:31:38.110 --> 01:31:45.640
that you were satisfying his
leadership, you might say.

01:31:45.640 --> 01:31:50.320
Plus this feeling-- this sort
of feeling of the astronauts,

01:31:50.320 --> 01:31:54.000
I would say, that certainly
affected me in the job.

01:31:54.000 --> 01:31:55.000
PRESENTER 4: Absolutely.

01:31:55.000 --> 01:31:55.720
They were here.

01:31:55.720 --> 01:32:00.700
I mean, which Apollo flight had
Jim [? McDevit, ?] Dave Scott,

01:32:00.700 --> 01:32:05.230
and Rusty [? Schweiker? ?]
That was Apollo 7, 8?

01:32:05.230 --> 01:32:06.580
Whatever.

01:32:06.580 --> 01:32:09.640
But they seemed to be around
frequently before that,

01:32:09.640 --> 01:32:12.220
and you just seemed to
see them doing this stuff,

01:32:12.220 --> 01:32:14.520
and get to know what they
were doing all the time.

01:32:14.520 --> 01:32:16.392
It felt very close.

01:32:16.392 --> 01:32:18.100
In fact, we used to
laugh at them picking

01:32:18.100 --> 01:32:21.910
on Dave [? Scott. ?] He wasn't
quite as fast picking up

01:32:21.910 --> 01:32:24.130
the stuff when you
showed them that.

01:32:24.130 --> 01:32:27.490
But they were there frequently.

01:32:27.490 --> 01:32:29.830
But even before that, I
remember the first time

01:32:29.830 --> 01:32:32.810
we went to Houston and gave a
presentation to a lot of them,

01:32:32.810 --> 01:32:34.540
that was earlier in the--

01:32:34.540 --> 01:32:36.100
still in the engineering mode.

01:32:36.100 --> 01:32:37.570
I mean, these guys
came out and--

01:32:37.570 --> 01:32:38.530
[GASPS]

01:32:38.530 --> 01:32:40.760
They didn't just sit
there and nod off.

01:32:40.760 --> 01:32:42.590
They were excited, and asked--

01:32:42.590 --> 01:32:44.660
I remember Neil
Armstrong overwhelmed me

01:32:44.660 --> 01:32:47.335
with the questions he asked,
and how well-prepared,

01:32:47.335 --> 01:32:48.315
and how thoughtful.

01:32:48.315 --> 01:32:53.600
And I said, jeez, these guys,
there's no fooling them.

01:32:53.600 --> 01:32:55.070
They know what
they're looking for,

01:32:55.070 --> 01:32:58.610
and we better be really careful
of what's going on here,

01:32:58.610 --> 01:33:00.300
and make sure we're prepared.

01:33:00.300 --> 01:33:03.484
When they ask questions like
that, you can't wing it.

01:33:03.484 --> 01:33:05.150
GEROVITCH: Were they
actually interested

01:33:05.150 --> 01:33:08.025
in software, as such?

01:33:08.025 --> 01:33:09.400
PRESENTER 6: Some
were, actually.

01:33:09.400 --> 01:33:10.340
PRESENTER 5: Not per se.

01:33:10.340 --> 01:33:12.320
PRESENTER 4: But they wanted
to know how this worked,

01:33:12.320 --> 01:33:14.653
and what was going to happen,
and what it meant to them,

01:33:14.653 --> 01:33:15.200
and how it--

01:33:15.200 --> 01:33:16.730
PRESENTER 5: They worked
through the controls,

01:33:16.730 --> 01:33:19.110
but a lot of the controls
were driven by the software.

01:33:19.110 --> 01:33:23.420
So what they said or didn't say
had an immediate impact on us,

01:33:23.420 --> 01:33:26.420
who were making those
things work in the front,

01:33:26.420 --> 01:33:28.880
through the software itself.

01:33:28.880 --> 01:33:32.960
I remember Steve
[INAUDIBLE] was the man--

01:33:32.960 --> 01:33:34.940
I've forgotten what the
hell that thing was.

01:33:34.940 --> 01:33:35.870
PRESENTER 4: Oh,
you should mention--

01:33:35.870 --> 01:33:37.328
PRESENTER 5: The
astronaut document

01:33:37.328 --> 01:33:39.610
that gave the whole sequences
of all of [INAUDIBLE]..

01:33:39.610 --> 01:33:40.401
PRESENTER 4: Right.

01:33:40.401 --> 01:33:42.190
The word for John [INAUDIBLE].

01:33:42.190 --> 01:33:43.120
What's the name of it?

01:33:43.120 --> 01:33:43.790
PRESENTER 7: The [? GSOP. ?]

01:33:43.790 --> 01:33:46.160
PRESENTER 5: The guidance
systems operations plan.

01:33:46.160 --> 01:33:48.950
PRESENTER 4: Steve and
Malcolm and Jack Schilling

01:33:48.950 --> 01:33:51.740
all cranked out all that stuff
that went on and on and on.

01:33:51.740 --> 01:33:53.240
PRESENTER 5: This
was the first time

01:33:53.240 --> 01:33:56.140
I'd ever seen a
specification for anything.

01:33:56.140 --> 01:33:58.847
And today, you design
the spec first,

01:33:58.847 --> 01:34:00.430
and the software
falls out the bottom.

01:34:00.430 --> 01:34:05.030
But those GSOPs, I
remember John [? Dolan ?]

01:34:05.030 --> 01:34:06.450
was the guy-- remember him?

01:34:06.450 --> 01:34:07.760
PRESENTER 4: Yeah, yeah.

01:34:07.760 --> 01:34:10.160
PRESENTER 5: The man with the
eight kids, I think he had.

01:34:10.160 --> 01:34:10.280
PRESENTER 4: That's right.

01:34:10.280 --> 01:34:11.570
PRESENTER 5: Who lived
in the South Shore

01:34:11.570 --> 01:34:13.236
somewhere, and came
into work every day.

01:34:15.436 --> 01:34:17.060
PRESENTER 4: That
was a thankless task,

01:34:17.060 --> 01:34:18.430
churning out all that stuff.

01:34:18.430 --> 01:34:19.150
PRESENTER 5: Yep.

01:34:19.150 --> 01:34:20.270
PRESENTER 4: And you
had to get it all--

01:34:20.270 --> 01:34:21.430
PRESENTER 5: Because he was--

01:34:21.430 --> 01:34:21.860
the astronaut--

01:34:21.860 --> 01:34:23.940
PRESENTER 4: They had to run
down and catch us to get--

01:34:23.940 --> 01:34:24.230
PRESENTER 5: Yeah.

01:34:24.230 --> 01:34:26.063
PRESENTER 4: Every time
they had a question,

01:34:26.063 --> 01:34:28.510
they'd start-- can I
talk to you a minute?

01:34:28.510 --> 01:34:30.110
PRESENTER 5: Yeah.

01:34:30.110 --> 01:34:38.810
PRESENTER 6: I still have
something like 280,000 Eastern

01:34:38.810 --> 01:34:43.190
Airlines miles that are now
part of the Continental system

01:34:43.190 --> 01:34:45.850
that never expire.

01:34:45.850 --> 01:34:46.700
And--

01:34:46.700 --> 01:34:47.690
PRESENTER 1: Look out.

01:34:47.690 --> 01:34:49.820
PRESENTER 6: These were
on these multiple--

01:34:49.820 --> 01:34:52.010
PRESENTER 1: Midway has
already thrown in the towel.

01:34:52.010 --> 01:34:53.106
Who's next?

01:34:53.106 --> 01:34:54.730
PRESENTER 6: Multiple
trips to Houston.

01:34:54.730 --> 01:34:59.431
But one of them, I want to just
bring up in the conversation

01:34:59.431 --> 01:34:59.930
here.

01:34:59.930 --> 01:35:03.350
I don't know how many trips
we all made to Houston.

01:35:03.350 --> 01:35:04.520
I can remember--

01:35:04.520 --> 01:35:08.540
I can just see myself having
this heated discussion

01:35:08.540 --> 01:35:12.530
for an hour and a half
between Boston and Atlanta

01:35:12.530 --> 01:35:15.500
with Margaret on some
programming issue,

01:35:15.500 --> 01:35:17.245
or something that we were doing.

01:35:17.245 --> 01:35:20.660
But we attended innumerable
meetings down at NASA,

01:35:20.660 --> 01:35:23.820
and we had a very
live wire at NASA who

01:35:23.820 --> 01:35:25.400
we went to see all the time.

01:35:25.400 --> 01:35:30.280
And that was this guy Jack
[? Garmin, ?] who really was--

01:35:30.280 --> 01:35:34.220
he really-- if there was anybody
at NASA who sort of understood

01:35:34.220 --> 01:35:36.950
what we were trying to
do, it was this guy,

01:35:36.950 --> 01:35:38.854
Jack [? Garmin. ?] And--

01:35:38.854 --> 01:35:39.770
PRESENTER 4: I think--

01:35:39.770 --> 01:35:42.540
I always felt like
Dr. Frankenstein,

01:35:42.540 --> 01:35:43.665
that I'd created a monster.

01:35:43.665 --> 01:35:44.180
PRESENTER 6: Yeah.

01:35:44.180 --> 01:35:45.638
PRESENTER 4: He
got assigned to me,

01:35:45.638 --> 01:35:47.775
and for six months, an
hour a day, I'd keep--

01:35:47.775 --> 01:35:48.650
PRESENTER 6: He was--

01:35:48.650 --> 01:35:51.150
PRESENTER 4: --telling him the
stuff, and he was persistent.

01:35:51.150 --> 01:35:53.720
Phone calls, phone
calls, phone--

01:35:53.720 --> 01:35:56.480
PRESENTER 6: But I remember
this day, this particular day

01:35:56.480 --> 01:36:00.020
that [? I had ?] finished
a meeting at NASA,

01:36:00.020 --> 01:36:02.960
and I was coming home.

01:36:02.960 --> 01:36:07.690
And we were standing--

01:36:07.690 --> 01:36:10.535
I guess walking between the
parking lot and the terminal.

01:36:13.120 --> 01:36:15.510
It was like a driveway
that headed up

01:36:15.510 --> 01:36:18.560
toward the terminal in Houston.

01:36:18.560 --> 01:36:23.180
I can't remember whether this
was Hobby or the International.

01:36:23.180 --> 01:36:24.660
I can't really remember.

01:36:24.660 --> 01:36:27.980
But anyway, everybody
said, well, wait a minute.

01:36:27.980 --> 01:36:29.410
There's the President.

01:36:29.410 --> 01:36:33.620
And so we watched, and this open
car came from the terminal--

01:36:33.620 --> 01:36:34.430
passed us.

01:36:34.430 --> 01:36:37.250
And there weren't very many
people standing around.

01:36:37.250 --> 01:36:41.210
Maybe one deep
along the road that

01:36:41.210 --> 01:36:42.620
might have been 1/2 a mile long.

01:36:42.620 --> 01:36:48.920
And he rode by in an open
car with his wife at maybe--

01:36:48.920 --> 01:36:51.260
I thought it was around
15 miles an hour,

01:36:51.260 --> 01:36:53.170
but nothing faster than that.

01:36:53.170 --> 01:36:54.650
Maybe 20.

01:36:54.650 --> 01:36:56.220
And I remember standing there.

01:36:56.220 --> 01:36:57.050
I don't know who was with me.

01:36:57.050 --> 01:36:58.550
And I said something
like, you know,

01:36:58.550 --> 01:37:01.220
if anybody wanted to kill this
guy, this would be pretty easy.

01:37:01.220 --> 01:37:05.600
Because he's just rolling
by in an open car and so on.

01:37:05.600 --> 01:37:09.980
Well that was November 21, 1963.

01:37:09.980 --> 01:37:14.000
And I think it was a Thursday,
I think, and I was coming home.

01:37:14.000 --> 01:37:15.890
And I was in my
office the next day.

01:37:15.890 --> 01:37:17.630
And he was no longer in Houston.

01:37:17.630 --> 01:37:22.740
He had gone that night, and then
he went to Dallas the next day.

01:37:22.740 --> 01:37:25.082
PRESENTER 4: Yeah, I went
there that same Thursday.

01:37:25.082 --> 01:37:26.540
I think it was a
different meeting.

01:37:26.540 --> 01:37:27.830
And he went right down--

01:37:27.830 --> 01:37:31.730
we were-- they hadn't
built that MSC yet.

01:37:31.730 --> 01:37:32.720
We were in Houston.

01:37:32.720 --> 01:37:35.900
It was an office park,
whatever it was called.

01:37:35.900 --> 01:37:38.630
And he went right down
the main highway there,

01:37:38.630 --> 01:37:40.420
although we didn't
go out and see him.

01:37:40.420 --> 01:37:42.760
But I stayed over till
Friday, and then Friday, I

01:37:42.760 --> 01:37:43.840
was heading to go home.

01:37:43.840 --> 01:37:49.220
And then, at the airport,
we got all this news.

01:37:49.220 --> 01:37:49.790
And--

01:37:49.790 --> 01:37:51.310
PRESENTER 5: But do you
think it was a Friday?

01:37:51.310 --> 01:37:51.940
PRESENTER 4: Yeah.

01:37:51.940 --> 01:37:52.190
PRESENTER 5: Yeah.

01:37:52.190 --> 01:37:52.810
That's my recollection.

01:37:52.810 --> 01:37:53.070
PRESENTER 4: Yeah.

01:37:53.070 --> 01:37:55.600
But Thursday, he was in
Houston, and went up and down

01:37:55.600 --> 01:37:58.240
whatever the name of
that [? Gulf ?] freeway,

01:37:58.240 --> 01:37:59.230
or whatever it is.

01:37:59.230 --> 01:38:00.610
There was a parade or something.

01:38:00.610 --> 01:38:01.330
And then Friday--

01:38:01.330 --> 01:38:04.245
I stayed over Thursday
night, some more meetings

01:38:04.245 --> 01:38:04.870
Friday morning.

01:38:04.870 --> 01:38:06.650
Was heading home
Friday afternoon,

01:38:06.650 --> 01:38:10.060
and then, in the Hobby Airport,
we heard the first news

01:38:10.060 --> 01:38:13.120
that all of this had happened.

01:38:13.120 --> 01:38:16.300
PRESENTER 6: I wanted to touch
upon this religious war I

01:38:16.300 --> 01:38:20.260
mentioned before, and get
into it just for a moment.

01:38:23.920 --> 01:38:28.570
The issue really came to a
head in the shuttle program,

01:38:28.570 --> 01:38:31.090
where there were
forces that believed

01:38:31.090 --> 01:38:34.300
in asynchronous,
priority-driven,

01:38:34.300 --> 01:38:36.760
interrupt-driven executives.

01:38:36.760 --> 01:38:40.450
And other forces that
believed in absolutely

01:38:40.450 --> 01:38:44.470
synchronous executives,
where you planned out

01:38:44.470 --> 01:38:47.080
all the software,
and you executed

01:38:47.080 --> 01:38:50.270
the software by tables.

01:38:50.270 --> 01:38:54.730
And you could tell what
part of the software

01:38:54.730 --> 01:38:58.420
was operating at
every instant of time

01:38:58.420 --> 01:39:01.750
because you had planned
it out so that it all

01:39:01.750 --> 01:39:04.450
operated very rigidly.

01:39:04.450 --> 01:39:08.470
And the people who really
used those kinds of executives

01:39:08.470 --> 01:39:11.440
were mostly people in
the aircraft industry.

01:39:11.440 --> 01:39:14.080
And they had come from
various airplanes, where

01:39:14.080 --> 01:39:16.180
they had these
computers and they had

01:39:16.180 --> 01:39:17.584
these synchronous executives.

01:39:17.584 --> 01:39:19.000
And it was very
important for them

01:39:19.000 --> 01:39:21.700
to know exactly
what was happening

01:39:21.700 --> 01:39:26.350
at every instant of time,
either from a testing standpoint

01:39:26.350 --> 01:39:27.255
or whatever.

01:39:27.255 --> 01:39:29.700
That was their mindset.

01:39:29.700 --> 01:39:34.240
And the people that had worked
on Apollo, for the most part,

01:39:34.240 --> 01:39:38.080
believed in this
asynchronous executive, where

01:39:38.080 --> 01:39:40.270
you have priorities
and you didn't

01:39:40.270 --> 01:39:43.810
have everything
structured completely,

01:39:43.810 --> 01:39:46.540
but you allowed
higher priority jobs

01:39:46.540 --> 01:39:51.260
to interrupt lower
priority jobs, and so on.

01:39:51.260 --> 01:39:55.150
And there were these
incredible discussions

01:39:55.150 --> 01:39:58.270
in the early shuttle
time, in deciding

01:39:58.270 --> 01:40:01.930
what kind of an executive
should be used in the--

01:40:01.930 --> 01:40:06.160
operating system should
be used in the shuttle.

01:40:06.160 --> 01:40:08.680
And the shuttle had its
own hardware issues,

01:40:08.680 --> 01:40:19.205
with multi-reliability
strings and IO processor,

01:40:19.205 --> 01:40:20.830
and other things that
had to be synced.

01:40:20.830 --> 01:40:24.740
And so it had a lot of reasons
why this was a big issue.

01:40:24.740 --> 01:40:30.280
But the roots of
the Apollo viewpoint

01:40:30.280 --> 01:40:34.990
really stemmed back
to the alarms that

01:40:34.990 --> 01:40:38.440
occurred on the lunar landing.

01:40:38.440 --> 01:40:45.100
And as you recall, in that
flight when that LEM was coming

01:40:45.100 --> 01:40:48.420
down, and all of a sudden,
he had these alarms,

01:40:48.420 --> 01:40:50.270
and the astronaut kept
getting these alarms.

01:40:50.270 --> 01:40:52.540
He said that he had
alarms and, finally,

01:40:52.540 --> 01:40:55.060
there was a decision in
Houston to just push on,

01:40:55.060 --> 01:40:56.650
and that he should land.

01:40:56.650 --> 01:40:58.370
And whoever made that decision--

01:40:58.370 --> 01:41:00.430
Steve [? Bailes ?]
or whoever it was--

01:41:00.430 --> 01:41:02.290
either he understood
what the problem was,

01:41:02.290 --> 01:41:04.454
or he felt that he--

01:41:04.454 --> 01:41:06.620
PRESENTER 4: Well, Jack
[? Garmin ?] was screaming--

01:41:06.620 --> 01:41:08.509
I heard it-- go, go, go, go.

01:41:08.509 --> 01:41:10.300
And I don't think he
had any evidence other

01:41:10.300 --> 01:41:12.935
than he thought we had
restarted it perfectly.

01:41:12.935 --> 01:41:14.560
PRESENTER 6: Anyway,
he landed safely.

01:41:14.560 --> 01:41:18.040
And following that
landing, there

01:41:18.040 --> 01:41:26.680
was just a frantic
session at the MIT lab

01:41:26.680 --> 01:41:30.550
to find out what in the world
was causing these alarms.

01:41:30.550 --> 01:41:35.380
And it took us the better
part of 24 hours, I think,

01:41:35.380 --> 01:41:36.970
as I recall.

01:41:36.970 --> 01:41:41.410
And going back to the
simulators, we simulated this.

01:41:41.410 --> 01:41:44.630
We tried everything
to make this happen,

01:41:44.630 --> 01:41:46.521
and we could not
make this happen.

01:41:50.620 --> 01:41:55.660
It was as if the AGC was
operating at about 25% slower

01:41:55.660 --> 01:41:58.640
than it usually operates.

01:41:58.640 --> 01:42:04.430
And so we tried lots of things,
and we couldn't make it happen.

01:42:04.430 --> 01:42:09.280
Eventually, [? over ?] the
suggestion of somebody who

01:42:09.280 --> 01:42:12.700
worked at the Cape a lot,
we tracked down the fact

01:42:12.700 --> 01:42:15.280
that they had a switch
in the wrong position,

01:42:15.280 --> 01:42:19.120
and it was stealing cycles.

01:42:19.120 --> 01:42:21.410
And there was a radar
rendezvous switch,

01:42:21.410 --> 01:42:25.650
and that made the
AGC run slower.

01:42:25.650 --> 01:42:27.580
And because it was
running slower,

01:42:27.580 --> 01:42:30.100
it was dropping
low-priority jobs,

01:42:30.100 --> 01:42:31.930
and not getting to
these low-priority jobs.

01:42:31.930 --> 01:42:35.210
And only doing the
highest-priority jobs.

01:42:35.210 --> 01:42:37.945
And so the job queue
filled up, and when

01:42:37.945 --> 01:42:40.140
the job queue filled
up, it caused this alarm

01:42:40.140 --> 01:42:41.730
to go off and so on.

01:42:44.790 --> 01:42:50.070
At any rate, MIT got
a lot of criticism

01:42:50.070 --> 01:42:55.500
for software era
causing this to happen.

01:42:55.500 --> 01:43:00.087
And so now you had
sort of a dichotomy,

01:43:00.087 --> 01:43:01.920
where people believed
that what had happened

01:43:01.920 --> 01:43:05.270
was actually an error, and
other people believed, no,

01:43:05.270 --> 01:43:07.830
the software actually
saved the program.

01:43:07.830 --> 01:43:16.832
Because in the face of
this mistake in the switch,

01:43:16.832 --> 01:43:22.410
the software, which was written
as a priority executive,

01:43:22.410 --> 01:43:26.370
was able to go on with the
highest-priority jobs, and not

01:43:26.370 --> 01:43:28.740
tank the mission
because it didn't

01:43:28.740 --> 01:43:32.940
have to do this
boxcar-structured, synchronous

01:43:32.940 --> 01:43:35.020
system, where it would
give time to everything,

01:43:35.020 --> 01:43:36.354
whether it was important or not.

01:43:36.354 --> 01:43:37.811
PRESENTER 7: Well,
you know, it was

01:43:37.811 --> 01:43:40.170
even more complicated than
that because the error was

01:43:40.170 --> 01:43:42.120
in the actual documentation.

01:43:42.120 --> 01:43:45.510
PRESENTER 6: Yes, I was going
to mention that in a moment.

01:43:45.510 --> 01:43:51.150
So this feeling went forward,
including the NASA folks--

01:43:51.150 --> 01:43:53.610
people like Jack
Garmin, who absolutely

01:43:53.610 --> 01:43:57.390
felt that it was important to
have such a system in shuttle--

01:43:57.390 --> 01:44:02.040
versus the airplane folks,
who felt that they could never

01:44:02.040 --> 01:44:04.470
be able to test the
software afterward

01:44:04.470 --> 01:44:07.800
if they couldn't have an exact
match, and know exactly what

01:44:07.800 --> 01:44:10.560
happened at any time.

01:44:10.560 --> 01:44:15.150
And the asynchronous
executive was almost not

01:44:15.150 --> 01:44:17.370
repeatable, you might say.

01:44:17.370 --> 01:44:20.700
So that caused a great
deal of difficulty.

01:44:20.700 --> 01:44:23.070
What Margaret is pointing
out is that, when we finally

01:44:23.070 --> 01:44:24.930
got, and found--

01:44:24.930 --> 01:44:27.389
I remember the incident
that we ran upstairs--

01:44:27.389 --> 01:44:28.680
PRESENTER 4: [? Ross ?] Larson.

01:44:28.680 --> 01:44:31.710
PRESENTER 6: --to
look at the telemetry

01:44:31.710 --> 01:44:34.350
to see where the bits were set.

01:44:34.350 --> 01:44:37.290
And sure enough, the
[? radars ?] bit-- the switch--

01:44:37.290 --> 01:44:41.790
the bit, which was picked up
by telemetry and downloaded,

01:44:41.790 --> 01:44:45.074
that was in, let's say, 15-bit
[? or ?] how many [? bits-- ?]

01:44:45.074 --> 01:44:46.740
[? words ?] there
were in the telemetry,

01:44:46.740 --> 01:44:48.420
and bit [? 9, ?]
or whatever it was,

01:44:48.420 --> 01:44:49.970
showed that the switch was on.

01:44:49.970 --> 01:44:51.220
PRESENTER 4: Rendezvous radar.

01:44:51.220 --> 01:44:52.011
PRESENTER 6: Right.

01:44:52.011 --> 01:44:54.480
Which meant that
that's what it was.

01:44:54.480 --> 01:44:56.700
And eventually, they told--

01:44:56.700 --> 01:44:58.470
the ground told
the astronaut, when

01:44:58.470 --> 01:45:00.120
he was about to take
off from the moon,

01:45:00.120 --> 01:45:01.786
to put the switch in
the right position,

01:45:01.786 --> 01:45:04.380
he said, in a very
low-key fashion.

01:45:04.380 --> 01:45:07.710
But when that was
run down to the end,

01:45:07.710 --> 01:45:10.410
it was found in
the crew procedures

01:45:10.410 --> 01:45:13.260
to put the switch
in this position.

01:45:13.260 --> 01:45:14.790
So the next question
was, well, how

01:45:14.790 --> 01:45:16.920
come, if you put the
switch in this position,

01:45:16.920 --> 01:45:22.380
wasn't this picked
up in the simulators

01:45:22.380 --> 01:45:26.160
at Grumman, which these
guys had been training on

01:45:26.160 --> 01:45:29.320
for, I don't know,
a couple of years.

01:45:29.320 --> 01:45:33.450
And in that crew
simulator, they had always

01:45:33.450 --> 01:45:35.640
done exactly what was
in the document, put

01:45:35.640 --> 01:45:38.200
the switch in that position.

01:45:38.200 --> 01:45:42.990
However, that switch wasn't
connected to anything.

01:45:42.990 --> 01:45:44.670
In other words,
that switch was not

01:45:44.670 --> 01:45:50.610
connected, in the simulator, to
the AGC, which would slow them

01:45:50.610 --> 01:45:51.630
down in that simulator.

01:45:51.630 --> 01:45:53.005
PRESENTER 4: You
mean at Grumman?

01:45:53.005 --> 01:45:54.990
PRESENTER 6: At Grumman.

01:45:54.990 --> 01:45:58.470
So they did-- they
trained exactly

01:45:58.470 --> 01:46:03.210
to what those crew
procedures said each time,

01:46:03.210 --> 01:46:05.940
and they did it on
the landing, too.

01:46:07.835 --> 01:46:09.460
PRESENTER 7: Well,
it kind of gives you

01:46:09.460 --> 01:46:12.850
an understanding more of
how important everything is

01:46:12.850 --> 01:46:18.010
with respect to the
entire system of software,

01:46:18.010 --> 01:46:21.290
[? peopleware, ?]
you know, that--

01:46:21.290 --> 01:46:22.990
PRESENTER 4: And I
put it the other way.

01:46:22.990 --> 01:46:26.059
Why didn't our simulations
follow the crew procedures?

01:46:26.059 --> 01:46:27.850
PRESENTER 6: I don't
know that the crew did

01:46:27.850 --> 01:46:29.920
that in our simulations.

01:46:29.920 --> 01:46:31.455
I don't know.

01:46:31.455 --> 01:46:32.980
It probably didn't--

01:46:32.980 --> 01:46:35.297
I don't think it was in
our digital simulator.

01:46:35.297 --> 01:46:36.130
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

01:46:36.130 --> 01:46:38.390
PRESENTER 4: --if I turned
on the rendezvous radar?

01:46:38.390 --> 01:46:39.100
PRESENTER 6: I don't know.

01:46:39.100 --> 01:46:40.780
PRESENTER 7: But
when you're testing--

01:46:40.780 --> 01:46:42.550
back to the system thing--

01:46:42.550 --> 01:46:45.470
what would make the software
do something like that?

01:46:45.470 --> 01:46:47.560
In other words,
you begin to think

01:46:47.560 --> 01:46:51.290
in terms of modeling
and designing a problem.

01:46:51.290 --> 01:46:52.570
And where do you stop?

01:46:52.570 --> 01:46:54.700
Where's the outside
of the outside?

01:46:54.700 --> 01:46:57.970
But it's modeling all
these things as one system

01:46:57.970 --> 01:47:00.340
of which part is
realized as software,

01:47:00.340 --> 01:47:04.210
part as [? peopleware, ?]
part as hardware.

01:47:04.210 --> 01:47:07.150
But then, you begin to model
it as an entire enterprise,

01:47:07.150 --> 01:47:09.010
and then simulate
it as an enterprise.

01:47:09.010 --> 01:47:13.490
But it makes you realize that
when people blame something

01:47:13.490 --> 01:47:16.420
as being a software error
or a hardware error,

01:47:16.420 --> 01:47:18.115
you're not necessarily
sure what is it,

01:47:18.115 --> 01:47:20.650
and what does it mean to be a
software error or a hardware

01:47:20.650 --> 01:47:21.820
error?

01:47:21.820 --> 01:47:25.600
And this brings up
all these questions

01:47:25.600 --> 01:47:28.345
when you have events
like this take place.

01:47:31.110 --> 01:47:36.620
And we've still not solved it
today, I mean, in the industry.

01:47:36.620 --> 01:47:39.260
GEROVITCH: So did they stick
to that asynchronous design

01:47:39.260 --> 01:47:40.922
in the shuttle program?

01:47:40.922 --> 01:47:42.880
PRESENTER 6: Did they
stick to the-- excuse me?

01:47:42.880 --> 01:47:44.070
GEROVITCH: Asynchronous
executive--

01:47:44.070 --> 01:47:45.750
PRESENTER 6: I think that
they had a mixed system.

01:47:45.750 --> 01:47:46.200
PRESENTER: It was a hybrid.

01:47:46.200 --> 01:47:46.620
Yeah.

01:47:46.620 --> 01:47:48.620
PRESENTER 6: It was some
sort of a mixed system.

01:47:48.620 --> 01:47:51.180
They had some hardware
constraints on their I/O

01:47:51.180 --> 01:47:53.580
processors, and the
synchronization of I/O

01:47:53.580 --> 01:47:56.850
with the computer that made it
impossible to have, I think,

01:47:56.850 --> 01:47:59.700
a purely asynchronous executive.

01:47:59.700 --> 01:48:04.530
So they had some real hardware
issues involved in it.

01:48:04.530 --> 01:48:08.620
And we, and the company
that we formed after the--

01:48:08.620 --> 01:48:11.850
we created a programming
system for shuttle,

01:48:11.850 --> 01:48:15.360
in which we built into
the programming language

01:48:15.360 --> 01:48:18.150
the concept of asynchronicity.

01:48:18.150 --> 01:48:23.280
That is, that you could have
multiple jobs and priorities

01:48:23.280 --> 01:48:25.110
and interrupts and
things like that.

01:48:25.110 --> 01:48:27.930
And the shuttle people,
for the most part,

01:48:27.930 --> 01:48:29.981
didn't want to use
those features that we

01:48:29.981 --> 01:48:30.980
had put in the language.

01:48:30.980 --> 01:48:32.760
We were very influenced
in the language

01:48:32.760 --> 01:48:37.500
by the experience in
Apollo, and very influenced

01:48:37.500 --> 01:48:41.690
by the executive that
Hal Laning had designed,

01:48:41.690 --> 01:48:45.510
and the manner in which he
had designed that whole thing.

01:48:45.510 --> 01:48:48.820
We were very influenced,
in the language design,

01:48:48.820 --> 01:48:51.420
to take advantage
of those things,

01:48:51.420 --> 01:48:53.070
of which they were,
I would say, used

01:48:53.070 --> 01:48:57.500
to a minor extent in the
manner in which the shuttle was

01:48:57.500 --> 01:48:58.220
put together.

01:49:03.974 --> 01:49:06.390
GEROVITCH: Maybe we'd make a
little break for five minutes

01:49:06.390 --> 01:49:07.890
while they're
changing the tape now.

01:49:07.890 --> 01:49:09.820
PRESENTER 6: OK.

01:49:09.820 --> 01:49:12.460
GEROVITCH: And then we'll
continue for about one hour.

01:49:12.460 --> 01:49:13.715
Because we started at 10:00.

01:49:13.715 --> 01:49:14.920
PRESENTER 6: That's fine.

01:49:14.920 --> 01:49:16.734
PRESENTER: That's fine.

01:49:16.734 --> 01:49:18.400
PRESENTER 4: All the
time on our Apollo,

01:49:18.400 --> 01:49:21.870
we had just about no
contacts to the military.

01:49:21.870 --> 01:49:23.860
The military has its
own-- were worrying

01:49:23.860 --> 01:49:26.720
about military
satellites and so on.

01:49:26.720 --> 01:49:30.100
But our paths just didn't cross.

01:49:30.100 --> 01:49:33.010
I don't know if shuttle is where
they finally came together,

01:49:33.010 --> 01:49:34.720
but not on Apollo.

01:49:34.720 --> 01:49:36.970
There were a big difference
between the people

01:49:36.970 --> 01:49:40.270
we dealt with in Houston
and most of NASA and Langley

01:49:40.270 --> 01:49:45.100
and the old people, versus
Huntsville, Wernher Von

01:49:45.100 --> 01:49:47.770
Braun and the German
gangs down there.

01:49:47.770 --> 01:49:51.070
They did things a
little bit differently.

01:49:51.070 --> 01:49:54.860
And so there was a lot
of difference of opinion.

01:49:54.860 --> 01:49:59.600
I mean, they built things in
a certain way, which was very,

01:49:59.600 --> 01:50:02.535
very solid, [? methodical. ?]

01:50:02.535 --> 01:50:03.910
PRESENTER 6: With
all due respect

01:50:03.910 --> 01:50:09.130
to Jim's time in
the service, I think

01:50:09.130 --> 01:50:14.140
there was an actual disdain for
the military in this program.

01:50:14.140 --> 01:50:18.976
I think that you were afraid
of the military getting

01:50:18.976 --> 01:50:23.890
some sort of influence on
the program, in some fashion.

01:50:23.890 --> 01:50:27.160
And you were very happy that
the program was completely open,

01:50:27.160 --> 01:50:29.620
and it was open to the
public, and it was--

01:50:29.620 --> 01:50:32.096
you could talk about it, and--

01:50:32.096 --> 01:50:33.970
PRESENTER 5: Well, there
were certain aspects

01:50:33.970 --> 01:50:36.730
that still had the stamp
of the military on them.

01:50:36.730 --> 01:50:39.880
Some of those inertial
instruments and stuff

01:50:39.880 --> 01:50:42.222
were still classified
documents, if I remember right,

01:50:42.222 --> 01:50:42.930
at the beginning.

01:50:42.930 --> 01:50:44.680
PRESENTER 6: Is that right?

01:50:44.680 --> 01:50:46.480
PRESENTER 5: Trying
to get some data.

01:50:46.480 --> 01:50:49.610
Because they sprang
from Polaris and things.

01:50:49.610 --> 01:50:52.312
PRESENTER 7: Were the computer
instructions classified?

01:50:52.312 --> 01:50:53.770
PRESENTER 5: Not
that I recall, no.

01:50:53.770 --> 01:50:55.353
PRESENTER 2: The
only thing I remember

01:50:55.353 --> 01:50:57.070
being classified
about the computer

01:50:57.070 --> 01:50:58.932
was the oscillator frequency.

01:50:58.932 --> 01:51:00.640
PRESENTER 7: Because
I wasn't able to get

01:51:00.640 --> 01:51:04.740
a hold of the [? Block ?]
1 instructions because

01:51:04.740 --> 01:51:07.500
of some reason.

01:51:07.500 --> 01:51:11.590
I thought it had to do with
being classified, but--

01:51:11.590 --> 01:51:13.564
when I first came to learn.

01:51:13.564 --> 01:51:14.980
PRESENTER 1: I
think ossified is--

01:51:14.980 --> 01:51:15.710
PRESENTER 7: Was it ossified?

01:51:15.710 --> 01:51:16.626
PRESENTER 1: Ossified.

01:51:16.626 --> 01:51:17.230
[LAUGHTER]

01:51:18.065 --> 01:51:19.690
PRESENTER 7: Do you
remember that, Dan?

01:51:19.690 --> 01:51:21.790
You weren't able to
give me-- or Al--

01:51:21.790 --> 01:51:23.290
that you couldn't
give me a document

01:51:23.290 --> 01:51:25.149
because it was classified.

01:51:25.149 --> 01:51:26.940
PRESENTER 5: I don't
remember that exactly.

01:51:26.940 --> 01:51:27.773
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

01:51:27.773 --> 01:51:30.334
PRESENTER 5: I know there
were source documents that

01:51:30.334 --> 01:51:31.000
were classified.

01:51:31.000 --> 01:51:33.430
PRESENTER 7: So you
had to write down--

01:51:33.430 --> 01:51:36.640
I can remember you had to
write down a few instructions

01:51:36.640 --> 01:51:37.960
to say here they are.

01:51:37.960 --> 01:51:40.910
PRESENTER 6: But didn't we
have safes in that building?

01:51:40.910 --> 01:51:42.770
I remember-- I can
picture the safe.

01:51:42.770 --> 01:51:43.810
PRESENTER 5: Yeah.

01:51:43.810 --> 01:51:44.710
PRESENTER 6: I can't
remember what I kept in it.

01:51:44.710 --> 01:51:45.130
PRESENTER 4: What was in there?

01:51:45.130 --> 01:51:47.050
It was definitely
a classified area.

01:51:47.050 --> 01:51:47.710
Yeah.

01:51:47.710 --> 01:51:48.280
PRESENTER 7: Yeah.

01:51:48.280 --> 01:51:50.154
PRESENTER 5: Having to
do with the inertial--

01:51:50.154 --> 01:51:52.520
PRESENTER 6: I thought it
had to do with the rocket.

01:51:52.520 --> 01:51:53.830
PRESENTER 4: I don't know.

01:51:53.830 --> 01:51:56.540
PRESENTER 7: You can see how
much we were influenced by--

01:51:56.540 --> 01:51:58.060
GEROVITCH: Did you have
to obtain any security

01:51:58.060 --> 01:51:58.790
clearance or something?

01:51:58.790 --> 01:51:59.498
PRESENTER 6: Yes.

01:51:59.498 --> 01:52:01.070
We all had security clearances.

01:52:01.070 --> 01:52:01.850
PRESENTER 7: Yeah.

01:52:01.850 --> 01:52:02.640
PRESENTER 6: But I don't
know whether the security

01:52:02.640 --> 01:52:02.650
clearance--

01:52:02.650 --> 01:52:05.115
PRESENTER 4: Nothing we ever
wrote was classified, though.

01:52:05.115 --> 01:52:06.740
PRESENTER 5: It was
[? unclassified. ?]

01:52:06.740 --> 01:52:08.580
PRESENTER 6: My
security clearance

01:52:08.580 --> 01:52:10.805
was initiated in the Polaris--

01:52:10.805 --> 01:52:12.430
PRESENTER 2: That's
where it came from.

01:52:12.430 --> 01:52:12.520
Yeah.

01:52:12.520 --> 01:52:14.740
PRESENTER 6: I think it was kept
going from that [INAUDIBLE]..

01:52:14.740 --> 01:52:15.830
PRESENTER 7: But then
I had to get one.

01:52:15.830 --> 01:52:17.330
PRESENTER 6: Oh,
you had to get one.

01:52:17.330 --> 01:52:18.080
PRESENTER 7: Yeah.

01:52:18.080 --> 01:52:19.121
PRESENTER 5: Interesting.

01:52:19.121 --> 01:52:19.660
I had one.

01:52:19.660 --> 01:52:22.243
PRESENTER 7: Although, actually,
I had one in the Sage system,

01:52:22.243 --> 01:52:24.100
so maybe mine was left over.

01:52:24.100 --> 01:52:25.009
I don't know.

01:52:25.009 --> 01:52:26.800
GEROVITCH: And so you
didn't have any sense

01:52:26.800 --> 01:52:29.080
that the military
were interested at all

01:52:29.080 --> 01:52:31.545
in innovations that
went on in your project?

01:52:34.824 --> 01:52:35.490
PRESENTER 6: No.

01:52:35.490 --> 01:52:36.310
PRESENTER 7: We never
thought in terms of--

01:52:36.310 --> 01:52:37.050
PRESENTER 1: The
only military person

01:52:37.050 --> 01:52:39.230
I can associate with
Apollo is General Phillips,

01:52:39.230 --> 01:52:41.240
and he didn't exactly
come around looking over

01:52:41.240 --> 01:52:42.020
our shoulder.

01:52:42.020 --> 01:52:42.887
PRESENTER 4: No.

01:52:42.887 --> 01:52:43.970
PRESENTER 6: I don't know.

01:52:43.970 --> 01:52:45.470
I think that's a
little too strong.

01:52:45.470 --> 01:52:47.780
I don't know that we knew
that they weren't interested.

01:52:47.780 --> 01:52:50.240
They just were out of sight.

01:52:50.240 --> 01:52:51.194
They just weren't--

01:52:51.194 --> 01:52:52.860
PRESENTER 7: They
just weren't part of--

01:52:52.860 --> 01:52:54.359
GEROVITCH: I think
they just thought

01:52:54.359 --> 01:52:57.175
we were going to
totally flop, so why

01:52:57.175 --> 01:52:58.530
did they want to talk to us?

01:52:58.530 --> 01:52:59.426
[LAUGHTER]

01:53:00.530 --> 01:53:01.280
PRESENTER 5: Yeah.

01:53:01.280 --> 01:53:03.113
Going to the moon was
a political thing, not

01:53:03.113 --> 01:53:04.190
a military thing.

01:53:04.190 --> 01:53:04.700
So--

01:53:04.700 --> 01:53:05.120
PRESENTER 7: Yeah.

01:53:05.120 --> 01:53:06.290
PRESENTER 5: There
were no objectives

01:53:06.290 --> 01:53:07.581
of the military involved in it.

01:53:07.581 --> 01:53:09.050
They weren't going to the moon.

01:53:09.050 --> 01:53:09.830
PRESENTER 6: Well,
there may have been,

01:53:09.830 --> 01:53:10.996
but they didn't [INAUDIBLE].

01:53:10.996 --> 01:53:12.800
PRESENTER 7: It was
an [? eagle ?] thing.

01:53:12.800 --> 01:53:13.550
PRESENTER 5: Yeah.

01:53:13.550 --> 01:53:14.180
PRESENTER 7: I mean--

01:53:14.180 --> 01:53:15.290
PRESENTER 6: It might
have been an edict.

01:53:15.290 --> 01:53:17.665
It might have been an edict
from the President, you know?

01:53:17.665 --> 01:53:20.030
That this is an open
program, that this

01:53:20.030 --> 01:53:22.569
is a big political
show for the world.

01:53:22.569 --> 01:53:23.360
PRESENTER 7: Right.

01:53:23.360 --> 01:53:24.220
PRESENTER 6: We're going to
have everything [? open, ?]

01:53:24.220 --> 01:53:24.720
you know?

01:53:24.720 --> 01:53:27.540
PRESENTER 7: Because Sputnik
happened, so we had to go and--

01:53:27.540 --> 01:53:29.290
PRESENTER 6: Contrasted
with the Russians,

01:53:29.290 --> 01:53:31.070
they were in the
middle of Siberia,

01:53:31.070 --> 01:53:32.450
and nobody knows
what's going on.

01:53:32.450 --> 01:53:35.642
And here, this is
completely open.

01:53:35.642 --> 01:53:37.850
GEROVITCH: In Russia, there
was always kind of a rule

01:53:37.850 --> 01:53:43.640
that if a significant invention
was made that might potentially

01:53:43.640 --> 01:53:46.160
have military significance,
they were classify it,

01:53:46.160 --> 01:53:50.750
even if it was done within
some open program, initially.

01:53:50.750 --> 01:53:56.510
But this here doesn't seem
to be that kind of oversight,

01:53:56.510 --> 01:53:58.190
in the sense of
military searching

01:53:58.190 --> 01:54:01.100
for things that are going
on that might potentially

01:54:01.100 --> 01:54:02.175
be important.

01:54:02.175 --> 01:54:03.800
PRESENTER 6: Well,
there's a big danger

01:54:03.800 --> 01:54:06.940
in that because the
military, it seemed to me,

01:54:06.940 --> 01:54:10.310
and the people who were
in charge of security,

01:54:10.310 --> 01:54:14.600
had a ever-expanding horizon.

01:54:14.600 --> 01:54:17.180
I mean, you'd have
everything being classified

01:54:17.180 --> 01:54:21.770
because you couldn't tell
whether this little gizmo might

01:54:21.770 --> 01:54:23.870
have a military significance.

01:54:23.870 --> 01:54:26.409
So after a while, you'd wind
up with almost everything.

01:54:26.409 --> 01:54:28.450
GEROVITCH: That's what
the Russians [? wanted. ?]

01:54:28.450 --> 01:54:29.200
PRESENTER 6: Yeah.

01:54:29.200 --> 01:54:29.920
[LAUGHTER]

01:54:34.830 --> 01:54:36.900
GEROVITCH: So I'm
getting a sense

01:54:36.900 --> 01:54:40.140
that there was a
lot of interaction

01:54:40.140 --> 01:54:43.870
[? in ?] small groups while
working on those programs.

01:54:43.870 --> 01:54:47.520
Or did you have mostly one
program, one programmer?

01:54:47.520 --> 01:54:50.640
Or several programmers
working on one program?

01:54:50.640 --> 01:54:53.250
How kind of knowledge
about what--

01:54:53.250 --> 01:54:55.584
PRESENTER 7: Maybe four or
five on each functional area.

01:54:55.584 --> 01:54:57.000
PRESENTER 6: Well,
you had-- yeah.

01:54:57.000 --> 01:54:58.050
You had different areas.

01:54:58.050 --> 01:55:02.070
I mean, if somebody was
working a navigation module,

01:55:02.070 --> 01:55:04.800
they probably did not
have too much contact

01:55:04.800 --> 01:55:08.280
with some people who were
working in powered flight.

01:55:08.280 --> 01:55:12.420
So they were worried about
star sightings and covariance

01:55:12.420 --> 01:55:14.430
matrices, and
things having to do

01:55:14.430 --> 01:55:16.800
with navigation computations.

01:55:16.800 --> 01:55:19.410
And somebody else was
worried about auto pilots

01:55:19.410 --> 01:55:22.804
and how they were going
to fire engines and so on.

01:55:22.804 --> 01:55:24.220
And they were aware
of each other,

01:55:24.220 --> 01:55:28.340
but there may not be very
strong interfaces between them.

01:55:28.340 --> 01:55:31.540
And these two groups, I
would say they might be--

01:55:31.540 --> 01:55:36.160
you might have a powered flight
group of maybe six people,

01:55:36.160 --> 01:55:42.600
and you know, a navigation group
of some other number like that.

01:55:42.600 --> 01:55:44.220
And you had people
who were testing,

01:55:44.220 --> 01:55:48.430
and you had people who
were writing specs.

01:55:48.430 --> 01:55:52.024
And so I'd say you had a
lot of programming groups.

01:55:52.024 --> 01:55:53.940
And when you add it up,
you get to the numbers

01:55:53.940 --> 01:55:55.450
we're talking about.

01:55:55.450 --> 01:56:02.700
But each group was a
somewhat contained group.

01:56:02.700 --> 01:56:05.160
PRESENTER 1: There was a
parallel group of analysis guys

01:56:05.160 --> 01:56:07.500
that were writing back
programs all the time,

01:56:07.500 --> 01:56:09.360
and testing algorithms
and so forth.

01:56:09.360 --> 01:56:11.700
They weren't really writing
software for the [INAUDIBLE]

01:56:11.700 --> 01:56:11.950
computer.

01:56:11.950 --> 01:56:14.116
PRESENTER 5: Yeah, I was
going to make that comment.

01:56:14.116 --> 01:56:17.730
Because programmer was
not a job description.

01:56:17.730 --> 01:56:20.550
Some people programmed
and did analysis,

01:56:20.550 --> 01:56:23.060
and some people
only did analysis.

01:56:23.060 --> 01:56:24.990
Somebody did all their
work in [? Mac, ?]

01:56:24.990 --> 01:56:30.530
this analytical programming
language we had.

01:56:30.530 --> 01:56:33.710
I got the brunt of that
because I remember,

01:56:33.710 --> 01:56:36.870
in trying to put this
first flight program--

01:56:36.870 --> 01:56:40.560
of which I was the inaugural
rope mother-- together,

01:56:40.560 --> 01:56:42.780
I had to interface
with these groups

01:56:42.780 --> 01:56:48.230
and make their stuff meet
the stuff of the next group I

01:56:48.230 --> 01:56:52.980
remember putting the launch
people stuff, which was just

01:56:52.980 --> 01:56:56.790
plain analyzing dynamics and
finding out where we were,

01:56:56.790 --> 01:56:59.400
to the powered flight
people, who steered the thing

01:56:59.400 --> 01:57:01.466
and took over the
mission at that point.

01:57:01.466 --> 01:57:03.090
Then it went into
this midcourse thing,

01:57:03.090 --> 01:57:05.010
where some of these
star-sighting people,

01:57:05.010 --> 01:57:06.450
who were wacko analysts--

01:57:06.450 --> 01:57:08.640
I mean, I didn't
understand what--

01:57:08.640 --> 01:57:11.874
I still, to this day, don't
really understand some of that.

01:57:11.874 --> 01:57:13.290
That was a whole
different regime.

01:57:13.290 --> 01:57:16.870
Because here, 10 minutes was
a short time to these guys.

01:57:16.870 --> 01:57:22.650
Whereas one second was a lot
to the powered flight people.

01:57:22.650 --> 01:57:27.070
And then, I survived going
through that midcourse stuff.

01:57:27.070 --> 01:57:28.410
Got all their stuff straight.

01:57:28.410 --> 01:57:32.220
Was able to, then, power up this
guy, who [? was ?] the entry.

01:57:32.220 --> 01:57:33.630
He'd been working [INAUDIBLE].

01:57:33.630 --> 01:57:36.300
And I remember the
first day at simulation,

01:57:36.300 --> 01:57:37.830
knitted these two
phases together,

01:57:37.830 --> 01:57:41.850
and we got a coordinated--
so these guys' program--

01:57:41.850 --> 01:57:45.120
Dan and Ray's program,
and Fred and company,

01:57:45.120 --> 01:57:47.640
and some of the
other-- and all my glue

01:57:47.640 --> 01:57:51.550
that I had hung this thing
together with actually worked.

01:57:51.550 --> 01:57:54.480
But there were a lot of
different programming styles

01:57:54.480 --> 01:57:57.600
and conventions that you have
to straighten out, you know?

01:57:57.600 --> 01:58:01.207
These guys expected
the delta V's in some--

01:58:01.207 --> 01:58:02.540
we were giving it in the wrong--

01:58:02.540 --> 01:58:05.260
you have to-- you know,
this kind of thing.

01:58:05.260 --> 01:58:09.199
So putting these cells together
was an integration job.

01:58:09.199 --> 01:58:10.740
We didn't integrate
at the beginning.

01:58:10.740 --> 01:58:14.100
It was not a programming
exercise for the whole mission.

01:58:14.100 --> 01:58:15.630
There were these
analytical groups,

01:58:15.630 --> 01:58:18.860
some who did programming,
some who did different styles.

01:58:18.860 --> 01:58:19.860
There were no standards.

01:58:22.650 --> 01:58:27.480
All that received a lot more
discipline and method later.

01:58:27.480 --> 01:58:31.050
PRESENTER 6: [INAUDIBLE]
[? model ?] that you had--

01:58:31.050 --> 01:58:34.890
that we didn't have more
interface-type errors.

01:58:34.890 --> 01:58:37.900
Because we did
have no standards.

01:58:37.900 --> 01:58:40.260
We had no programming standards.

01:58:40.260 --> 01:58:46.680
Each group, or each little
entity, would have a style.

01:58:46.680 --> 01:58:49.620
I think, when we got
this project management,

01:58:49.620 --> 01:58:53.520
I did try, to some extent,
to get some standardization,

01:58:53.520 --> 01:58:55.860
but it was hard.

01:58:55.860 --> 01:59:00.220
I think people used different
expressions for constants

01:59:00.220 --> 01:59:02.405
in their programs.

01:59:02.405 --> 01:59:04.280
PRESENTER 1: But don't
forget Don [? Boler ?]

01:59:04.280 --> 01:59:07.630
and the stuff he did with ICDs.

01:59:07.630 --> 01:59:11.730
If we hadn't had a function
like that, with all of its specs

01:59:11.730 --> 01:59:15.220
from all of [? those ?] other
vendors, as well as our own,

01:59:15.220 --> 01:59:16.910
we would have really
been up a creek.

01:59:16.910 --> 01:59:17.982
And that was absolutely--

01:59:17.982 --> 01:59:19.065
PRESENTER 5: Who was that?

01:59:19.065 --> 01:59:19.500
PRESENTER 1: Don [? Boler. ?]

01:59:19.500 --> 01:59:20.458
PRESENTER 6: Don Boler.

01:59:20.458 --> 01:59:21.690
PRESENTER 5: Don Boler.

01:59:21.690 --> 01:59:22.970
Big Don.

01:59:22.970 --> 01:59:25.630
Another guy I remember, even
bigger, Peter [? Peck-- ?]

01:59:25.630 --> 01:59:27.360
PRESENTER 1: Yeah,
he was a big guy.

01:59:27.360 --> 01:59:30.330
PRESENTER 5: --who
instituted control

01:59:30.330 --> 01:59:31.980
over revisions and stuff.

01:59:31.980 --> 01:59:35.190
I don't know if that group
that you said that took over--

01:59:35.190 --> 01:59:37.202
wasn't that Peter's group?

01:59:37.202 --> 01:59:38.910
PRESENTER 4: Well, he
started it earlier,

01:59:38.910 --> 01:59:39.600
[? when ?] he did it all along.

01:59:39.600 --> 01:59:40.350
PRESENTER 5: But this
came out of nowhere.

01:59:40.350 --> 01:59:43.016
He thought this needed to be
done, and he started doing it.

01:59:43.016 --> 01:59:45.390
I mean, there was no "you are
the configuration manager."

01:59:45.390 --> 01:59:46.806
PRESENTER 7: Oh,
Peter [? Peck. ?]

01:59:46.806 --> 01:59:48.723
PRESENTER 5: Peter started
that kind of stuff.

01:59:48.723 --> 01:59:49.930
He'd come and argue with you.

01:59:49.930 --> 01:59:51.890
You couldn't put changes
in that, [INAUDIBLE]..

01:59:51.890 --> 01:59:52.723
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

01:59:52.723 --> 01:59:53.630
[LAUGHTER]

01:59:54.200 --> 01:59:55.080
PRESENTER 5: Yes.

01:59:55.080 --> 01:59:56.485
He was a very large person.

01:59:56.485 --> 01:59:57.354
[LAUGHTER]

01:59:57.354 --> 01:59:59.270
PRESENTER 7: You don't
want him to sit on you.

01:59:59.270 --> 02:00:01.380
PRESENTER: I know what this is.

02:00:01.380 --> 02:00:05.590
PRESENTER 5: But we invented
that in the course of the work.

02:00:05.590 --> 02:00:08.100
PRESENTER 1: There certainly
were integration problems

02:00:08.100 --> 02:00:11.540
outside our building.

02:00:11.540 --> 02:00:14.760
And I think that our experiences
within the building--

02:00:14.760 --> 02:00:19.760
and we were all within a
building which wasn't that big

02:00:19.760 --> 02:00:25.200
of a building-- were
relatively well-controlled.

02:00:25.200 --> 02:00:28.470
But some of the interface
problems we had that

02:00:28.470 --> 02:00:32.580
were experienced between our
stuff and other people's stuff,

02:00:32.580 --> 02:00:35.100
and between various
other people's stuff--

02:00:35.100 --> 02:00:38.130
I remember, for example--

02:00:38.130 --> 02:00:41.610
was it 501, Dan, or 502 where
there was an engine failure,

02:00:41.610 --> 02:00:45.150
and the software-- it had
nothing to do with us--

02:00:45.150 --> 02:00:47.352
shut down another
engine, thinking

02:00:47.352 --> 02:00:49.560
that they were shutting down
the one that had failed.

02:00:49.560 --> 02:00:52.189
And so it was down two
engines out of five.

02:00:52.189 --> 02:00:52.980
PRESENTER 4: Right.

02:00:52.980 --> 02:00:58.170
We ended up in orbit,
and they were supposed

02:00:58.170 --> 02:01:01.050
to do a translunar injection.

02:01:01.050 --> 02:01:02.940
But because of the
failure, and they

02:01:02.940 --> 02:01:07.590
didn't have a [? S4B ?] left,
or whatever it was, we ended up,

02:01:07.590 --> 02:01:12.030
instead of on a trajectory
headed towards a translunar,

02:01:12.030 --> 02:01:15.870
we ended up either in orbit
or an elliptical thing.

02:01:15.870 --> 02:01:20.610
So instead of doing a burn to
knock us out of a translunar

02:01:20.610 --> 02:01:24.090
trajectory, we burned
first to put us in,

02:01:24.090 --> 02:01:25.860
then we turned around
and knocked us out.

02:01:25.860 --> 02:01:27.000
PRESENTER 1: That
was one example

02:01:27.000 --> 02:01:28.530
of one of these
integration problems

02:01:28.530 --> 02:01:29.980
that woke up a lot of people.

02:01:29.980 --> 02:01:33.360
I think the accident
on the pad was probably

02:01:33.360 --> 02:01:37.740
the single biggest disaster
that woke people up and stopped

02:01:37.740 --> 02:01:40.860
the project for, what, a
year and a half or something.

02:01:40.860 --> 02:01:43.530
Investigations were done,
and block tub was invented.

02:01:43.530 --> 02:01:46.380
They hustled into place.

02:01:46.380 --> 02:01:48.570
The mission that I
was involved with,

02:01:48.570 --> 02:01:52.470
which was subsequently
called Apollo 5, which

02:01:52.470 --> 02:01:55.740
we called Apollo 2-- or
didn't call Apollo 206,

02:01:55.740 --> 02:01:57.480
it was just 206.

02:01:57.480 --> 02:02:00.045
First flight of the LEM,
which is unmanned and--

02:02:00.045 --> 02:02:00.930
PRESENTER 6: This is
the flight I think

02:02:00.930 --> 02:02:02.020
I mentioned the last time.

02:02:02.020 --> 02:02:03.600
Is this the one that only--

02:02:03.600 --> 02:02:06.210
it was at the Cape,
and the engine shut off

02:02:06.210 --> 02:02:07.800
in two seconds or four seconds?

02:02:07.800 --> 02:02:09.990
PRESENTER 1: Well, I'll
mention a little bit

02:02:09.990 --> 02:02:15.260
about what happened because
I think it was revealing.

02:02:15.260 --> 02:02:17.490
We'd never flown a
Grumman spacecraft before,

02:02:17.490 --> 02:02:19.110
and I'd never guided one.

02:02:19.110 --> 02:02:21.210
We'd never flown a block
tube system before.

02:02:21.210 --> 02:02:24.010
We'd never flown a
digital autopilot before.

02:02:24.010 --> 02:02:28.740
So there was a lot of new stuff,
and its rope mother was new.

02:02:28.740 --> 02:02:33.210
And we had a lot of veterans
and a lot of other people

02:02:33.210 --> 02:02:34.470
that were new.

02:02:34.470 --> 02:02:36.750
And our relations
with Grumman were

02:02:36.750 --> 02:02:40.550
different than the relations
with North America.

02:02:40.550 --> 02:02:41.550
PRESENTER 4: Were they?

02:02:41.550 --> 02:02:43.538
[? Hoag ?] [? talked ?]
last time about it.

02:02:46.290 --> 02:02:49.050
PRESENTER 1: It was just a
whole different ballgame.

02:02:49.050 --> 02:02:52.590
And I, for one, had never had
any contact with the mission

02:02:52.590 --> 02:02:56.730
control people, and didn't
have much as it was.

02:02:56.730 --> 02:02:59.940
But I went down there to one
flight procedures meeting.

02:02:59.940 --> 02:03:01.650
It was run by the
guy who was going

02:03:01.650 --> 02:03:03.350
to be the flight director.

02:03:03.350 --> 02:03:05.930
And let me not to
mention his name.

02:03:05.930 --> 02:03:09.820
But it was a terrible
meeting, I thought.

02:03:09.820 --> 02:03:14.340
The guy was so arrogant and
obnoxious that people just

02:03:14.340 --> 02:03:17.310
stayed quiet who probably should
have asked questions or spoken

02:03:17.310 --> 02:03:18.180
up.

02:03:18.180 --> 02:03:22.650
But in the event
of the flight, we

02:03:22.650 --> 02:03:24.780
had the first burn scheduled.

02:03:24.780 --> 02:03:27.630
And although we'd
simulated it lots

02:03:27.630 --> 02:03:32.550
of times, when the command
went to the engine,

02:03:32.550 --> 02:03:36.510
unknown to me, as the
mission program guy

02:03:36.510 --> 02:03:39.900
and as the simulation
guy, there was

02:03:39.900 --> 02:03:42.000
a lot of pressurization
of tanks and stuff

02:03:42.000 --> 02:03:45.030
that took place
between the fire signal

02:03:45.030 --> 02:03:47.940
and when the engine
actually lit up.

02:03:47.940 --> 02:03:50.550
And I had been told
by the guy that

02:03:50.550 --> 02:03:56.100
wrote the descent
burn software, that it

02:03:56.100 --> 02:03:59.254
had to have a very narrow
window on the start-up because

02:03:59.254 --> 02:04:00.170
of something or other.

02:04:00.170 --> 02:04:01.086
I don't remember what.

02:04:01.086 --> 02:04:03.900
But we had a fairly
narrow window in there.

02:04:03.900 --> 02:04:07.170
And it was-- the
timing of the interrupt

02:04:07.170 --> 02:04:12.450
and so forth, it just missed
reaching the delta V threshold

02:04:12.450 --> 02:04:15.240
by milliseconds, if I remember.

02:04:15.240 --> 02:04:16.630
And so we shut it down.

02:04:16.630 --> 02:04:17.880
The software shut it down.

02:04:17.880 --> 02:04:23.640
And utter chaos took place
in mission control center.

02:04:23.640 --> 02:04:25.530
Everybody was climbing
all over everybody

02:04:25.530 --> 02:04:29.400
to find out what happened,
totally preventing anybody

02:04:29.400 --> 02:04:31.200
from finding out what happened.

02:04:31.200 --> 02:04:34.900
Because managers were
insisting on knowing

02:04:34.900 --> 02:04:36.200
stuff that you didn't know yet.

02:04:36.200 --> 02:04:42.640
And it was bad
procedure, and I don't

02:04:42.640 --> 02:04:44.530
know if that continued
on other flights,

02:04:44.530 --> 02:04:46.600
but it certainly
screwed up this one.

02:04:46.600 --> 02:04:48.360
PRESENTER 6: Were
you in Houston then?

02:04:48.360 --> 02:04:49.110
PRESENTER 1: Yeah.

02:04:49.110 --> 02:04:49.400
PRESENTER 6: All right.

02:04:49.400 --> 02:04:49.970
This is the--

02:04:49.970 --> 02:04:50.710
PRESENTER 1: But let me go on.

02:04:50.710 --> 02:04:50.920
PRESENTER 6: Go ahead.

02:04:50.920 --> 02:04:51.640
I'm sorry.

02:04:51.640 --> 02:04:53.500
PRESENTER 1: We
had put in there,

02:04:53.500 --> 02:04:56.830
as a part of the
mission software, a very

02:04:56.830 --> 02:04:59.920
clever and fairly
extensive ability

02:04:59.920 --> 02:05:03.550
to reprogram all of these burns.

02:05:03.550 --> 02:05:08.380
And when we-- through the
hierarchy of Jack Garmin,

02:05:08.380 --> 02:05:10.990
up to the flight director--

02:05:10.990 --> 02:05:15.070
told them that we could
easily reprogram this thing

02:05:15.070 --> 02:05:17.800
and try it again,
the answer was,

02:05:17.800 --> 02:05:21.970
it's no longer within
telemetry coverage.

02:05:21.970 --> 02:05:25.720
Somehow, they had designed this
mission, unbeknownst to us,

02:05:25.720 --> 02:05:27.800
in a way that there
was no possible way

02:05:27.800 --> 02:05:31.900
to use this reprogramming
stuff if anything went wrong.

02:05:31.900 --> 02:05:33.610
[? Of ?] course,
was the only purpose

02:05:33.610 --> 02:05:35.470
for which this
reprogramming software

02:05:35.470 --> 02:05:37.870
was in the flight computer.

02:05:37.870 --> 02:05:39.580
But there was no
telemetry coverage

02:05:39.580 --> 02:05:42.080
if the mission didn't
go exactly perfectly.

02:05:42.080 --> 02:05:44.080
And of course, there was
no need to reprogram it

02:05:44.080 --> 02:05:45.950
if that's what happened.

02:05:45.950 --> 02:05:48.520
So here we were, out
of telemetry coverage.

02:05:48.520 --> 02:05:55.800
And then, they decided to use
the LEM abort guidance system--

02:05:55.800 --> 02:05:58.810
[? AGGS-- ?] to do
that burn, and drop

02:05:58.810 --> 02:06:01.250
the thing without any knowledge
of the guidance software.

02:06:01.250 --> 02:06:03.346
It took control away from
the guidance software,

02:06:03.346 --> 02:06:04.970
gave it to the onboard
[? guy at the ?]

02:06:04.970 --> 02:06:09.520
LEM abort guidance system,
which fired the descent stage,

02:06:09.520 --> 02:06:13.150
separated and did an
ascent stage burn.

02:06:13.150 --> 02:06:17.230
And then, they handed
the control back

02:06:17.230 --> 02:06:19.030
to the guidance system.

02:06:19.030 --> 02:06:23.320
Well, nobody had ever planned or
thought that that would happen.

02:06:23.320 --> 02:06:25.300
And the guidance system on--

02:06:25.300 --> 02:06:27.400
our guidance system
on the LEM knew

02:06:27.400 --> 02:06:29.290
that it was in the
descent configuration.

02:06:29.290 --> 02:06:31.540
By then, the descent
stage had been thrown off.

02:06:31.540 --> 02:06:35.680
And so the digital autopilot
had this little sporty vehicle

02:06:35.680 --> 02:06:39.310
when it thought it was
controlling this massive thing,

02:06:39.310 --> 02:06:41.590
and so it was doing
horrible limit cycles

02:06:41.590 --> 02:06:45.010
and burning fuel
like nobody ever saw.

02:06:45.010 --> 02:06:46.520
And they said, what's going on?

02:06:46.520 --> 02:06:49.240
Well, I knew right
away what was going on.

02:06:49.240 --> 02:06:51.894
Nobody, of course,
had asked MIT anything

02:06:51.894 --> 02:06:53.060
when all this was happening.

02:06:53.060 --> 02:06:57.280
They just knew better
and took over, of course.

02:06:57.280 --> 02:07:00.220
I thought they
didn't know better.

02:07:00.220 --> 02:07:03.190
But I, again, sent
the information up

02:07:03.190 --> 02:07:06.770
that if they do this heat soak
thing that we had put in there,

02:07:06.770 --> 02:07:10.930
that the first thing it
did was to check agreement

02:07:10.930 --> 02:07:17.290
between the bits that indicated
what stages were present

02:07:17.290 --> 02:07:19.840
and the configuration the
guidance system thought.

02:07:19.840 --> 02:07:21.494
And if it found a
mismatch, it would

02:07:21.494 --> 02:07:23.410
correct the situation
for the guidance system,

02:07:23.410 --> 02:07:25.820
and then pop out
of this program.

02:07:25.820 --> 02:07:27.460
So it was a way to
get the thing back

02:07:27.460 --> 02:07:30.400
in sync with one uplink command.

02:07:32.920 --> 02:07:35.110
When this was made known
to the flight director,

02:07:35.110 --> 02:07:36.710
he said, we can't
do that program.

02:07:36.710 --> 02:07:39.240
We've never run it in
our practice simulations

02:07:39.240 --> 02:07:40.240
here at mission control.

02:07:40.240 --> 02:07:42.040
[LAUGHTER]

02:07:42.040 --> 02:07:44.950
And here was this thing that
we'd spent a lot of time

02:07:44.950 --> 02:07:47.320
putting in the-- it was the
second thing in the flight

02:07:47.320 --> 02:07:49.480
software that couldn't,
or wouldn't, be used

02:07:49.480 --> 02:07:52.340
by the mission control people.

02:07:52.340 --> 02:07:56.980
Well, the result was not good,
and we took a lot of flak

02:07:56.980 --> 02:07:59.500
for that.

02:07:59.500 --> 02:08:00.970
I think a lot of it undeserved.

02:08:00.970 --> 02:08:04.740
But I think it really shook up
the mission control people, who

02:08:04.740 --> 02:08:08.890
realized that their
abilities to handle things

02:08:08.890 --> 02:08:10.690
was much lower
than they thought.

02:08:10.690 --> 02:08:13.180
And their ability to
understand and deal

02:08:13.180 --> 02:08:16.750
with the possibilities of what
would go wrong in the flight

02:08:16.750 --> 02:08:19.570
computer, and in the
system other within that,

02:08:19.570 --> 02:08:21.460
was completely undeveloped.

02:08:21.460 --> 02:08:24.020
And I think they went
through a lot of learning,

02:08:24.020 --> 02:08:27.430
and made a lot of changes that
led to the success in things

02:08:27.430 --> 02:08:30.130
like the lunar landing, where
something really unexpected

02:08:30.130 --> 02:08:31.180
came up.

02:08:31.180 --> 02:08:34.210
And there were people there
who were confident enough,

02:08:34.210 --> 02:08:35.230
or foolish enough--

02:08:35.230 --> 02:08:36.250
[LAUGHTER]

02:08:36.250 --> 02:08:38.780
--to say, go, whatever it was.

02:08:38.780 --> 02:08:40.360
But it worked.

02:08:40.360 --> 02:08:44.410
But without some of these
blunders that happened,

02:08:44.410 --> 02:08:47.720
I think there would have
been a much worse outcome,

02:08:47.720 --> 02:08:48.490
or could've.

02:08:48.490 --> 02:08:49.698
PRESENTER 6: Let me ask you--

02:08:49.698 --> 02:08:52.180
did you-- did the clever
things that you had put in,

02:08:52.180 --> 02:08:54.310
were they known to
the mission controls?

02:08:54.310 --> 02:08:55.540
PRESENTER 1: They were
[? in the ?] software.

02:08:55.540 --> 02:08:56.623
They were in the software.

02:08:56.623 --> 02:08:57.900
They had tested all its--

02:08:57.900 --> 02:09:01.100
no, our contacts knew
that they were in there.

02:09:01.100 --> 02:09:03.262
It wasn't our job, and
we didn't have time

02:09:03.262 --> 02:09:04.720
to do anything that
wasn't our job.

02:09:04.720 --> 02:09:06.580
We didn't have time to do
things that were our job.

02:09:06.580 --> 02:09:08.680
I was working 80 hours
a week during that time.

02:09:08.680 --> 02:09:09.040
PRESENTER 6: Sure.

02:09:09.040 --> 02:09:11.623
PRESENTER 1: [? Aaron ?] Cohen
kept saying, go down to Grumman

02:09:11.623 --> 02:09:12.520
and talk to them.

02:09:12.520 --> 02:09:14.210
When?

02:09:14.210 --> 02:09:16.065
Who's going to do the
configuration control?

02:09:16.065 --> 02:09:18.190
I would stay there till 8
or 9 o'clock every night,

02:09:18.190 --> 02:09:20.470
looking at the
changes to the listing

02:09:20.470 --> 02:09:23.500
to make sure that the
assembly was successful.

02:09:23.500 --> 02:09:24.281
Usually, it was.

02:09:24.281 --> 02:09:25.780
I didn't have time
to go to Grumman.

02:09:25.780 --> 02:09:28.060
I would've stopped--
how many people were

02:09:28.060 --> 02:09:29.960
working in putting restarts in?

02:09:33.440 --> 02:09:36.780
But we ran out--

02:09:36.780 --> 02:09:38.530
PRESENTER 6: But when
you were down there,

02:09:38.530 --> 02:09:42.690
that was one of the
only launches I went to.

02:09:42.690 --> 02:09:45.630
And I was at the
Cape at that time.

02:09:45.630 --> 02:09:50.750
And when that happened, I was
telling the group here last

02:09:50.750 --> 02:09:54.230
time, that there was a lot of
finger-pointing that occurred

02:09:54.230 --> 02:09:57.200
on this narrow window
you were talking about,

02:09:57.200 --> 02:10:02.180
as to whether this was a
programming error or whether--

02:10:02.180 --> 02:10:06.665
we were programming
exactly what the engine--

02:10:06.665 --> 02:10:12.050
we were programming that
to the characteristics

02:10:12.050 --> 02:10:16.887
of the engine that were
given to us from Grumman.

02:10:16.887 --> 02:10:18.470
And there was a lot
of finger-pointing

02:10:18.470 --> 02:10:21.050
as to whose fault it was.

02:10:21.050 --> 02:10:23.480
And of course, along
came Doc Draper,

02:10:23.480 --> 02:10:24.770
who was down at the Cape.

02:10:24.770 --> 02:10:27.510
He'd like to know
what was going on.

02:10:27.510 --> 02:10:32.360
So there was this picture
of this madhouse room

02:10:32.360 --> 02:10:37.520
with a listing this big,
opened up to page 179,

02:10:37.520 --> 02:10:38.330
or whatever it is.

02:10:38.330 --> 02:10:40.940
And somebody sticking
Doc Draper's nose

02:10:40.940 --> 02:10:44.180
into these various
assembly language things,

02:10:44.180 --> 02:10:47.870
trying to talk about how
this engine was cut off.

02:10:47.870 --> 02:10:50.640
PRESENTER 1: Well, that
points out something else

02:10:50.640 --> 02:10:54.530
that I didn't think of, but
I think is worth saying.

02:10:54.530 --> 02:10:58.440
I don't know if 206 was the
first mission of this sort,

02:10:58.440 --> 02:11:01.050
but it was a first--

02:11:01.050 --> 02:11:02.240
it may have been--

02:11:02.240 --> 02:11:06.250
where Houston had told us
that they, not we, were going

02:11:06.250 --> 02:11:09.080
to be responsible for what
we call the erasable load,

02:11:09.080 --> 02:11:10.940
which was the stuff
that was preloaded

02:11:10.940 --> 02:11:15.590
into the erasable
memory before launch.

02:11:15.590 --> 02:11:17.900
So the responsibility
for the erasable load

02:11:17.900 --> 02:11:23.700
was ostensibly with the
guys down in Houston.

02:11:23.700 --> 02:11:25.220
Well, that was fine.

02:11:25.220 --> 02:11:27.830
But for the simulator, we
had to have a lot of stuff

02:11:27.830 --> 02:11:29.720
preloaded into erasable memory.

02:11:29.720 --> 02:11:34.640
And there was this piece of
code called Mr. Clean, which

02:11:34.640 --> 02:11:39.080
would go clean out stuff and
preload all of these constants

02:11:39.080 --> 02:11:43.040
that we thought would be fine
for the erasable to start with.

02:11:43.040 --> 02:11:45.450
And people could override
those and so forth.

02:11:45.450 --> 02:11:48.430
But that's what that
thing did, if I remember.

02:11:48.430 --> 02:11:50.730
It was there for some reason.

02:11:50.730 --> 02:11:53.450
I remember it was a
popular commercial

02:11:53.450 --> 02:11:55.695
on the radio at the
time that happened

02:11:55.695 --> 02:11:57.820
to fit in eight letters,
and so that's what it got.

02:11:57.820 --> 02:11:59.200
[LAUGHTER]

02:12:01.200 --> 02:12:04.730
But it turned out, Houston never
touched the erasable, at least

02:12:04.730 --> 02:12:06.290
for 206.

02:12:06.290 --> 02:12:09.560
And so the stuff that we had in
there for the simulator, which

02:12:09.560 --> 02:12:12.950
always worked in the
simulator, and Grumman

02:12:12.950 --> 02:12:14.984
ran the simulations,
and we never heard--

02:12:14.984 --> 02:12:16.650
I never heard anything
from Grumman that

02:12:16.650 --> 02:12:18.930
said something's funny there.

02:12:18.930 --> 02:12:22.370
But there was another
one of these mismatches,

02:12:22.370 --> 02:12:26.000
where we never saw
what Houston might

02:12:26.000 --> 02:12:27.720
have done to the erasable load.

02:12:27.720 --> 02:12:29.750
It turns out they
didn't do anything.

02:12:29.750 --> 02:12:33.620
But there were these interface
gaps that weren't local,

02:12:33.620 --> 02:12:38.900
but there were between
geographic locations, that

02:12:38.900 --> 02:12:43.670
went wrong, and I think caused
people to shore up the defenses

02:12:43.670 --> 02:12:47.490
a little bit on missions
that came later.

02:12:47.490 --> 02:12:50.150
But without some
of those mishaps,

02:12:50.150 --> 02:12:53.150
I don't know where
we would've come out.

02:12:53.150 --> 02:12:56.360
But that was a tremendous
disappointment to all of us

02:12:56.360 --> 02:13:00.160
that busted our
tails on the 206.

02:13:00.160 --> 02:13:02.930
And it was never possible
to point a finger.

02:13:02.930 --> 02:13:04.800
I could never point
a finger at anybody.

02:13:04.800 --> 02:13:07.310
I remember exactly
where I was standing

02:13:07.310 --> 02:13:10.520
when the person who was
responsible for the dips burn--

02:13:10.520 --> 02:13:13.100
I'm not going to mention his
name, but everybody here,

02:13:13.100 --> 02:13:14.720
I think, knows who I mean--

02:13:14.720 --> 02:13:17.790
told me there were
restrictions on that thing.

02:13:17.790 --> 02:13:19.820
And so the number came
directly from him.

02:13:19.820 --> 02:13:22.610
I didn't make it up.

02:13:22.610 --> 02:13:27.320
And so it wasn't something
that we did just crazy.

02:13:27.320 --> 02:13:31.820
But mishaps helped us a lot.

02:13:31.820 --> 02:13:34.050
The accident, of course,
took three lives.

02:13:34.050 --> 02:13:37.550
It was way beyond our purview.

02:13:37.550 --> 02:13:38.865
But had the biggest effect.

02:13:38.865 --> 02:13:40.490
Because I think,
without that accident,

02:13:40.490 --> 02:13:42.401
we never would have
made the schedule,

02:13:42.401 --> 02:13:44.150
and maybe never would've
made the landing.

02:13:44.150 --> 02:13:46.149
PRESENTER 5: Something
else I remember about it,

02:13:46.149 --> 02:13:49.220
it was to [? have ?] [? flown ?]
the flight program 204?

02:13:49.220 --> 02:13:50.240
PRESENTER 1: Yeah.

02:13:50.240 --> 02:13:54.590
PRESENTER 5: That program,
which we were forced to release,

02:13:54.590 --> 02:13:57.229
was just so lousy, so
[? chocked full ?] of bugs.

02:13:57.229 --> 02:13:58.520
It would've killed them anyway.

02:13:58.520 --> 02:14:00.100
That's my feeling.

02:14:00.100 --> 02:14:01.120
It was so--

02:14:01.120 --> 02:14:02.170
PRESENTER: Woof.

02:14:02.170 --> 02:14:04.030
PRESENTER 5: It was
sort of a blessing,

02:14:04.030 --> 02:14:06.200
if you can call it that,
that that was ditched

02:14:06.200 --> 02:14:08.180
and was never used, and we
went into block [? tube. ?] But

02:14:08.180 --> 02:14:10.410
PRESENTER 1: It's an example of
how-- and I mentioned before,

02:14:10.410 --> 02:14:12.410
each program was supposed
to be an evolutionary step

02:14:12.410 --> 02:14:12.870
from the one before.

02:14:12.870 --> 02:14:13.670
PRESENTER 5: Yeah.

02:14:13.670 --> 02:14:14.630
PRESENTER 1: This
one had a crew on it.

02:14:14.630 --> 02:14:16.130
The one before it
had no crew on it.

02:14:16.130 --> 02:14:18.130
If you don't think that
was utterly different,

02:14:18.130 --> 02:14:19.889
it was utterly different.

02:14:19.889 --> 02:14:22.430
PRESENTER 5: And I remember, we
had six weeks to turn it out.

02:14:22.430 --> 02:14:24.280
PRESENTER 1: Yeah,
very short schedule.

02:14:24.280 --> 02:14:26.300
And of course, nobody
wanted to be the one that

02:14:26.300 --> 02:14:27.510
caused the schedule to slip.

02:14:27.510 --> 02:14:28.218
PRESENTER 5: Yep.

02:14:30.867 --> 02:14:32.450
PRESENTER 1: We were
confident Grumman

02:14:32.450 --> 02:14:33.830
would make the schedule slip.

02:14:33.830 --> 02:14:35.750
We just knew they'd
never make it.

02:14:35.750 --> 02:14:40.899
Of course, they did, which
caught a lot of us by surprise.

02:14:45.390 --> 02:14:48.110
A lot of good things
happened, too.

02:14:48.110 --> 02:14:51.820
I remember, when we
ordered the first 360,

02:14:51.820 --> 02:14:55.750
Lyndon Johnson had just ensured
that IBM got a sole-source

02:14:55.750 --> 02:14:59.440
contract to supply four
[? 36075's ?] to the

02:14:59.440 --> 02:15:01.390
[? manned ?] Spacecraft Center.

02:15:01.390 --> 02:15:04.510
And all the other vendors were
just completely up in arms.

02:15:04.510 --> 02:15:08.320
And IBM, who was the only bidder
that could do this, supposedly,

02:15:08.320 --> 02:15:10.450
had to supply a fifth
computer because the four

02:15:10.450 --> 02:15:12.940
computers didn't meet
the specs and so forth.

02:15:12.940 --> 02:15:15.110
It was a horrible situation.

02:15:15.110 --> 02:15:17.770
And I went to the
guy down there that I

02:15:17.770 --> 02:15:20.560
had to go to say we
needed a faster machine,

02:15:20.560 --> 02:15:24.070
and we want to put out a
request for a proposal.

02:15:24.070 --> 02:15:26.140
And got approval to do that.

02:15:26.140 --> 02:15:29.890
We got bids from at
least three vendors.

02:15:29.890 --> 02:15:33.400
And I called him up
and I said, well,

02:15:33.400 --> 02:15:35.142
we've done our
proposal evaluation.

02:15:35.142 --> 02:15:36.100
We're ready to proceed.

02:15:36.100 --> 02:15:37.016
Any problem with that?

02:15:37.016 --> 02:15:38.520
And he said, no, not any at all.

02:15:38.520 --> 02:15:42.140
He said, who is the
winner of your proposal?

02:15:42.140 --> 02:15:43.150
I said, IBM.

02:15:43.150 --> 02:15:44.620
He said, oh.

02:15:44.620 --> 02:15:48.445
He said, we're going to need a
lot of justification for that.

02:15:48.445 --> 02:15:50.980
So we spent about a
month writing down what

02:15:50.980 --> 02:15:53.800
we thought they wanted to hear.

02:15:53.800 --> 02:15:56.410
People's [? time-- ?] Cliff
[? Hyde ?] was a guy that

02:15:56.410 --> 02:16:01.360
[? wants ?] to sort of
[? saying ?] why IBM was

02:16:01.360 --> 02:16:04.000
the chosen vendor.

02:16:04.000 --> 02:16:06.730
So there were a certain
amount of politics in there

02:16:06.730 --> 02:16:09.160
that didn't have
anything to do with us.

02:16:09.160 --> 02:16:19.860
But other funny things happened.

02:16:19.860 --> 02:16:23.270
I don't know if this
is useful or not.

02:16:23.270 --> 02:16:27.480
Houston wanted to have an
independent computer simulation

02:16:27.480 --> 02:16:32.389
from the one we had,
as a cross-check.

02:16:32.389 --> 02:16:36.469
And so they assigned somebody
to write a simulator--

02:16:36.469 --> 02:16:42.559
and they wrote it in
Fortran, I think--

02:16:42.559 --> 02:16:47.090
of not the computer,
but some other stuff.

02:16:47.090 --> 02:16:51.709
And I had personally modified
our [? Mac ?] executive,

02:16:51.709 --> 02:16:56.389
so that some things that we
needed to do could be done

02:16:56.389 --> 02:16:59.450
by runtime software in the
[? Mac ?] executive that ran

02:16:59.450 --> 02:17:04.280
at runtime for [? Mac, ?]
that involved stealing some

02:17:04.280 --> 02:17:07.040
functions that I knew nobody, or
thought nobody would ever use.

02:17:07.040 --> 02:17:12.450
We had hyperbolic cosecant
function in the language

02:17:12.450 --> 02:17:15.000
that I didn't think
anybody had ever used.

02:17:15.000 --> 02:17:18.050
And so if you put hyperbolic
cosecant of minus 1

02:17:18.050 --> 02:17:21.040
in there or something, it would
type on the console typewriter

02:17:21.040 --> 02:17:22.250
or something.

02:17:22.250 --> 02:17:27.350
And I got a look at the listing
of their independent simulator

02:17:27.350 --> 02:17:28.700
written in Fortran.

02:17:28.700 --> 02:17:34.620
And right there, [INAUDIBLE]
hyperbolic cosecant of minus 1

02:17:34.620 --> 02:17:36.620
to write on the console
of a machine that

02:17:36.620 --> 02:17:40.059
wasn't even the one they used.

02:17:40.059 --> 02:17:43.925
And certainly, not what you'd
call an independent simulation.

02:17:43.925 --> 02:17:44.920
[LAUGHTER]

02:17:44.920 --> 02:17:48.320
[INAUDIBLE] this code
directly into Fortran

02:17:48.320 --> 02:17:51.600
without the slightest
idea of what it was doing.

02:17:51.600 --> 02:17:54.080
But here we are, with
a real cross-check.

02:18:01.070 --> 02:18:03.320
We did get there.

02:18:03.320 --> 02:18:04.730
I was going to say--

02:18:04.730 --> 02:18:07.040
I lost the thread.

02:18:07.040 --> 02:18:08.139
Senior moment.

02:18:08.139 --> 02:18:12.230
It happens almost
continually with me now.

02:18:12.230 --> 02:18:15.530
After having gotten this first
IBM machine against all odds,

02:18:15.530 --> 02:18:19.820
and getting it installed,
and stealing it from somebody

02:18:19.820 --> 02:18:21.200
that we were doing
business with.

02:18:21.200 --> 02:18:23.420
And George Miller called
them up and told them

02:18:23.420 --> 02:18:26.210
they were going to have
to lose their machine.

02:18:26.210 --> 02:18:28.670
And it got very ugly
because they got really--

02:18:28.670 --> 02:18:31.549
this company got
really angry with us

02:18:31.549 --> 02:18:34.670
for identifying them as
somebody who might give up

02:18:34.670 --> 02:18:37.330
a machine and so forth.

02:18:37.330 --> 02:18:41.570
It took us quite a long time
to get it in and installed.

02:18:41.570 --> 02:18:46.820
And we were running lots
of simulations on there,

02:18:46.820 --> 02:18:50.840
and a lot of other
lab stuff, as well,

02:18:50.840 --> 02:18:54.980
when we realized we just
needed more simulation time

02:18:54.980 --> 02:18:58.940
than there was on one
machine in one a week.

02:18:58.940 --> 02:19:01.400
So I went to Chris
[? Craft ?] and said,

02:19:01.400 --> 02:19:03.230
we've got to have
a second machine.

02:19:05.870 --> 02:19:09.230
It was absolutely astonishing.

02:19:09.230 --> 02:19:13.910
Within six weeks, that machine
was running in our place.

02:19:13.910 --> 02:19:16.430
Before, it had taken
probably 10 months.

02:19:16.430 --> 02:19:20.480
And he just was able
to make things happen

02:19:20.480 --> 02:19:23.600
for us, and for everybody else
in the project, that really

02:19:23.600 --> 02:19:25.500
gave it what it
needed to succeed.

02:19:25.500 --> 02:19:29.120
And I think without influences
like Chris [? Craft, ?] both

02:19:29.120 --> 02:19:32.270
inspirationally-- because
he really was a guy we all

02:19:32.270 --> 02:19:34.370
respected and liked--

02:19:34.370 --> 02:19:39.017
and without his clout, the
project would have not made it.

02:19:39.017 --> 02:19:41.600
There were just lots of things
that happened to come together.

02:19:41.600 --> 02:19:44.260
PRESENTER 6: By the way,
he's written a recent book.

02:19:44.260 --> 02:19:46.405
Has anybody read that?

02:19:46.405 --> 02:19:47.780
PRESENTER 4: I've
seen him on TV,

02:19:47.780 --> 02:19:50.060
and heard him on the
radio talking about it,

02:19:50.060 --> 02:19:52.460
but I haven't had a chance
to look at the book.

02:19:52.460 --> 02:19:55.190
PRESENTER 6: It's supposed
to be pretty good.

02:19:55.190 --> 02:19:56.630
PRESENTER 7: But on Apollo?

02:19:56.630 --> 02:20:00.270
PRESENTER 6: Yeah, and the
space program and issues, and--

02:20:00.270 --> 02:20:01.270
PRESENTER 4: Everything.

02:20:01.270 --> 02:20:02.200
PRESENTER 6: Very frank.

02:20:02.200 --> 02:20:03.800
PRESENTER 1: I also read,
in Jean [? Kranza's ?] book,

02:20:03.800 --> 02:20:06.170
that the volume of
Tindall grams was actually

02:20:06.170 --> 02:20:07.700
published and circulated.

02:20:07.700 --> 02:20:09.740
I've never seen that, either.

02:20:09.740 --> 02:20:14.170
PRESENTER 4: They have some here
because Malcolm sent his packet

02:20:14.170 --> 02:20:16.200
to you guys, right?

02:20:16.200 --> 02:20:18.150
I think he sent it--

02:20:18.150 --> 02:20:19.040
GEROVITCH: To Sandy.

02:20:19.040 --> 02:20:19.831
PRESENTER 4: Sandy.

02:20:19.831 --> 02:20:20.690
To Sandy.

02:20:20.690 --> 02:20:23.550
PRESENTER 1: Way
after [? words-- ?]

02:20:23.550 --> 02:20:26.090
it must have been
'91 or so, I saw

02:20:26.090 --> 02:20:28.370
Bill Tindall at
Intermetrics and urged him

02:20:28.370 --> 02:20:30.500
to publish his Tindall grams.

02:20:30.500 --> 02:20:34.610
And he maybe already had,
but he didn't say so.

02:20:34.610 --> 02:20:37.202
He said, why would anybody
want to read that stuff?

02:20:37.202 --> 02:20:40.080
And I tried to sell
him on the idea,

02:20:40.080 --> 02:20:43.340
but he didn't further
do it, as far as I know.

02:20:43.340 --> 02:20:45.150
But I did read that,
from the [? press, ?]

02:20:45.150 --> 02:20:46.648
that they were
circulated around.

02:20:46.648 --> 02:20:47.586
[INAUDIBLE]

02:20:51.860 --> 02:20:54.440
Another guy, without which
things would have gone

02:20:54.440 --> 02:20:55.890
far differently, and worse.

02:20:55.890 --> 02:20:56.681
PRESENTER 4: Right.

02:20:56.681 --> 02:21:00.380
Bill Tindall helps
solve so many--

02:21:00.380 --> 02:21:03.830
especially at
Houston down there.

02:21:03.830 --> 02:21:07.180
Drag them all together
and beat enough heads--

02:21:07.180 --> 02:21:12.410
pound enough heads
together to get agreement.

02:21:12.410 --> 02:21:15.440
It was amazing how he
did it without any--

02:21:15.440 --> 02:21:17.930
just by dint of his personality.

02:21:17.930 --> 02:21:22.190
I'm not sure he really was
the guy-- the boss of any

02:21:22.190 --> 02:21:22.880
of those people.

02:21:22.880 --> 02:21:27.220
But when he said
it, it happened.

02:21:27.220 --> 02:21:31.160
PRESENTER 5: Well, I remember,
before Bill, the ill-fated Dr.

02:21:31.160 --> 02:21:32.540
[? Joe ?] Shay.

02:21:32.540 --> 02:21:33.410
Remember that--

02:21:33.410 --> 02:21:34.326
PRESENTER 4: Poor Joe.

02:21:34.326 --> 02:21:36.454
PRESENTER 5: [? FACI ?]
or whatever we had there.

02:21:36.454 --> 02:21:37.370
PRESENTER 4: Joe got--

02:21:37.370 --> 02:21:38.736
PRESENTER 5: He got the axe.

02:21:38.736 --> 02:21:41.360
PRESENTER 4: Blamed for a lot of
things that weren't his fault,

02:21:41.360 --> 02:21:41.860
I thought.

02:21:41.860 --> 02:21:44.230
PRESENTER 5: But he had a
style about him in conducting.

02:21:44.230 --> 02:21:47.390
You remember those [? FACIs-- ?]
first article configuration

02:21:47.390 --> 02:21:48.730
inspection?

02:21:48.730 --> 02:21:51.980
It was Joe [? Shay ?] sitting
like this [INAUDIBLE] red

02:21:51.980 --> 02:21:54.020
[? socks ?] on his boots.

02:21:54.020 --> 02:21:57.370
He didn't-- he was a
rather unassuming guy.

02:21:57.370 --> 02:22:01.130
I was kind of fond of him,
and looked at him from afar.

02:22:01.130 --> 02:22:06.650
Then he got-- he was
the scapegoat for all--

02:22:06.650 --> 02:22:09.530
PRESENTER 6: You know, in the
later stages of the program,

02:22:09.530 --> 02:22:11.420
we talked a lot
about the personnel

02:22:11.420 --> 02:22:16.400
and the people at MIT, and
good people that we got,

02:22:16.400 --> 02:22:19.060
and so on, and the closeness.

02:22:19.060 --> 02:22:22.120
In the later stages
of the program,

02:22:22.120 --> 02:22:26.830
it was the determination that
we didn't have enough personnel.

02:22:26.830 --> 02:22:32.840
And that we needed to run this
more like an aerospace program,

02:22:32.840 --> 02:22:34.380
you might say.

02:22:34.380 --> 02:22:37.580
And so there was
actually an effort

02:22:37.580 --> 02:22:40.440
to bring in
contractor personnel.

02:22:40.440 --> 02:22:44.240
And I don't know how many were
brought in, but quite a few--

02:22:44.240 --> 02:22:46.020
a lot.

02:22:46.020 --> 02:22:50.430
And by and large, these
people were very good people.

02:22:50.430 --> 02:22:55.180
And they were integrated
into our efforts,

02:22:55.180 --> 02:22:58.610
but it was just
another example of how

02:22:58.610 --> 02:23:03.650
something that began relatively
small and close started to get,

02:23:03.650 --> 02:23:06.350
toward the end, look like
a lot of other programs,

02:23:06.350 --> 02:23:09.740
where you brought in people
under different badges

02:23:09.740 --> 02:23:12.830
and different
management, and so on.

02:23:12.830 --> 02:23:15.770
And then you also faced--

02:23:15.770 --> 02:23:19.220
tried to parcel
or proportion them

02:23:19.220 --> 02:23:22.370
in intelligent
ways, either testing

02:23:22.370 --> 02:23:26.810
or trying to create
tasks that they could

02:23:26.810 --> 02:23:30.080
do that were self-contained.

02:23:30.080 --> 02:23:32.210
But in many cases,
they were integrated

02:23:32.210 --> 02:23:37.850
into groups that [INAUDIBLE]
IT personnel, as well.

02:23:37.850 --> 02:23:41.270
I think that, by
and large, that we

02:23:41.270 --> 02:23:43.554
did that fairly successfully.

02:23:43.554 --> 02:23:45.470
PRESENTER 7: And in the
flight software group,

02:23:45.470 --> 02:23:47.510
I think they were--

02:23:47.510 --> 02:23:50.940
you couldn't even remember
that they were subcontractors.

02:23:50.940 --> 02:23:51.910
PRESENTER 6: Right.

02:23:51.910 --> 02:23:53.270
PRESENTER 7: [? You're ?]
told to [? sign ?] them.

02:23:53.270 --> 02:23:54.853
PRESENTER 4: Yeah,
Joe [? Sapinaro, ?]

02:23:54.853 --> 02:23:58.110
who ran that large team,
cracked the whip on all of--

02:23:58.110 --> 02:23:58.860
PRESENTER 7: Yeah.

02:23:58.860 --> 02:24:01.100
PRESENTER 1: It's been a
bunch of guys from CUC.

02:24:01.100 --> 02:24:01.560
PRESENTER 4: Yeah.

02:24:01.560 --> 02:24:01.910
PRESENTER 6: Yeah.

02:24:01.910 --> 02:24:02.785
PRESENTER 4: Phyllis.

02:24:02.785 --> 02:24:04.490
PRESENTER 1: And
I don't remember

02:24:04.490 --> 02:24:07.190
how many from AC, but
if Jay Sampson hadn't

02:24:07.190 --> 02:24:09.500
been in that group, we
would've languished.

02:24:09.500 --> 02:24:10.291
PRESENTER 4: Right.

02:24:10.291 --> 02:24:13.040
We did the 501 with
Jay and the gang.

02:24:13.040 --> 02:24:14.552
Jay did most of it.

02:24:14.552 --> 02:24:15.260
PRESENTER 1: Who?

02:24:15.260 --> 02:24:16.676
PRESENTER 5:
[? Ronnie ?] Gilbert.

02:24:16.676 --> 02:24:18.140
PRESENTER 1: Oh, yeah.

02:24:18.140 --> 02:24:19.640
GEROVITCH: Was there
any interaction

02:24:19.640 --> 02:24:22.037
between the instrumentation
lab and the rest of MIT

02:24:22.037 --> 02:24:23.120
as a teaching institution?

02:24:23.120 --> 02:24:26.842
Did undergrads or grad
students [INAUDIBLE]??

02:24:26.842 --> 02:24:28.800
PRESENTER 7: We hired a
lot of people from MIT.

02:24:28.800 --> 02:24:31.640
PRESENTER 4: Well, there
was close interaction

02:24:31.640 --> 02:24:34.460
about the time Apollo started,
because it wasn't so big,

02:24:34.460 --> 02:24:37.240
and it was closely tied
with the aero department.

02:24:37.240 --> 02:24:40.460
And the instrumentation
lab was part of MIT then.

02:24:40.460 --> 02:24:42.725
You remember the faculty
[? club ?] [? athletic, ?]

02:24:42.725 --> 02:24:43.380
and so on.

02:24:43.380 --> 02:24:44.810
Like Lincoln Lab--

02:24:44.810 --> 02:24:47.120
I don't know, maybe
Lincoln Lab still is.

02:24:47.120 --> 02:24:51.380
But then, at some point,
due to Vietnam protests,

02:24:51.380 --> 02:24:53.840
the lab split off.

02:24:53.840 --> 02:24:56.040
I don't remember--
early '70s or so--

02:24:56.040 --> 02:24:57.260
PRESENTER 5: '69, I think.

02:24:57.260 --> 02:24:59.210
PRESENTER 4: --when
that happened.

02:24:59.210 --> 02:25:00.742
And became less connected with--

02:25:00.742 --> 02:25:02.450
PRESENTER 6: Well,
there was connections.

02:25:02.450 --> 02:25:06.800
There were students-- you always
had research assistants that

02:25:06.800 --> 02:25:07.730
were around.

02:25:07.730 --> 02:25:11.690
People like me,
who did that twice.

02:25:11.690 --> 02:25:15.740
I went from employee to--
and then became a student

02:25:15.740 --> 02:25:18.920
and got a master's, and then
became a student, got a PhD,

02:25:18.920 --> 02:25:19.924
and so on.

02:25:19.924 --> 02:25:22.340
But there were other students--
there were always students

02:25:22.340 --> 02:25:22.840
around.

02:25:22.840 --> 02:25:26.140
Some of them turned into
astronauts, like Buzz Aldrin

02:25:26.140 --> 02:25:27.890
and, I think, Ed
Mitchell, and some others

02:25:27.890 --> 02:25:31.804
that were in the aero
department at one time.

02:25:31.804 --> 02:25:33.470
PRESENTER 1: Dave
Scott was another one.

02:25:33.470 --> 02:25:33.680
PRESENTER 6: Who?

02:25:33.680 --> 02:25:34.380
PRESENTER 1: Dave Scott.

02:25:34.380 --> 02:25:35.060
PRESENTER 6: I think so.

02:25:35.060 --> 02:25:35.550
PRESENTER 1: Yeah.

02:25:35.550 --> 02:25:37.640
PRESENTER 6: These
turned out to be students

02:25:37.640 --> 02:25:40.160
of Dick [? Batton, ?]
and they all

02:25:40.160 --> 02:25:44.300
wanted to know something
about guidance and control.

02:25:44.300 --> 02:25:48.830
And I think, probably, students
of [? Wally Vanderbilt ?]

02:25:48.830 --> 02:25:49.950
as well.

02:25:49.950 --> 02:25:56.590
So there was a
connection into it,

02:25:56.590 --> 02:25:58.360
although I don't think
that the people--

02:25:58.360 --> 02:26:00.460
any of the people, other
than these students

02:26:00.460 --> 02:26:01.490
that I mentioned--

02:26:01.490 --> 02:26:06.370
I don't think that the
employees thought of themselves,

02:26:06.370 --> 02:26:09.295
in any way, as
academics of any kind.

02:26:09.295 --> 02:26:13.870
They were working engineers in a
laboratory associated with MIT.

02:26:16.420 --> 02:26:22.260
And there was some vague, let's
say, organizational connection

02:26:22.260 --> 02:26:26.140
up above, that certain MIT
policies would flow down,

02:26:26.140 --> 02:26:31.120
whether it was retirement
benefits or health benefits,

02:26:31.120 --> 02:26:32.590
and other things
of that nature--

02:26:32.590 --> 02:26:33.615
library cards and--

02:26:33.615 --> 02:26:35.240
PRESENTER 7: Going
to the faculty club.

02:26:35.240 --> 02:26:37.156
PRESENTER 6: Going to
the faculty club, things

02:26:37.156 --> 02:26:39.085
of that nature that you had.

02:26:42.370 --> 02:26:44.530
One big difference
in the laboratory,

02:26:44.530 --> 02:26:48.130
as opposed to some other
laboratories, like--

02:26:48.130 --> 02:26:49.110
I don't know.

02:26:49.110 --> 02:26:50.860
I'm not sure about
the radiation lab,

02:26:50.860 --> 02:26:53.830
but I know, with
respect to Lincoln Lab.

02:26:53.830 --> 02:26:57.940
Lincoln, at that time,
was a line item laboratory

02:26:57.940 --> 02:26:59.230
in the Air Force.

02:26:59.230 --> 02:27:01.810
So they got-- the Air
Force had a budget,

02:27:01.810 --> 02:27:04.310
and they said, here's the
budget for Lincoln laboratories.

02:27:04.310 --> 02:27:08.470
And they did all their work
under this budget item.

02:27:08.470 --> 02:27:10.000
And the instrumentation
laboratory

02:27:10.000 --> 02:27:13.110
didn't have any budget
angel, in that sense,

02:27:13.110 --> 02:27:16.420
and they had to scrounge around
and do their own marketing,

02:27:16.420 --> 02:27:18.250
and find work and so on.

02:27:18.250 --> 02:27:22.180
And of course, Draper got
this huge sole-source contract

02:27:22.180 --> 02:27:25.210
to do Apollo, which
was magnificent.

02:27:25.210 --> 02:27:31.090
But basically, the lab
was not a kept lab.

02:27:31.090 --> 02:27:32.160
They had to do--

02:27:32.160 --> 02:27:35.260
they had to get out there in the
world and find that business.

02:27:35.260 --> 02:27:40.720
And that was a big distinction
between this laboratory

02:27:40.720 --> 02:27:44.080
and some of the other MIT labs.

02:27:44.080 --> 02:27:46.577
I think the thing that
Dan is referring to

02:27:46.577 --> 02:27:48.160
is that, during the
Vietnam War, there

02:27:48.160 --> 02:27:53.890
were tremendous student
gatherings on campus here,

02:27:53.890 --> 02:27:56.500
and all kinds of protests.

02:27:56.500 --> 02:27:58.900
I think I had just
left the lab, and I

02:27:58.900 --> 02:28:02.140
can't remember what the
essence of what they

02:28:02.140 --> 02:28:03.530
were protesting at the lab.

02:28:03.530 --> 02:28:05.206
I think it was
ballistic missile--

02:28:05.206 --> 02:28:06.455
PRESENTER 1: Defense contract.

02:28:06.455 --> 02:28:07.010
PRESENTER 5: Yeah.

02:28:07.010 --> 02:28:08.620
Just military and
defense contract.

02:28:08.620 --> 02:28:09.960
It had no place in academia.

02:28:09.960 --> 02:28:10.880
PRESENTER 1: No.

02:28:10.880 --> 02:28:11.580
PRESENTER 6: So you had--

02:28:11.580 --> 02:28:12.360
PRESENTER 2: [INAUDIBLE]

02:28:12.360 --> 02:28:14.610
PRESENTER 6: You had the
instrumentation laboratories.

02:28:14.610 --> 02:28:17.036
This whole section was
hollow, you might say.

02:28:17.036 --> 02:28:18.910
But there were other
sections of the lab that

02:28:18.910 --> 02:28:23.200
were doing inertial guidance
work for ballistic missiles

02:28:23.200 --> 02:28:25.030
and so on.

02:28:25.030 --> 02:28:27.760
And we heard stories
of them having

02:28:27.760 --> 02:28:31.590
barriers across the doors,
and bolts, and this and that.

02:28:31.590 --> 02:28:36.570
And pressure to divest the
laboratory from the school.

02:28:36.570 --> 02:28:39.670
And eventually,
some accommodation

02:28:39.670 --> 02:28:42.589
was made with respect to the
organizational structure.

02:28:42.589 --> 02:28:44.380
PRESENTER 4: All of us
were in one building

02:28:44.380 --> 02:28:46.750
right around the corner
here, in Cambridge Parkway.

02:28:46.750 --> 02:28:49.690
We weren't over at main MIT.

02:28:49.690 --> 02:28:53.800
We were down towards Lotus.

02:28:53.800 --> 02:28:55.030
Lotus wasn't there then.

02:28:55.030 --> 02:28:55.987
A bunch of warehouses.

02:28:55.987 --> 02:28:57.820
PRESENTER 2: The
building's gone now, right?

02:28:57.820 --> 02:28:58.570
PRESENTER 4: Yeah.

02:28:58.570 --> 02:29:00.277
PRESENTER 1: The
building's gone.

02:29:00.277 --> 02:29:02.610
PRESENTER 7: Right next to
the hotel, what's that hotel?

02:29:02.610 --> 02:29:02.950
PRESENTER 4: Sonesta.

02:29:02.950 --> 02:29:03.440
PRESENTER 7: Sonesta.

02:29:03.440 --> 02:29:04.880
PRESENTER 6: Well, that
was a couple of buildings

02:29:04.880 --> 02:29:05.910
further down.

02:29:05.910 --> 02:29:08.110
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

02:29:10.032 --> 02:29:11.740
PRESENTER 7: Was that
what it was called?

02:29:11.740 --> 02:29:14.480
PRESENTER 1: Before
it was the Sonesta.

02:29:14.480 --> 02:29:16.780
PRESENTER 6: Of course,
Apollo didn't start in there.

02:29:16.780 --> 02:29:19.570
We sort of gathered
into that building.

02:29:19.570 --> 02:29:22.090
We started in other places.

02:29:22.090 --> 02:29:22.809
The lab had--

02:29:22.809 --> 02:29:23.350
I don't know.

02:29:23.350 --> 02:29:25.720
That was W6, right?

02:29:25.720 --> 02:29:29.410
No, that was 7.

02:29:29.410 --> 02:29:31.420
W6 was way down Vassar Street.

02:29:31.420 --> 02:29:33.910
And then, there was
1, 2, 3, 4, and 5.

02:29:33.910 --> 02:29:37.390
And you had a garage, and
you had an old shoe polish

02:29:37.390 --> 02:29:39.190
building, and so on.

02:29:39.190 --> 02:29:42.190
And Apollo began in
these other buildings,

02:29:42.190 --> 02:29:44.106
with various groups
of a few people

02:29:44.106 --> 02:29:45.730
here and a few people
there, and so on.

02:29:45.730 --> 02:29:47.710
And I can't remember
the exact date

02:29:47.710 --> 02:29:48.970
when everybody was gathered.

02:29:48.970 --> 02:29:50.053
PRESENTER 4: I don't know.

02:29:50.053 --> 02:29:53.081
We had the big computers down
there in the first floor,

02:29:53.081 --> 02:29:54.580
and Dick [? Batten ?]
in the corner.

02:29:54.580 --> 02:29:55.190
And all of us--

02:29:55.190 --> 02:29:57.689
PRESENTER 6: But I know Dick--
when I went to work for Dick,

02:29:57.689 --> 02:30:02.650
he was in W1, near that
elevator shaft that was in W1.

02:30:02.650 --> 02:30:03.310
And so--

02:30:03.310 --> 02:30:03.370
PRESENTER 1: Yeah.

02:30:03.370 --> 02:30:04.204
On the fourth floor.

02:30:04.204 --> 02:30:04.953
PRESENTER 6: Yeah.

02:30:04.953 --> 02:30:05.950
So it must have been--

02:30:05.950 --> 02:30:07.100
PRESENTER 5: Where the 650 was?

02:30:07.100 --> 02:30:08.433
PRESENTER 1: About '62, we had--

02:30:08.433 --> 02:30:10.150
PRESENTER 6: Yeah,
it must've been 1962

02:30:10.150 --> 02:30:12.490
or so, that everybody
was gathered and brought

02:30:12.490 --> 02:30:15.540
into this one building.

02:30:15.540 --> 02:30:17.390
And they had things
on the roof, too.

02:30:17.390 --> 02:30:19.790
Didn't you have a
sextant and something

02:30:19.790 --> 02:30:24.394
on the roof of this building
that did star sightings?

02:30:24.394 --> 02:30:26.560
PRESENTER 4: When we all
moved to the second floors,

02:30:26.560 --> 02:30:29.740
when you had the project
organization and all of that,

02:30:29.740 --> 02:30:33.205
we took over all
that second floor.

02:30:33.205 --> 02:30:36.640
PRESENTER 6: But I thought that
either [? Nevins, ?] or John

02:30:36.640 --> 02:30:41.430
[? Dolan, ?] or somebody
had optical equipment.

02:30:41.430 --> 02:30:44.740
PRESENTER 1: It seemed to me
that was down in [? 6. ?] 224

02:30:44.740 --> 02:30:45.587
[INAUDIBLE] Street.

02:30:45.587 --> 02:30:47.170
PRESENTER 4: A lot
of it was, but they

02:30:47.170 --> 02:30:48.700
had the thing on
the roof that you

02:30:48.700 --> 02:30:51.590
can look at the-- they had
that thing you sat up there,

02:30:51.590 --> 02:30:53.700
Doc Draper's toy.

02:30:53.700 --> 02:30:55.090
PRESENTER 6: Yeah.

02:30:55.090 --> 02:30:55.590
Yeah.

02:30:58.270 --> 02:31:01.850
PRESENTER 4: Anyway,
it was quite a time

02:31:01.850 --> 02:31:04.120
in there starting
in the early '60s,

02:31:04.120 --> 02:31:09.380
and escalated as you got closer
and closer to each flight.

02:31:09.380 --> 02:31:12.590
And I mean, Alex did
the first one, 201.

02:31:12.590 --> 02:31:14.510
I don't remember
the sequences after.

02:31:14.510 --> 02:31:17.900
The first few were all unmanned,
and I don't even remember--

02:31:17.900 --> 02:31:19.400
PRESENTER 5: Yeah,
201 was unguided.

02:31:19.400 --> 02:31:21.980
I think 202 was the first
[? HEC-guided ?] [INAUDIBLE]..

02:31:21.980 --> 02:31:23.310
PRESENTER 4: 202, I mean.

02:31:23.310 --> 02:31:23.810
Right.

02:31:23.810 --> 02:31:26.522
201 was just open-ended.

02:31:26.522 --> 02:31:28.980
GEROVITCH: Just let me ask you
the last question, probably.

02:31:28.980 --> 02:31:31.990
But did you have any
international contacts?

02:31:31.990 --> 02:31:34.450
Did you go abroad
t Europe, whatever?

02:31:34.450 --> 02:31:40.010
Did you have any contacts
with foreign [? venues? ?] Was

02:31:40.010 --> 02:31:43.242
anybody interested from
abroad in what you were doing?

02:31:43.242 --> 02:31:45.200
PRESENTER 6: I'm sure
there was great interest,

02:31:45.200 --> 02:31:48.450
but I can't recall--

02:31:48.450 --> 02:31:51.600
it wasn't like space lab.

02:31:51.600 --> 02:31:53.120
I did some work on
space lab, where

02:31:53.120 --> 02:31:55.203
you had a real international
group that you were--

02:31:55.203 --> 02:31:58.040
PRESENTER 4: Nobody in the
world was doing much yet,

02:31:58.040 --> 02:32:00.080
and so it all pretty early.

02:32:00.080 --> 02:32:01.970
There were individuals
interested,

02:32:01.970 --> 02:32:04.550
but there wasn't any
institutional interest.

02:32:04.550 --> 02:32:06.450
PRESENTER 5: There was
huge popular interest.

02:32:06.450 --> 02:32:07.241
PRESENTER 4: Right.

02:32:07.241 --> 02:32:10.100
PRESENTER 7: I presented
a paper in Paris

02:32:10.100 --> 02:32:13.980
at a conference on a
theory that had come out

02:32:13.980 --> 02:32:16.650
of the Apollo project.

02:32:16.650 --> 02:32:18.560
It was part of the--

02:32:18.560 --> 02:32:21.410
part of our organization
sent us there.

02:32:21.410 --> 02:32:24.149
So in that way--

02:32:24.149 --> 02:32:25.940
but it wasn't really
what we did in Apollo.

02:32:25.940 --> 02:32:29.290
It was what came
as a result of it.

02:32:29.290 --> 02:32:31.200
GEROVITCH: Were they interested?

02:32:31.200 --> 02:32:33.020
PRESENTER 7: Very interested.

02:32:33.020 --> 02:32:35.510
I just can't-- my
mind can't remember--

02:32:35.510 --> 02:32:39.560
PRESENTER 5: That was about
the mid-'70s, though, right?

02:32:39.560 --> 02:32:41.240
PRESENTER 7: You
know, early '70s.

02:32:41.240 --> 02:32:42.380
PRESENTER 5: Early '70s.

02:32:42.380 --> 02:32:45.880
But it was still part
of the Apollo group.

02:32:45.880 --> 02:32:50.960
The Colloquium
Program [INAUDIBLE]

02:32:50.960 --> 02:32:53.840
colloquium or
something, in Paris.

02:32:53.840 --> 02:32:56.010
Mostly academics.

02:32:56.010 --> 02:32:59.230
But yeah, there was
a lot of interest.

02:32:59.230 --> 02:33:03.650
But it was mostly academics,
not application types.

02:33:03.650 --> 02:33:06.560
PRESENTER 4: No, during
Apollo, most of our trips

02:33:06.560 --> 02:33:08.330
were to Houston,
or for those of us

02:33:08.330 --> 02:33:11.190
that dealt with Rockwell,
out to Rockwell.

02:33:11.190 --> 02:33:13.420
It was all the
business meetings.

02:33:13.420 --> 02:33:15.890
PRESENTER 6: But some of us
were very shocked when we--

02:33:15.890 --> 02:33:19.340
I was shocked when I first
made a trip to North America.

02:33:19.340 --> 02:33:21.170
And we had--

02:33:21.170 --> 02:33:25.460
I don't know-- you have the
Cambridge scene here, you know?

02:33:25.460 --> 02:33:29.370
Everybody has his own office,
and everything's sort of quiet.

02:33:29.370 --> 02:33:31.289
And you look out the window.

02:33:31.289 --> 02:33:32.330
You might see the river--

02:33:32.330 --> 02:33:34.140
PRESENTER 7: And watch
the planes take off.

02:33:34.140 --> 02:33:35.180
PRESENTER 6: Yeah, watch
the planes take off.

02:33:35.180 --> 02:33:37.513
PRESENTER 7: From my office,
that was my favorite thing.

02:33:37.513 --> 02:33:40.220
PRESENTER 6: And then you
made these business trips out

02:33:40.220 --> 02:33:43.322
to Rockwell, to North America.

02:33:43.322 --> 02:33:44.530
PRESENTER 4: North American--

02:33:44.530 --> 02:33:46.238
PRESENTER 6: What you
were faced with was

02:33:46.238 --> 02:33:54.590
this three-block-long, open
hangar with God knows how

02:33:54.590 --> 02:33:56.100
many people in that hangar.

02:33:56.100 --> 02:33:58.474
And telephones
ringing and noise--

02:33:58.474 --> 02:34:00.890
PRESENTER 5: The [INAUDIBLE]
edge of the building was lost

02:34:00.890 --> 02:34:01.500
in the smoke--

02:34:01.500 --> 02:34:02.291
PRESENTER 6: Right.

02:34:02.291 --> 02:34:03.320
PRESENTER 5: [INAUDIBLE]

02:34:03.320 --> 02:34:04.790
PRESENTER 6: The contrast--

02:34:04.790 --> 02:34:09.440
you wondered how anybody
got any work done.

02:34:09.440 --> 02:34:12.770
I mean, the contrast
between what

02:34:12.770 --> 02:34:17.270
we used to think of as
the aerospace industry,

02:34:17.270 --> 02:34:19.760
and the norm of
how people worked

02:34:19.760 --> 02:34:23.720
in this aerospace industry
versus the norm of what we were

02:34:23.720 --> 02:34:26.870
doing at MIT was night and day.

02:34:26.870 --> 02:34:28.412
It was just a different world.

02:34:28.412 --> 02:34:29.120
PRESENTER: Right.

02:34:29.120 --> 02:34:30.703
PRESENTER 4: There
were metal vendors,

02:34:30.703 --> 02:34:33.970
and they wanted to build 10,000
more airplanes if we told them

02:34:33.970 --> 02:34:34.800
how.

02:34:34.800 --> 02:34:37.820
PRESENTER 7: Our offices looked
like graduate students' offices

02:34:37.820 --> 02:34:39.940
at MIT.

02:34:39.940 --> 02:34:41.730
You walked in there--

02:34:41.730 --> 02:34:43.355
I came from the
meteorology department,

02:34:43.355 --> 02:34:46.670
and I felt like it was part
of the same environment.

02:34:46.670 --> 02:34:51.622
It was not like a big
corporation outside of MIT.

02:34:51.622 --> 02:34:54.080
PRESENTER 1: It was interesting,
in the early years, to me,

02:34:54.080 --> 02:34:57.280
to find that MIT people--

02:34:57.280 --> 02:34:59.150
we were never
instrumentation lab people.

02:34:59.150 --> 02:35:02.490
We were always MIT people,
for whatever reason.

02:35:02.490 --> 02:35:05.360
Although we had this local
environment of our own,

02:35:05.360 --> 02:35:08.070
when we went out
to North America,

02:35:08.070 --> 02:35:10.010
we found that we were
the communication

02:35:10.010 --> 02:35:12.140
means between parts
of North America

02:35:12.140 --> 02:35:14.960
that we interfaced with that
didn't talk to each other.

02:35:14.960 --> 02:35:17.360
And often, we were the
ones that told them things

02:35:17.360 --> 02:35:20.310
that their own management,
perhaps, should have told them.

02:35:20.310 --> 02:35:21.970
But they were
really glad to know.

02:35:21.970 --> 02:35:24.560
And we were served a very
useful, if inadvertent,

02:35:24.560 --> 02:35:25.720
function that way.

02:35:25.720 --> 02:35:27.620
PRESENTER 7: That's
interesting that you

02:35:27.620 --> 02:35:32.960
say that because the glue
that Alex was talking about,

02:35:32.960 --> 02:35:35.510
when we put together the
different powered flight

02:35:35.510 --> 02:35:37.550
and navigation
and everything, we

02:35:37.550 --> 02:35:39.980
found that we were
a communication

02:35:39.980 --> 02:35:42.650
vehicle [? that ?]
the entire mission

02:35:42.650 --> 02:35:44.750
software for the
different groups

02:35:44.750 --> 02:35:48.210
that were being
interfaced, as well.

02:35:48.210 --> 02:35:50.030
And that's how we
learned about--

02:35:50.030 --> 02:35:52.700
we didn't really know what
was inside the-- or at least,

02:35:52.700 --> 02:35:55.740
I was less familiar than
you-- inside the black boxes.

02:35:55.740 --> 02:36:00.320
But we really knew how
they all tied together.

02:36:00.320 --> 02:36:03.000
PRESENTER 4: One more thing you
probably should say about this.

02:36:03.000 --> 02:36:05.640
Working at the
lab, which was MIT,

02:36:05.640 --> 02:36:07.430
we were in a very
privileged position.

02:36:07.430 --> 02:36:11.180
We got to see and talk to not
only all astronauts, everybody

02:36:11.180 --> 02:36:13.830
at NASA, all over the place.

02:36:13.830 --> 02:36:16.140
I mean, Wernher Von Braun
would come and visit.

02:36:16.140 --> 02:36:18.560
And I remember giving him
lectures on what we were doing,

02:36:18.560 --> 02:36:19.760
and so forth.

02:36:19.760 --> 02:36:22.100
All those people,
we got to talk to

02:36:22.100 --> 02:36:25.220
as a small, elite organization.

02:36:25.220 --> 02:36:27.680
And when the flights were going
off-- the Apollo flights--

02:36:27.680 --> 02:36:32.690
we were tied in very closely
on those headsets and boxes.

02:36:32.690 --> 02:36:34.190
I can remember one flight--

02:36:34.190 --> 02:36:36.764
I don't remember what and who
I was talking with in Houston.

02:36:36.764 --> 02:36:37.930
Might have been Jack Garmin.

02:36:37.930 --> 02:36:40.140
I said-- it was a problem
we had, and I said,

02:36:40.140 --> 02:36:41.560
I think we need
an erasable dump.

02:36:41.560 --> 02:36:42.620
Will you get one?

02:36:42.620 --> 02:36:46.457
Within 30 seconds on the
main [? cap ?] commander--

02:36:46.457 --> 02:36:47.540
PRESENTER 5: [? Capcom. ?]

02:36:47.540 --> 02:36:49.914
PRESENTER 4: [? Capcom-- ?]
whatever-- to the astronauts.

02:36:49.914 --> 02:36:50.770
MIT wants a dump.

02:36:50.770 --> 02:36:51.440
Will you do it?

02:36:51.440 --> 02:36:52.730
Something or other.

02:36:52.730 --> 02:36:55.790
And it was always, MIT
wants this and that.

02:36:55.790 --> 02:36:58.150
And so I felt
anything we did was

02:36:58.150 --> 02:37:01.349
going to get there
and back very quickly.

02:37:01.349 --> 02:37:03.140
That might have been
the time we're worried

02:37:03.140 --> 02:37:06.467
about erasable being messed up.

02:37:06.467 --> 02:37:08.550
PRESENTER 6: I also thought
some people attributed

02:37:08.550 --> 02:37:14.459
a pedestal standing
to us that maybe, I

02:37:14.459 --> 02:37:15.750
don't think we really deserved.

02:37:15.750 --> 02:37:16.250
But--

02:37:16.250 --> 02:37:17.469
PRESENTER 1: Oh, yeah.

02:37:17.469 --> 02:37:19.760
PRESENTER 6: There was one
thing that happened, though,

02:37:19.760 --> 02:37:24.710
that I still vividly recall.

02:37:24.710 --> 02:37:27.290
It was near the end of the--

02:37:27.290 --> 02:37:30.205
there was a landing on the
moon, and it was successful,

02:37:30.205 --> 02:37:30.830
and they were--

02:37:33.600 --> 02:37:35.920
the program-- it either was--

02:37:35.920 --> 02:37:37.550
yeah, it was just
after that landing,

02:37:37.550 --> 02:37:39.110
or it could have
been just before.

02:37:39.110 --> 02:37:44.080
But we used to go to these
dinners at [? Lockovers. ?]

02:37:44.080 --> 02:37:48.110
And I don't know how many I went
to, maybe four or something.

02:37:48.110 --> 02:37:50.720
And Draper was very
expansive about this,

02:37:50.720 --> 02:37:53.000
and he'd want to get
all these people there,

02:37:53.000 --> 02:37:55.460
and he'd always have
some astronauts in there

02:37:55.460 --> 02:37:57.660
because that was kind of fun.

02:37:57.660 --> 02:38:01.610
And so I remember
this dinner, and I

02:38:01.610 --> 02:38:04.970
was sitting next to
Frank [? Borman, ?]

02:38:04.970 --> 02:38:06.070
and we were eating dinner.

02:38:06.070 --> 02:38:10.260
And Frank Borman-- somewhere
in the conversation,

02:38:10.260 --> 02:38:14.930
he felt that the people at
MIT-- we were all geniuses,

02:38:14.930 --> 02:38:20.390
and we were all just these
almost like everybody

02:38:20.390 --> 02:38:22.220
was a guru, and
everybody was a genius.

02:38:25.100 --> 02:38:28.880
Then he said to me something
like, you guys at MIT,

02:38:28.880 --> 02:38:31.170
you ought to get out
of this program now.

02:38:31.170 --> 02:38:33.200
He said, this program
is nothing but--

02:38:33.200 --> 02:38:36.140
it's going to be
just grinding it out.

02:38:36.140 --> 02:38:39.040
He said, there's not going to
be anything more of interest.

02:38:39.040 --> 02:38:40.790
There's nothing more
that you guys can do.

02:38:40.790 --> 02:38:44.420
You ought to go off and do
something bigger and more

02:38:44.420 --> 02:38:47.222
interesting than Apollo.

02:38:47.222 --> 02:38:48.680
And I thought that
was interesting.

02:38:48.680 --> 02:38:50.284
[LAUGHTER]

02:38:50.284 --> 02:38:52.200
PRESENTER 4: So we
followed Frank [? Borman ?]

02:38:52.200 --> 02:38:53.150
into Eastern Airlines.

02:38:53.150 --> 02:38:56.321
[LAUGHTER]

02:38:57.404 --> 02:38:58.820
PRESENTER 6: That's
exactly right.

02:39:01.515 --> 02:39:02.390
GEROVITCH: All right.

02:39:02.390 --> 02:39:05.390
PRESENTER 4: Yeah, we
did have a good position.

02:39:05.390 --> 02:39:07.760
But also, there were so
many of the astronauts that

02:39:07.760 --> 02:39:11.900
had previous connections
at MIT that we

02:39:11.900 --> 02:39:14.690
weren't poked fun at too much.

02:39:14.690 --> 02:39:15.980
We had some inside tracks.

02:39:15.980 --> 02:39:18.410
PRESENTER 6: I might tell
you, just as a closing comment

02:39:18.410 --> 02:39:24.860
for me, that Dick [? Batten ?]
asked me, last year,

02:39:24.860 --> 02:39:32.880
to be a guest lecturer
at one of his sessions.

02:39:32.880 --> 02:39:37.790
He runs a freshman
seminar on Apollo.

02:39:37.790 --> 02:39:39.950
And he has these kids,
and kids come in.

02:39:39.950 --> 02:39:45.260
And last year, he
had Harrison Schmidt.

02:39:45.260 --> 02:39:46.300
Is that his name?

02:39:46.300 --> 02:39:47.630
The astronaut.

02:39:47.630 --> 02:39:51.000
And he had a number
of other people.

02:39:51.000 --> 02:39:51.750
And I was there.

02:39:51.750 --> 02:39:56.240
He's trying to get Don
[? Isles ?] to come in.

02:39:56.240 --> 02:39:57.860
And it's really remarkable.

02:39:57.860 --> 02:39:58.980
I only did it once.

02:39:58.980 --> 02:40:01.400
And so what you do
is you're talking

02:40:01.400 --> 02:40:03.580
to these kids, who are
about 18 years old,

02:40:03.580 --> 02:40:06.520
and they really know
almost nothing--

02:40:06.520 --> 02:40:07.029
zero.

02:40:07.029 --> 02:40:08.570
I mean, whatever
they know, they know

02:40:08.570 --> 02:40:11.600
from television or
cartoons, or maybe they

02:40:11.600 --> 02:40:12.600
read something or other.

02:40:12.600 --> 02:40:14.090
They know nothing.

02:40:14.090 --> 02:40:16.850
But it is interesting,
the level of interest

02:40:16.850 --> 02:40:18.560
that they have in the program.

02:40:18.560 --> 02:40:22.430
And usually, a certain
percentage of them,

02:40:22.430 --> 02:40:25.340
like four out of eight,
or five out of 10,

02:40:25.340 --> 02:40:27.510
all have-- they all
want to be astronauts.

02:40:27.510 --> 02:40:29.260
That's one of the
things that drives them.

02:40:29.260 --> 02:40:34.100
Because you wonder why this
person, as a freshman, takes

02:40:34.100 --> 02:40:37.640
this freshman seminar with
Dick [? Batten ?] on Apollo,

02:40:37.640 --> 02:40:39.730
from 30 years ago.

02:40:39.730 --> 02:40:41.600
But it is interesting.

02:40:41.600 --> 02:40:43.590
Of course, Dick loves it.

02:40:43.590 --> 02:40:45.980
And I did bring
in some artifacts.

02:40:45.980 --> 02:40:48.320
This is a program listing.

02:40:48.320 --> 02:40:50.230
This is a this, this is a that.

02:40:50.230 --> 02:40:54.430
And most of it is just
getting up to a blackboard,

02:40:54.430 --> 02:40:58.360
and drawing a few stick
figures, and telling them

02:40:58.360 --> 02:41:01.510
a little bit about how we got
to the moon, and something

02:41:01.510 --> 02:41:02.500
about the computer.

02:41:02.500 --> 02:41:04.450
And each person has
something else to say.

02:41:04.450 --> 02:41:08.080
Of course, I'm sure that
Harrison Schmidt gave them

02:41:08.080 --> 02:41:10.180
the whole astronaut viewpoint.

02:41:10.180 --> 02:41:13.630
It really is remarkable
for these kids

02:41:13.630 --> 02:41:15.940
to have this connection,
though Dick [? Batten, ?]

02:41:15.940 --> 02:41:17.200
to this world.

02:41:17.200 --> 02:41:21.220
Because it's something
that very few people--

02:41:21.220 --> 02:41:24.370
very few high school
students, or very few freshman

02:41:24.370 --> 02:41:25.060
in college--

02:41:25.060 --> 02:41:28.360
could have an opening to.

02:41:28.360 --> 02:41:33.480
And to have these individuals
appear in front of them.

02:41:33.480 --> 02:41:36.296
And Dick just loves it.

02:41:36.296 --> 02:41:38.670
PRESENTER 5: This is something
I felt coming from Europe.

02:41:38.670 --> 02:41:40.570
I've got friends
and relatives there.

02:41:40.570 --> 02:41:43.270
Incredible intensity,
that interest

02:41:43.270 --> 02:41:46.030
in Apollo, what happened.

02:41:46.030 --> 02:41:49.060
I often feel like,
since I was in it,

02:41:49.060 --> 02:41:51.520
I haven't done my
bit in satisfying

02:41:51.520 --> 02:41:53.170
some of the yearning
for information,

02:41:53.170 --> 02:41:57.100
and contact with
this glamorous world.

02:41:57.100 --> 02:41:59.110
And that's just sort
of a popular reaction.

02:41:59.110 --> 02:42:02.050
It's nothing to do with
military, academia,

02:42:02.050 --> 02:42:03.190
or business or anything.

02:42:03.190 --> 02:42:08.980
The men in the street view the
American Apollo program as just

02:42:08.980 --> 02:42:10.990
an incredible achievement.

02:42:10.990 --> 02:42:12.720
PRESENTER 6: If it
ever really happened.

02:42:12.720 --> 02:42:13.553
PRESENTER 5: Indeed.

02:42:13.553 --> 02:42:14.500
[LAUGHTER]

02:42:14.779 --> 02:42:15.320
Is this true?

02:42:15.320 --> 02:42:18.814
Yeah, people who weren't
even alive then still--

02:42:18.814 --> 02:42:20.980
GEROVITCH: Yeah, we hope
that the website that we're

02:42:20.980 --> 02:42:23.200
working on would--
one of its functions

02:42:23.200 --> 02:42:26.020
would be to provide a
window into the history

02:42:26.020 --> 02:42:30.460
of that program for students,
general audience who

02:42:30.460 --> 02:42:31.900
might be interested in that.

02:42:31.900 --> 02:42:36.980
[INAUDIBLE] both the
basis in documents,

02:42:36.980 --> 02:42:40.774
but also a human subject
perspective on this event.

02:42:40.774 --> 02:42:42.190
I wish to thank
you all very much.

02:42:42.190 --> 02:42:42.970
It's been very, very--

02:42:42.970 --> 02:42:45.430
PRESENTER 1: Your job is going
to be a severe one, in terms

02:42:45.430 --> 02:42:50.150
of getting young people
now interested in things

02:42:50.150 --> 02:42:52.930
that, by comparison,
with today's technology,

02:42:52.930 --> 02:42:56.410
are so ancient and so primitive.

02:42:56.410 --> 02:42:59.185
Why would anybody want
to look back at stuff

02:42:59.185 --> 02:43:04.150
from a machine that had a 3-bit
op code and 12-bit address?

02:43:04.150 --> 02:43:07.230
I just bought the Microsoft
flight simulator 2000,

02:43:07.230 --> 02:43:11.750
and it says, you need to have
635 megabytes of disk space

02:43:11.750 --> 02:43:12.250
available.

02:43:12.250 --> 02:43:13.710
[LAUGHTER]

02:43:13.710 --> 02:43:15.455
Good heavens.

02:43:15.455 --> 02:43:17.830
PRESENTER 4: Wait until you
see the operating system that

02:43:17.830 --> 02:43:18.719
comes out this fall.

02:43:18.719 --> 02:43:21.010
PRESENTER 1: If this is what
the kids are dealing with,

02:43:21.010 --> 02:43:25.462
why would they care
about a 36K word machine?

02:43:25.462 --> 02:43:26.920
And the problems
that went with it,

02:43:26.920 --> 02:43:30.140
that are just so completely
removed from today's problems.

02:43:30.140 --> 02:43:32.440
PRESENTER 5: But I think
this could be a lesson--

02:43:32.440 --> 02:43:34.510
PRESENTER 1: It can be knitted
into today, but you have--

02:43:34.510 --> 02:43:35.490
that's a challenge for--

02:43:35.490 --> 02:43:36.865
PRESENTER 7: I
would like to make

02:43:36.865 --> 02:43:38.830
a comment about your comm--

02:43:38.830 --> 02:43:40.150
along with your comment.

02:43:40.150 --> 02:43:46.000
I think that, in looking around
at software problems today,

02:43:46.000 --> 02:43:47.380
they still have
the same problems

02:43:47.380 --> 02:43:49.510
that they had yesterday.

02:43:49.510 --> 02:43:52.330
And I think many of the
things that we learned,

02:43:52.330 --> 02:43:53.500
and that evolved--

02:43:53.500 --> 02:43:58.040
lessons learned could
still be learned today.

02:43:58.040 --> 02:44:00.880
The hardware is different,
but I think software, in fact,

02:44:00.880 --> 02:44:02.670
is worse in many ways now.

02:44:02.670 --> 02:44:05.979
It's done in a more primitive
way now than it was back then.

02:44:05.979 --> 02:44:08.020
PRESENTER 5: Because the
resources are limitless,

02:44:08.020 --> 02:44:09.777
people just don't try--

02:44:09.777 --> 02:44:11.860
PRESENTER 7: So I think
we could go back and learn

02:44:11.860 --> 02:44:17.510
a lot from what has
evolved from that.

02:44:17.510 --> 02:44:21.510
So if we can't, then some
of us are in trouble.

02:44:21.510 --> 02:44:22.600
GEROVITCH: Right, right.

02:44:22.600 --> 02:44:25.510
So look at history not as
just progression of machines

02:44:25.510 --> 02:44:27.167
getting bigger and
better, but also

02:44:27.167 --> 02:44:28.375
the evolution of human skill.

02:44:28.375 --> 02:44:29.300
PRESENTER 7: Of how you think.

02:44:29.300 --> 02:44:30.160
GEROVITCH: Right, right.

02:44:30.160 --> 02:44:31.650
PRESENTER 7: And
model and design.

02:44:31.650 --> 02:44:32.810
Exactly.

02:44:32.810 --> 02:44:35.110
GEROVITCH: And some of
those skills in the '60s

02:44:35.110 --> 02:44:39.574
might have been very valuable
that is not really in use now.

02:44:39.574 --> 02:44:40.990
PRESENTER 7:
Sometimes, we go back

02:44:40.990 --> 02:44:43.900
to the very foundations,
the very core,

02:44:43.900 --> 02:44:48.099
and learn from what we
might have thrown away.

02:44:48.099 --> 02:44:50.140
PRESENTER 6: The other
thing is that people are--

02:44:50.140 --> 02:44:54.040
even though they can hold a
handheld GPS in their hand,

02:44:54.040 --> 02:44:56.890
they're still
interested in how people

02:44:56.890 --> 02:45:04.370
did things with a sextant and
a watch, or not even a watch.

02:45:04.370 --> 02:45:05.620
PRESENTER 7: Or a [? disky. ?]

02:45:05.620 --> 02:45:07.317
PRESENTER 6: Or a [? disky. ?]

02:45:07.317 --> 02:45:09.400
PRESENTER 7: Which I
sometimes call it by mistake.

02:45:09.400 --> 02:45:11.160
[LAUGHTER]

02:45:12.079 --> 02:45:13.870
GEROVITCH: Maybe we
should have a simulator

02:45:13.870 --> 02:45:16.213
for [? disky ?] on the website
where they can actually press--

02:45:16.213 --> 02:45:16.636
PRESENTER 7: Yeah.

02:45:16.636 --> 02:45:17.230
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

02:45:17.230 --> 02:45:17.790
PRESENTER 7: Oh, definitely.

02:45:17.790 --> 02:45:18.070
PRESENTER 6: That'd be great.

02:45:18.070 --> 02:45:18.380
GEROVITCH: You think so?

02:45:18.380 --> 02:45:19.330
PRESENTER 7: Yes.

02:45:19.330 --> 02:45:20.230
That would be great.

02:45:20.230 --> 02:45:22.230
GEROVITCH: Then you can
call up all the programs

02:45:22.230 --> 02:45:24.310
and get [? Jim's ?]
simulator going again.

02:45:24.310 --> 02:45:25.310
PRESENTER 7: [INAUDIBLE]

02:45:25.310 --> 02:45:26.330
GEROVITCH: Right.

02:45:26.330 --> 02:45:27.505
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

02:45:27.505 --> 02:45:28.255
PRESENTER 7: Yeah.

02:45:28.255 --> 02:45:29.490
Priority alarms.

02:45:29.490 --> 02:45:31.470
PRESENTER 6: You can have
your verbs and nouns.

02:45:31.470 --> 02:45:33.215
PRESENTER 7: Yeah.

02:45:33.215 --> 02:45:34.090
GEROVITCH: All right.

02:45:34.090 --> 02:45:35.277
Thank you very much.

02:45:35.277 --> 02:45:36.110
PRESENTER 7: Thanks.

02:45:36.110 --> 02:45:37.474
GEROVITCH: I will be in touch.

02:45:37.474 --> 02:45:38.890
As soon as we have
a transcript, I

02:45:38.890 --> 02:45:42.850
will send it to all of you
for corrections and details

02:45:42.850 --> 02:45:43.540
and all of that.

02:45:43.540 --> 02:45:45.760
PRESENTER 1: I'd appreciate
